      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THE biggest waterfall is but a drop of water. AN UNQUESTIONABLE ACQUAINTANCE —G e ral respectability.. THE saying "Who breaks, pays," evidently wasn't intended to apply to bankrupts. CAN tripping up an objectionable policeman be described as laying down the law? A RECENT novel has this passage : "And with both arms clasped tightly about her slender waist, he stroked her hair fondly." LOQUACIOUS BARBER: "I notice your hair is falling off rapidly, sir. What do you think it is caused by ? Worry ?" Customer : " Undoubtedly. Your incessant chattering worries me to death!"</p>
        <p>TEACHEE, (to class) : " In this stanza, what is meant by the line, The shades of night were falling fast?' " Clever Scholar : "The people were pulling down the blinds." TfiE greatest magicians of the age are the paper- makers. They tramiform the beggars' rags into sheets for the editors to lie on. SNIGGLE: " You never know the worth of a wife until some great trouble befalls you. Snaggle " " Yes, it's nice to lay the blame on Ler."</p>
        <p>A MAN will unblushing'y comb his hair over a bald spot on the top of his head, and yet expect a grocer to put his smallest apples in the top layer of a box.</p>
        <p>"Jr I have ever wed any unkind words to you, Sarah," said Mr. lif:npeel--, calmly, "I will take them all back." " Yes, indeed" she replied. "I suppose so that you can me them all on:er again."</p>
        <p>MAMMA : " Poor boy—how did you hurt your fiygfx so " Lttla Son : " With a hammer. " When?" "A good while ago." " I didn't bear you cry." "1, thought you were out." . _</p>
        <p>_ - A SAVING CLAUSE. —First Swell: "Look here- Fred, you :honldn't wear so many big diamonds. awfuily bad form." Second Swell : "Bless your soul, dear boy, they ain't real !" IlEvENGE.—Customer : " Waiter, what is there for dinner to-day? ' Waiter (under notice to leave) : " Oh, the same as usual I Tough steaks, leathery fowls, sinewy beef, and flavourless mutton I''</p>
        <p>MISTRESS (engaging new'servant) :"Anil what is your name ?' Servant : " Sylva Morley. But my last mistrei:s used to call me Sarah, because she said she didn't consider Sylva a proper name for a servant."</p>
        <p>" Jmus, why. didn't you oblong yOur stay at de seaside?" "Kase, Mr. Smith, dey charge so much."- " How so Julius " Why, de landlord charged dis coloured iudividual wid stealing de silver spoons." BAD ACTOR.: "My ! my ! The audience is leaving the theatre right in the middle of the play." Call Boy: "They ain't goin' home; they'll be back soon." Bad Actor : " Will they? What did they go out for? " Call Boy : "Eggs, you bet." BAcnEr.on, FRIEND (to newly married man) : "Why this dejection, dear boy?. Have you suffered a disappointment?" "Yes, my wife can't sing." " Why, that should not distress you ; I think you are to be congratulated." "Au, but she thinks she can.'</p>
        <p>" NOT " AND " NO."—A newspaper, speaking of a deceased gentleman, said : "He was not more respected in public than revered in private." A contemporary, quoting the paragraph completely reversed its signification by dropping the "t," and writing: "He was no more respected in public than revered in private."_ . .</p>
        <p>CREDITOR (angrily): "This makes exactly thir- teen times I've called for my account." Debtor (blandly) : " Ah, my dear sir, as you know, thir- teen is a very unlucky number. I can't pay you to-day !" "Mm. RIIUNNY : "I want a new bonnet, James." Mr. P. : "And you shall have one, dear." Mrs. P. (throwing her arms round his neck): " You sweet darling old dear!" Mr. P. : "Yes, you shall have one—next summer !" Mrs. P. : " You old wretch !"</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOLDER (to plumber) : "I say, how much longer are you going to be over this job?" Plumber : " I really don't know, sir. But you can rely upon its being faithfully carried out, for when I die my son will succeed to the business !" " ITS meat and drink to me to fall acros a jolly good fellow like Smith," said Jones. " Well, judging from the condition you come home in when you have been with Smith," said Mrs. Jones, "I should say it was meet and drink." " PERKS ! "—Landlady: "The apartments are two pounds a week, with cooking and attendance, sir.' Gentleman: "Oh, we Shan't, require any cooking or attendance. My wife will see to that.' "Landlady : "Then I can't let the rooms under two-pounds-ten, sir !" NEEDS ‘VHITEWASHING.—PottIe was taking a, country cousin round to see the sig*As of London the other day, and stopped before Westminster Abbey, with a "There now, what do you think of that, Cousin Hodge?" " Well, it is grand, but it dew Want whitewashing," was the worthy cousin's reply. "HERE'S your money, dolt. And now, you intolerable donkey, tell me why your scoundrelly master wrote me eighteen letters about this con- temptible sum?" said the exasperated debtor. "If you'll excuse me, sir, I think it was because seventeen letters didn't bring the money." "WHATEVER became of that tall, handsome valet of yours, Lord Noodleby?" asked Miss Debonair. "Oh, I had to discharge him. Strangers would mix us up, you know, and take him for Lord Noodleby and me for the valet,,' returned the peer. "Oh, dear me ! " said Ethel. "And doesn't that prove the truth of the old saying, 'Never judge by appearances '?"</p>
        <p>A BROAD FrINT. - The great man of the village being at dinner, allowed one of his tenants to stand while he conversed with him. "What news, my friend?" asked the squire. "None that I know of," replied the farmer, " except that a sow of mine has a litter of thirteen pigs, and she has only twelve teats." " What will the thirteenth do?" asked the landlord. "Do as I do," replied Hodge—" stand and look on while the others feed.</p>
        <p>CHARACTERISTIC PREFERENCE. —Club Friend: "Which of the two Miss Blenkinsopps do you pre- fer?" O'Bull, junior: "Faith, whin I look at the one I prefer the other, and whin I look at the other I prefer the one." Club Friend: "But when you look at the two ?" O'Bull, junior : " Ah, then it's bothered I am intoirely, for, begorra, if I don't prefer both I" A RELIGIOUS SPIRIT.-Mr, Isaacstein : "I sells you dot coat, my frent, for sayventeen tollar. You &lt;lake him along ?" Customer: "I thought, Isaac- stein, that you didn't do business on Saturday. Isn't this your Sunday ?" Mr. Isaacstein (in a low, reverent tone of voice) : "My f rent, to sell a coat like dot for sayventeen tollar vas not peesness—dot vas charity." How STUPID: Drill Sergeant: "I say, Smith, have you any idea of how slow and stupid you are?" Private Smith : "I don't know." D.S. : "Of course you don't; but let me tell you that an Egyptian mummy is frisky compared with you."</p>
        <p>IMPECUNIOUS.-A friend of Alexandre Dumas the elder, who had just fought a duel, told Dumas that his adversary's ball had been turned aside by a five-franc piece he had in his waistcoat pocket. "You're a lucky fellow," said Dumas; "if I had been in your place I should have been killed." No Docrorts WANTED. —A wayfarer in a primi- tive part of Kent, who had hurt his foot, asked a rustic he met whether there was a doctor near. "Doctor, sir !" said the man, " there ain't no such thing about here. If we sprains ourselves or has the toothache, we goes to the blacksmith; but thank Heaven, we all dies nat'ral deaths !"</p>
        <p>EASILY IMPRESSED.—" Only those who have 'Dem roused from midnight slumber on ship-board by the terrible cry, 'Man overboard I'" said the traveller, " e,n comprehend to the full its terrible meaning—the fear and horror in its sudden alarm." " Oh yes, they can," replied the little lame shoe- maker, who wasn't much of a traveler. "I heard it once when I wasn't near the aiip, and I realised the horror of it more than anyone else." " You couldn't," said the tra-elicr, scornfully. " Yes, I could," persisted the littlt lame shoemaker. "I was the man who was overboard."</p>
        <p>LORD PALMERSTON'S good humour as a distinct element of his character is wen known. We find it even during his last illnes, when his physician wa forced to mention death. "IJie, my dear do •ter exclaitnA. " That's the last thing I hall ,lc."</p>
        <p>YOUR A-171.l;NTION i.‘• from Oravel. Larnbags. Piles, Paitts In the B ck.iiv, V:n.l W,.t.r Complaints. Diseases of ii!adder, (Trinary Organs, Stone, Oleet. Strii t tote. uiatiC.z, liheiirratin. it'd Govt, will find a positive cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pilk. Try a small box, and If not s%ltisfied your money will he returned. Pri,e is lid. Of ail et.etnizts, p,,st tree for 12 stamps from 1-loLn- ROTD'S Medical flail, Cleckheat.fm, Yorks. Don't to put off. If you cannot (ret them write to the Pro;nrietor. and a box v,</p>
        <p>INIOXON'S NURSERY BOON (Unequalled for DESTROYING NITS, and PARASITES in tae HUMAN HAIR.) OF CHEMISTS and the PROPRIETOR, J. L. MOXO N, 252, PARK-ROAD. LIVERPOOL. Ed. and is. per box. Post Free 71 and Iv. Pbssse mention this pA7e::.</p>
