      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>It is a curious fact two of the counsel for the prosecution in the Avenue murder case, Mr. Charles Mathews and Mr. Bancroft, are sons of two cele- brated actors, the great Mathews and Mr. S. B. Bancroft.</p>
        <p>Sporting men will be sorry to learn that the Prince of Wales does not intend racing the Britan- nia in American waters, his principal reason being, I believe, that the notice he would have to give would be so long that when the time came for actual racing he would find himself matching an old boat against a craft specially designed for the purpose of beating her. Of course this is a per- fectly logical reason, and it is a pits that we can- not see the Vigilant and Britannia meeting each other in American waters after the success of the British boat here ; especially as our Transatlantic friends—while handsomely acknowledging their defeat on the Clyde—claim that the Vigilant would be the victor on their side.</p>
        <p>Mr. Low, the American passenger agent, says that there is an increase of 75 per cent. over last year in the number of Americans visiting London. The season has been quite equal to the phenomenal one of 1892. "An extraordinary number," he says, "left New York for Europe in April—in fact, I believe the number was quite unprecedented. The status of our American visitors this year is superior as well. Many of the rich people have come over here to economise ; and, although this appears strange, Mr. Low insists that it is true. "It is much cheaper to some people," he argues," to shut up their town houses in New York and other big American cities, and come to Europe, than to keep open those houses and entertain company on an extensive scale." He gives an example of what he means. Last year a friend of his in America had a beautiful place at the seaside. Well, this year he has let it for six hundred pounds for four months, closed his country house, and come to Europe.</p>
        <p>The fact that the Marquis of Lorne has just written a baptismal ode to prince Edward of York seems to be regarded in some quarters as if he might possibly have his eye on the Poet .Laureate- ship. Who knows ? If the chief fanction of a Laureate be to produce courtly poetry, who more suitable than a C&amp;urt poet.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Burns has started a political organisa- tion, to which he and the other projectors of it have given the name of ",The Social Progressive Club." It is to be a rival to the Independent Labour Party. which is the creation of Mr. lieir Hardie, and its aim and object are to give an inde- pendent support to the Labour Party. Mr. Burns has for colleagues five other Radical members of Parliament. At first sight it would seem to be a matter for regret that the Parliamentary Labour Party should be thus split up, but it really makes little difference, for those associated with Mr. Burns in his new "club" are men who could not accept Mr. Hardie's uncompromising theory and his highly concentrated methods of promoting the Labour cause. Mr. Burns, who has key, his Socialism com- pletely out of sight for a long time, and who is working on the ordinary parliamentary lines, will thus attract into his new organisation all the Radi- cal:, in the House, for they are all prepared "to give an independent support to the Labour Party," and continually do :so when the opportunity arises. In the House, therefore, the " club!' will count for nothing; but outside it will draw large numbers of the working classes who are "progresAve " in spirit and aim but who are not Socialist. Its action will be directed on old orthodox trades union lines, which everybody in the House understands; and, as it will gather into its folds the Labour representa- tives in the ifouse, Mr. Burns may succeed in gal- vanising a little life into this very inert mass.</p>
        <p>While the present Parliament lasts, therefore, all will go on as usual; but when another one is returned, ana if Mr. Hardie should come back with a little compact following, we shall see Labour ranged into two camps—t he old school headed by Mr. Burns, and the new one bi Mr. Hardie. The sympathies of the mass of members on both sides, who will, of course, be landed gentleman,employers of labour, and capitalist Radicals, will all flow with Mr. Burns's party, and will continue to do so until the extre me Lett, with Mr. Hardie, Mr. Tom Mann, Mr. Ben Tillett, and the like, at their head, become a real parliamentary force. The whole labours of the last-named are devoted to the creation of a force in the country which will affect the elections, even where it cannot return members, and Mr. Hardie is accordingly rarely in the House. As a matter of strategical generalship and prudence he is perfectly right. His influence in the tiou,e is nil, as th 3 influence of every party that consists only of one vote necessarily is. But Sir Charles Dilke and Mr. Storey, and others akin to them, who will join Mr. Burns's "Progressive Club," will not accept too much of Mr. Burns's lead, and the member for Battersea is sagacious enough to know that as the labour problem develops he and they must part company. In the meantime he may accomplish something by it; at least there will be common counsel and joint action on many of the questions that will be ripe for solution long before Mr. Hardie's ideas grow to a matter of serious con- troversy.</p>
        <p>Wednesday was really rather an interesting day so far as the Treasury was concerned. The Finance Bill provides that - the estate duty clauses are to come into operation "after the expiration of the I,t day of August." That means that the estate duty will take effect on the estates of all persons who die after twelve o'clock on that night. Some very nice questions may th erefore arise. For instance, if a man should die five minutes, or even one minute, before midnight his succes--or would pay the present probate and estate duty; if he died one minute after midnight the estate would be taxed under the new duty. In the case of large fortunes the question would be one of great import- ance. For instance, if a millionaire died a minute before midnight on Wednesday his estate would have to pay forty thousand pounds to the Treasury, but if the death took place a minute after the sum claimed by the Exchequer would be eighty thou- sand pounds.</p>
        <p>I believe it has been understood to be the fact that the fate of the Registration Bill was not greatly lamented by the more advanced reformers, who regarded the measure as a purely temporary one, and as certain to lead, if withdrawn, to something more drastic and pronounced. Intimations have now been given to friendly supporters that the cause of reform will not suffer by the holding over of the bill to next session, and that a much more ambitious scl..eme is certain to come out of the delib- erations of the autumn. The main lines of the new plan have been provisionally settled, and there is reason to believe that they will meet the wishes of the critics, who say that a mere half-hearted and unsettling measure will be a great mistake. It would be a little amusing if some of the suggestions made to Mr. Shaw-Lefevre were published. •</p>
        <p>It is practically certain that opportunity will be found for a more or less exhaustive account of the views of the Nonconformist leaders, whose con- science is so often sneered at. A project is on foot for a joint undertaking, in the form of a volume to which distinguished Nonconformists will contri- bute,dealing with the several points of an advanced political creed in an authoritative fashion. The names, when they can be mentioned, will command assent in their representative character. lamin a position to say that there are several leading Church of England divines dissatisfied with some aspects of current politics who have agreed to a similar method of pronouncement, and perhaps the project may finally become that of a small volume with a title indicating that an attempt is made to put forward the whole case of the Christian con- science in politics.</p>
        <p>REPORTED SURRENDER OF J ABI7, BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>The " Central News " says it learns from an authori- tative source that Jabcz Balfour has been surrendered to the British authorities, and will return to England f(1111 with, in charge of Inspector Tonbridge. He is now in safe custody on board a vessel ft3ing the British flag.</p>
        <p>The city haying the greatest death .rat- in the world is iierinany, the proportion heir! 23 62 per 1,000 in each year. Dublin follows with 27 05, and New York with 2647.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>EXAMINER. SAT-UR I)A Y,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SAT-UR I)A Y,</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Far and away the smartest tailor gowns ever made, are of a new fabric, silk and linen duck, and handsome enough for the gayest garden fetes, be.ng really almost too dressy for street wc:ar, as the fabric looks like a rich silk matelaste. They are shown in pure white and in cream and beurre shades. Toe skirts are perfectly plain, cut with five gcr,2s. A waistcoat completes it, or a full front cf plaited chiffon. and lace is worn with the popular throe quarter leegth costume coat. For morning wear either in town or in the country, pretty costumes of write cotton duck and linen are made in exactly the same fashion. Some of the duck is clic._ked off with thread stripes of colour—tiars red or blue—or is sprinkled with dots. The linens are the plain unbleached, or dark blue, and there is also a black and -white brocaded in fine figures, which is very neat, and suitable for half mourning. A very stylish morning costume shows the skirt with strapped seams, a Norfolk bodice, closs fitting, with a step collar and fu 1 plaited besues. The bodice is perfectly neat without the smart sash of linen with its cut ti, Jer buttons which, however, gives a finish to the costume.</p>
        <p>Young ladies' hats are usually in the style of thou which are worn by grown-up people ; they are trimmed with lace or ribbons, and a generous supply of artificial flowers. Smaller children are wearing hats of a coarse burnt straw, trimmed with black ribbons and a few flowers. Pink roses tone well with these brown straws, and they can be readily replaced when soiled. The roses are grouped together in small posies, and disposed like rosettes upon the hats. White chip shapes are the most suitable head-gear to provide wben dead white frooks are worn. These are trimmed with white moire ribbons and ostrich tips in place of flowers. Tao season for pretty sun bonnets. has now set in. Many are made of nainsook and embroidery, and others of coloured prints. Wash- able hats are also composed of the same materials; the latter give a good shade to the f-ne, and are</p>
        <p>almost al pretty as the bonnets. A toque In fancy straw is trimmld with ribbon, twisted over the brim and forming an upriAt bow in front, inter- mixed with bows of guipure. Hats show the greatest variety of materials ever used in their construction. Straws are of every description, rough and smooth braids, fine and coarse ones, and those of Neapolitan are interwoven with ribbon in every conceivable colour. Novel lace-brimmed hats have only a skull crown of fancy straw, which is surrounded with a wide flounce of lace held out to form an irregularly dented and fluted brim by ocoasional wires ; a great bow. of moire ribbon in front and clusters of 113wer8 at the back form the trimming.</p>
        <p>Black has bop much in vogue this season, and nothing can really look better than a simple gown of black ctepon or grenadine, brightened with a big Ccomwellian collar of yellow or cream lace and one of the fashicnade coloured velvet neck-bands. These bands are easily rn-3de of a strip of velvet ul twice the depth of the ordinary collar, on which it is lightly tacked in rather irregular folds, the ends being ntatly hOmmed and fastelei at the back of the neck by means of very small hook 3 and eyes, piaced rather less than half an inch from the extreme ends. Dialltif3l: rose is a favourite colour for these velvet bands, although apple-green, helio- trope and deep yellow are often seen, A pretty bloute is of tobacco-coloured erepon with vest and sash of black silk uni black lace cravat. Yours lovingly, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>The Empress of China has sent five ladies to the Court of Berlin, in order to learn German manners and etiquette.</p>
