      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>AUGUST 4, 1894.</head>
        <p>AUGUST 4, 1894.</p>
        <p>Rosa Bonheur was a dressmaker's apprentice when she was a girl of 15 years.</p>
        <p>The Princess Maude of Wales is said to be known in her own family circle almost exclusively as " Harry, ' she is so playful and sportsma n-like. But she is also an excellent cook and gardener. Count Tolstoi lives in Moscow only as the guest of his wife. The winter residence at the Russian capital was built for her and the children,who grow up amid all the pleasures and joys of youth, their hermit-like father begrudging them none of the vanities of the world. One of the best known figures in Paris is Mdme. Yoer, who, upon the death of her husband, a reporter for the French press, took up his work and has conducted it with marked enterprise. In her rounds she is accom- panied by a great white poodle called" The Doctor," who permits no one to trifle with his mistress. The Empress of Austria has her hair shampooed once a month. It is still beautiful, luxuriant, and perfectly black, and, when it is let down, touches the ground as she stands, and she is tall. It is said the hair-wash requires forty eggs, and the other ingredients are obtained from no less than twenty mysterious bottles. Mrs. Humphries, "Madge" of " Truth " has been telling her history to the " Sketch ": "I began journal- istic work in 1872, " she says, "in conjunction with Mrs. Briggs, who was my sponsor in literary life. Those were days when the woman journalist, can scarcely be said to have existed. 'Women's topics' were narrowed down to the sphere of home, the world not having opened out to them then in the remarkable way it has done of late years. Then I took over the editorship of " Sylvia.'s Journal "and of two other magazines belong- ing to Ward and Lock. It was very hard work, but I held it until 1888. My connection with ' Truth ' began fortunately for me, at what might be called the psycho- logical moment. The professional beauty had just come into notice, and all the world was wild for society news. The idea was fresh then, and people did not seem able to have too muc h of it. A woman's hand touched such matters with more lightness than a man's, and it was well for those who were ready to make an opening.</p>
        <p>A Princess on .a Bicycle.</p>
        <p>Where do the rights and privileges-of a Princess end ? Is she entitled to the customary expressions of homage and royalty if she is riding a bicycle and a regiment of soldiers passes by ? And—in parentheses—does this still , bold good if the Princess is wearing the Rational cos- tume ? These interrogatories are interesting in view of the report from Turin, near where, a few days ago, a major of the Piemontereale regiment, marching with his men in the Stupimgi Valley, encountered the Princess Letitia on a bicycle, on her way to the Castle Stupimgi, accompanied by a lady and two gentlemen. The major neither stopped his men nor saluted the Princess, as was the rule, for he had not recognised her, The Princess, it seems, was irritated at this, and as soon as she re- turned to Turin, complained to General de la Batie, commander of the corps at Turin. He caused the major to be arrested, and then asked him for particulars. The major excused himself for not having recognised the Princess. He could never have imagined, he said, that her Highness would run about the city on a bicycle. General de la, B itie referred the matter to the Minister of war, and the latter reported to the King. King Hum- bert, who scorns trifling affairs, was much annoyed ,and ordered that the major should be immediately released from arrest.</p>
        <p>Dress an Important Adjunct. • There is no need to tell an American girl that dress is an important adjunct, writes Mrs. Burton Kingsland in the " Ladies' Home Journal." She lkas an instinct for taste and grace in dress, but at 16 shelias little apprecia- tion of the quality best expressed by the word " girlish- ness." It is a red-letter day with her when the gowns are lengthened and the long braid of hair is looped up on the sleek little head. No thing compliments her so much as to he thOught older than she really is, and she imagines that certain styles of dress confer this distinc- tion. She sees no charm in simplicity, and does not know that she is lovelier for the absence of ornament. In France,where the reverent ad miration for the "young girl" amounts almost to a religion, she is never permitted to wear a diamond, a bit of lace, or even a feather (quite recently the stringency of this rule has been somewhat relaxed). Those who have little or no beauty often aspire to what they call " style," and in the immaturity of their tasfe fancy that it consists in exaggeration of the prevailing fashions. If sleeves are worn large,theirs are enormous ; if fashion decrees that hats shall be tall, theirs tower in rampant aggressiveness. To tell a girl that anything ultra-fashionable is in bad taste has llt tle effect, hut educat'ng her sense of.the artistic in dress will undermine her fondness for extremes.</p>
        <p>Pickling Onions and Cabbages. fle onions anti cabbages should be selected of the proper quality and at the right season. The onions should tie globular and sOond, and have the appearance of being got in dry weather and being well dried by the air and sun previcus to being stored. The peeling,,how- ever, will prove much, and if you have suspicions that they are not right do not proceed with them. In the pickling it is highly necessary to avoid the use of metal- lic vessels as both vinegar and brine rapidly corrode brass, cgapper, lead, &amp;a., and thus become poisonous. Both brine and vinegar may be best heated or boiled in stoneware by the heat of a water bath or a stove. Common glazed earthenware should be avoided either for making or keeping the pickles in, as the glazing usually contains lead. The best malt, known as No. 22 and 24, or white wine vinegar, should be used, though wood or distilled vinegar is commonly used. In those for early use the veg€ tables should be steeped in hot or boiling brine, by which they will be ready for use in a much shorter time ; but, as a rule, for onions and cab- bage—which are esteemed for their crispness as a mark of excellence—this is inadmissible ; and the vinegar should be added cold, or at most should be only slightly warm. The spices usually employed are allspice, black and white pepper, capsicums, ginger, and mustardseed. The most economical plan is to bruise the spice and steep it for some time, or to simmer it in the vinegar before using. Cabbage, whether red or white, should be cut into thin slices,and (to save time) may be steeped in strong brine or sprinkled with salt, and allowed to lie for two days; after which it is drained for about twelve hours in a warm room ,and then put into jars or bottles, and at once covered with cold (spiced) vinegar. An- other plan is to steep the sliced cabbage in strong alum water for twelve hours, and after draining and drying it to pour the spiced vinegar on it as before. The product of this last preparation eats very fresh and crisp, but takes longer to mature than the former. As regards onions, deprive them of their outer skin, and either at once put into bottles or jars and cover with strong white spiced vinegar or previously soak teem for two days in strong brine or alum water. If required for immediate use, the vinegar must be poured on boiling hot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SCHOOLGIRL'S REMARKABLE RECoRD.</head>
        <p>A SCHOOLGIRL'S REMARKABLE RECoRD.</p>
        <p>The Boutcher School, Grange-road, Bermondsey, has just been closed for the summer holidays, and in a report concerning the work sf the schosl a remarkable case of regular attendance on the part of a girl named Ivy Freestone is mentioned. She has distinguished herself by attending seven years without being absent a single half-day, or being once late; and it is further stated that she has never missed paying her school money on a Monday morning.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Conference on Tuesday had under con- sideration an application by a cleryman of the Anglican Church for admission into connection with this Confer- ence. The president was instructed to reply that all appointments were now filled, and that there was a large list of young ministers in reserve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DESECRATION OF NATURE.</head>
        <p>DESECRATION OF NATURE.</p>
        <p>Although the Homocea Co. have had one of the greatest schemes in rural advertising put before them, they have declined to entertain it on account of the widespread disgust existing in the minds cf all who love their woods, dales, fields, streams, rocki and hills in the glory of their natural beauties, undefiled by the modern billposter. They (The Flomocea Co.), are content with the legitimate channel of the Press, and by Pamphlets containing Testimonials. They can point to cures, far more convincing than-mere assertion.</p>
        <p>TESTIMONIAL FROM BISHOP TAYLOR. HIGH BARNET.</p>
        <p>My dear old friend,—l distributed a variety of your remedies among afflicted natives,, and among missionaries in Sierra Leone. Liberia, Coast of Guinea, Congo Country, and Angola, South of Congo, I have not been over those different and distant fields since, and have no definite information in regard to their curative power. I have used HOMOCEA and have proved its healing virtue both for severe bruises and flesh wounds, and also to kill the virus of mosquitoes and chiggees (jiggers). Yours very truly (Bishop) sivar. TAYLOR. American Methodist Episcopal Mission.</p>
        <p>LORD CARRICK says FIOMOCEA cured him of bleed- ing piles, when all else failed; that he gave some t,) a labourer who was lamed by a stone falling upon him, whom it cured. A woman had a pain in the elbow and could not bend it for a year, and it cured her, and another used it for scurvy on her leg, and it was doing her good—one letter clos,s from him with the words " Its the most wonderful stuff that I ever came across." Remember that HOMOCEA subdues inflammation and allays irritation almost as soon as applied for it</p>
      </div>
