      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHAT MAKES MEN COWA RDS ?</head>
        <p>WHAT MAKES MEN COWA RDS ?</p>
        <p>If one lacks that "boldness which pr'evatieth with wise men at weak times," it is usually a result of shal- lowness of judgment or weakness in courage. Is it pos- sible that we are in danger of an epidemic of mond cowardice? We are surely not less bold, fearless, in- trepid and brave to face danger than in, the past. The rapid progress of civilization, increasing our capa unities has not lessened the examples of heroism, mix depreci- ated our reverence for exhibitions of skill, endurance, manly strength, valour and courage. The greatest present danger is from "moral Coea ar- dice." Too many fear the force of custom; tremble at the voice of criticism ; hesitate to brave public opinion ; waver in the popular balance, lacking that strength of force and character which enables them to bravely and boldly stand up for and defend all virtues, in the face of proud, courtly, even blindly, ignorant prejudice. The Rev. H. Elwyn Thomas, Pastor of Toltner's Square ' Church, London, is not one of those who bows before the voice of unbelievers against the dictates of his con- ,science and knowledge, but boldly, fearlessly stands up for, and unhesitatingly perpetuates the memory of Warner's Safe Cure, admitting the principle "moderate praise used with opportunity is not vulgar." "As a rule," he says, " I put very little faith in the much ad- vertised patent medicines of the day. The more adver- tised they are, and the more numerous the testimonials' published concerning them the weaker is my faith in their efficiency. TherA are exceptipns, however, to every rule. The modest and straightforward manner in which Warner's Safe Cure is made known to the public convinced me long ago that its proprietors had a stronger faith in what they asserted concerning its eroperties than the general run of public advertisers. Being at the time a constant sufferer from liver complaints 1 acted on my conviction, and tried the cure,' with the result that 1 almost immediately got rid of the bilious headaches, which fora long time had almost made my life a bur- den. After taking the medicine for a few weeks the bitter taste I had in the mornings, the constant bilious attacks I was subject to, and the terrible headaches I suffered from disappeared, and I became 'another man. Since then I have recommended the cure to many of my friends, always with the most favourable results.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SPEECH DAY AT THE WARRINGTON GRAMMAR</head>
        <p>SPEECH DAY AT THE WARRINGTON GRAMMAR</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The annual Speech Day and distribution of prizes took place on Tuesday afternoon at the Grammar School. There was a very large attendance of parents and friends of the pupils of the school pre- sent, and the chair was occupied by the head mas- ter, the Rev. E. J. Wilcocks. The prizes consisted of handsomely-bound books, and they were presented to the successful pupils by Dr. Charles White. Among those present in the audience were :—The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Greenall), Mr. Robert Davies, Major and Mrs. Picktnere, Mrs. Beamont, the Rector and Mrs. Willis, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, Mr. J. D. Buckton, Dr. and Mrs. Gornall, and Miss Gornall, Dr. and Mrs. Sherratt, Miss Smith, the Rev. G. B. Thurston, Miss Pierpoint, Mrs. Newman, Mrs. and the Misses Broadbent,the Rev. E. D. Garven, Mrs. Sharp. the Misses White, Miss Dawson, Mrs. Wilcocks,Miss Wilcocks, Miss Fullager, the Rev. J. J. and Mrs. Langham. the Rev. Canon and Mrs. Pen- rhyn, the Rev. C. W. Spencer Stanhope, Mr. and Mrs. S. Johnson, Councillor and Mrs. Bolton, Mr. and Mrs. W. Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. Dunbabin, Councillor and Mrs. F. W. Monks, Mr. and Mrs. Gorse, Mr. and Mrs. James Fairclough, Mr. and Mrs. Chandley, Dr. and Mrs. Willett, Mr. and Mrs. S. Davies, Miss Craik, Mr. and Mrs. Richardson, Mr. and Mrs. G. Dakin, Mr. Cochrane, Mr. and Mrs. Lacey. Mr. aed Mrs. Furness, Mrs. Pierpoint, Mrs. W. Silcock, Mrs. F. Monks, Mr. and Mrs. P. Pearce, the Misses Booth, Mr. S. Jones, Mr. and Mrs. Milling, Mr. end Mrs. Poole, Mr. and Mrs. Gough,Sergeant McCarthy, Mr. and Mrs.G.Wallington,Mrs. D. Jones, Mr. an Mrs. Jeans, Mr. and Mrs. Atkin, Mrs. Hannah, Dr. and Mrs. Adams, the Rev. J. Cooil, Mr. and Mrs. John Reynolds, Mr. and Mrs. Crossley, Mrs. Gill, Mr. and Mrs. Lythgoe, Mr. and Mrs. Wood, Mr. and Mrs. Partington. Mrs. Savage, Mr. and Mrs. Milner, Captain and Mrs. Waring, Mrs. R. Fairclough and MissJ. Fairclough, Mr. and Mrs. John Hesketh, the Misses Rimmer, Mrs. Owen, Mr. and Mrs. Moore, Mrs. and Miss Coxon, Mr. and Mrs. C. S. Burgess, Mrs. and the Misses Reynolds, Mr. and Mrs. McGregor, the Rev. J. J. and Mrs. Bryant, Councillor and Mrs. A. Woods. Mr. and Mrs. Woovenden. Mr. and Mrs. Forshaw, Miss Massey, Dr. Jack, Mrs. Brumfit, Mr. and Mrs. Gibson, Councillor and Mrs. Roberts, Mr. F. Smith, the Rev. W. R. and Mrs. Hartwright, Mr. and Mrs. W. Pierpoint, Mrs. W. Appleton, Mr. and Mrs. Place, Mrs. Morris, Mr.and Miss Gray, Miss Gandy, Mr. and Mrs. Coates, Mr. E. Gschwind, Mr.and Mrs. Bailey, Mr. and Mrs. Rose, Mrs. Yeomans, Mr. and Mrs. Madeley,Mrs. Mather, Mr. and Mrs. Walton, Miss Cash, Mr. and Mrs. Gandy, the Rev. J. and Mrs. Kirtlan, Miss Humphries, Miss Mackie, Mr. and Mrs. Davis, Mrs. Porter, Mr. and Mrs. Taylor, Miss J. Kidd, Mr. and Mrs. Kidd, Mr. T. Brandrsth, Mr. and Mrs. Moores, the Misses Royston, &amp;c. The assistant masters present were—the Rev. L. B.Radford, the Rev. J. Forbes, Mr. J. Young, Mr. Herzog, Mr. W. Woods (music master), and Mr. C. B. Aylward (drawing master). At the commencement of the proceedings, the Latin hymn " 0 gentes omnes." as sung, after which The Headmaster gave his annual report. He said : It is a great pleasure to welcome you here this afternoon, and I hope you will find our proceedings interesting. It is customary for me to give you, first of all, a report on the successes of boys—both past and present—during the year since our last Speech Day. The whole school was examined as usual under the Oxford Local Examination Authorities, and most of the boys in our sixth and fifth forms competed for the University Certificate granted by the Board. Fifteen of our candidates were successful in gaining these certifi- cates, of whom three were specially distinguished, one in German and two in religious knowledge, while four were placed in the Honours Division. In the recent open competitive examination conducted by Her Majesty's Civil Service Commissioners, to select engineer students for the Royal Navy, E. A. Davies was successful in ob- taining the 26th place. R. D. Wilcocks has passed the Entrance in Arts, University College, Liverpool,Victoria University. C. H. Ferguson has passed the necessary examinations required before registration as a Medical Student. P. H. Gornall has obtained a first class in the preliminary examination, Owen's College, Victoria Uni- versity. Our other successes include—S. Wallhead and E. E. Woods, the Intermediate examination of the In- corporated Law Society. H. H. Ferguson, first profes- sional examination of the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons, Edinburgh; H. H. Wilson, the seventeenth place among the successful candidates in the recent Militia Competitive Examinations for Commissions in the regular army ; W. H. Payton, first examina- tion for the degree of bachelor of music, Vic- toria University. (Applause.) R. Fennelly, of Owen's College, Victoria University, has won (1) the Victoria Scholarship of £5O per annum for years. (2). An exhibition of £5O per annum at Corpus Christi College, Oxford. (3). Fennelly has held four scholarships at Owens'—viz :—TI:e Oliver Heywood—The Gilchrist—The Bishop Frazer—and now the Victoria. (Applause.) E. A. Coates. of S. Cath- arine's College, Cambridge, has been elected on the results of his May College Examinations to a Foundation Scholarship of £4O per annum; H. Gorse, Beainont Exhibitioner and scholar of Queen's Colleee, Cambridge, ' has graduated B.A. in the second Class of the Classical Tripos ; R. E. Kenyon has graduated in Honours in the Final Bachelor of Science (B. Sc.) Examination, Univer- sity College, Liverpool, Victoria University ; F. C. Scot- son has successfully passed the Examination which will qualify him to be elected —when lie re aches the required age—a fellow of the Royal College of Saireeons, London, and I have just been informed that F. C. Scotson has also graduated M.B. and Ch. B. in Honours—Victoria University. (Applause.) Mr. F. Allcroft, MA., late scholar of Jesus College, Cambridge, and formerly a member of this school, has been appointed to the Head Mastership of Blackburn Grammar School. It is a very pleasing duty for me to record any honours won by the assistant masters in this school, and more especially because all the masters deserve il,cognition for the untir- ing energy and zeal they display in forwarding the real interests of the boys under their charge. Now this year we have a distinction gained by one of our staff which not only does very great honour to himself but also to the school with which he is connected. Most of us will have seen or heard of the announcement that the Adjudicators at Cambridge of the Prince Consort Prize 1894 for original historical research recommended for publication the dissertation sent in by the Rev. Lewis Bostock Radford, M.A., formerly scholar of St. John's College, now a mas- ter at the Warrington Grammar School. (Applause.) We are hoping that this success is tke prelude to another great event in November next. (Hear, hear.) I wish to call your special attention to the following resolution of the governing body at their last meeting :—Resolved " that all boys in the lower division under twelve years of age shall pay the minimum fee allowed by the scheme viz , £2 per term.' I have always felt that the tuition fees to be paid for little boys were large and I hope this reduction will meet the difficulty. (Hear, hear.) I shall be glad if you will make this alteration as widely known as possible among the friends and supporters of the school. We have made a new venture with cur School Magazine under its old title of Pnwerna, and hope it will be well supported by both past and present members of the seho4l in order to ensure its success in the future. I must not omit to mention that the eilver medal offered yearly to the champion swimmer of the schoel by Mr. Alfred Woods was won last year by A. G Thompson. (Applause.) I regret that I have to announce the retire- ment of Mr. J. C. Thompson, who has been efficially connected with us as drawing master for the last thirty years. Mr. Thompson has felt old ace creeping on apace and desires more rest. And no one deserves it more than he. Personally I shall always he grateful to Air:Thomp- son for the time and trouble which he gave in conjunc- tion with Mr. John Hceluth, to the painting and pro-</p>
        <p>ducing of the many beautiful scenes which have from time to time adorned this stage on our Speech Days during the past twelve years. (Applause.) I cannot let this ppor- tunity pass without reierring to the great loss thisschool has sustained in the removal from among Us t)y death of one of our oldest friends. The late Sir Gilticrt Green- all was a Governor of this school for neatly 46 years, his election dating from September 25th, 1848. He snb- scribed most handsomely for many years to our Uni- versity Exhibition Fund and always evinced very great interest in the success of this old foundation. (Hear, hi ar.; To his generous and ready aid I have often been indebted and it would be a surprise to many if I were to sate the large sums he has gin me privately to help on deserving boys. I have a lively recollection of the last time that he was present at our prize distribution— four years ago—and I am sure no old Botelerian will for- get his kindly smile and encouraging words on that and many other occasions when he honoured us with his presence. We desire to express how deeply we sym- pathize with Lady Greenall in her great sorrow. (Hear, hear.) Before calling on Mr. Charles White—whom we all heartily thank for his presence among us this after- noon to distribute the prizes and certificates, I wish to read you some short extracts from our report just sent us by the Oxford Local Examination. Delegates : EXTRACT FROM EXAMINER'S REPORT.—" I must speak highly of of the order and discipline of the school and of the sfficie.ncy with which everything connected with the Examination was conducted. The general standard of the work shown up by the different forms was distinctly good. The papers were done very carefully and neatly. and the handwriting was uniformly good as also the spelling. As this is the second year that I have ex- amined the school, I have been better able to riots the effect of the energy and care with which the boys in the different forms are taught. Thoroughness is a mark of the teaching. The boys have also a general desire to do their work in good style, for which the staff are to be commended. A good spirit of work seems te prevail. The tone of the school is very good." (Applause.) The prizes were then distributed by Dr. White to the succiasful students as follows : CLASSICS.—VI. Form, Kidd ; V. Form, Richardson ; Upper IV. Form, Price ,• Lower IV. Form,Reynolds ma ; 111. Form, Miles; 11. Form, Owen ml; I. Form, Hol- ford mi. •</p>
        <p>MATHEMATICS.—VI.Set, Walton ; V. Set,Wileocks ml; Upper and Lower IV. Sets Roberts. A RITHMETIC.—III. and 11. Sets, Gorse; Upper and Lower li. Sets, Monks. ENorasn HISTORY (1688-1815).—Upper and Lower IV. Forms, Bryant ; 111. and 11. Forms, Halton ; Upper and Lower I. Forms, Poole.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE.—VI. Set, Physics,Davis ; V. Set, Chemistry, Johnson ma ; IV. Set, Physical Geography, Hampton. SCRIPTURE KNOWLEDGE.—LampIugh Memorial St. Luke and I. Corinthians with the Greek, Walton; V. Form, St. Luke with Greek and I. Samuel, (Rev. M. Stevenson's prize), Richardson; IV. Form—l. Kings XII. 11. Kings, (Rev. E. C. E. Carleton's Prize) Milling mi; Massingham Memorial St. Luke, Davies ma; Upper and Lower I. Forms, Holford mi. MODERN LANGUAGES (Dr. Gornall's Prizes).—Vl. Set German, Wallace Dia ; V. Set German, Johnson ma; VI. Set French, Kidd; V. Set French, Johnson ma; IV. Set French, Forshaw ; 111. Set French, Johnson mi. DICTATION—V. and IV. Forms, Gibson ma; 111., 11. and I. Forms, Miles. WRITING.—V. and IV. Forms, Beevers ; 111., 11. and I. Forms, Wood. DnAwirib.—Advanced Model and Freehand, Place; Elementary Model, Hampton; Elementary Freehand, Mather.</p>
        <p>MICHAELMAS TERM—FORM PRIZES.—VI. Form, Kidd; V. Form, Rishardson ; Upper IV. Form, Cuppage; Lower IV. Form, Porter; 111. Form, Reynolds ma; 11. Form' Jackson ; Upper I. Form, Lyth goe. Latin Essay : Alexander ille Magnus, Kidd. English Essay : Romans in Britain, Willcocks mi. Ancient History : Greek 510, 404 B.C. Roman 268, 91 8.C., Walton. Ashton Mem- orial, Gough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>111 E KAM' N ER, ;4,kr-rtiIIDAY, AUGUST 4, 1894.</head>
        <p>111 E KAM' N ER, ;4,kr-rtiIIDAY, AUGUST 4, 1894.</p>
        <p>OXFORD LOCAL EXAMINATIONS 1893.</p>
        <p>SENIOR CANDIDATES.—Honours Division, Third Class— E. S. Kidd, Distinguished in German ; Pass Division, J. R. Evans, S. Walton. JUNIOR CANDIDATES.—HOHOUrS Division. Second Class J. S. Wallace, C. P. Gough, distinguished in Religious Knowledge. Third Class, E. A. Davis. Pass Division, J. Richardson, distinguished in Religious Knowledge, W. T. Gornall, R. G. Johnson, R. Leigh, .1. W. Lunt, U. A. Newman, J. H. Partington, E. Pierpoint, F. G. R. Wood. Recommended for election to Senior Boteler Scholar- ships, H. Gibson, W. T. Gornall, G. B. Holford. After the distribution of prizes to the pupils, an in- teresting event took place. Mr. Thompson, the draw- ing master has retired after holding the position for upwards of 30 years. Master Walton, in the name of the school presented him with a handsome silver tray. Mr. THOMPSON in reply said that he thanked them most sincerely for presenting to him on behalf of the old and present boys of the Boteler .Grammar School, that substantial and costly silver tea tray. He accepted with pride that mark of esteem and respect, and thauked the boys most heartily for their generosity and kindness in considering him worthy of that handsome testimonial, which he would always value with his other treasures. During the thirty years he had been connected with the Grammar School, his duties had been made easy and pleasant by the attention and diligence of the boys in carrying out his instructions, and also from the help and courtesy of Mr. Wilcocks, the head master, and that of his predecessor, Mr. Carey. In conclusion, on saying "Good-bye," he would ever look back with fond recol- lections and with more pleasure than he could express for the honour conferred upon him that day. (Ap- plause.) Dr. WHITE then delivered an interesting address in which he said that he never thought that in his old age, he would go on the stage—(laughter)—but he supposed that the world was a stage in which men played many parts. When he was asked to play his present part, he was rather disposed to decline the honour. However, as his education had been obtained at a grammar school, it would be ungrateful and ungracious to have refused. He first became acquainted with the Warrington Grammar School forty years ago. At that time, the Rev. Henry Bostock was head master, and Mr. Cuisins,.the second master. Be could not tell them very much that went on at the school at that thne,but one of the pupils, the Rev. Eggleton Bailey attained considerable fame as a literary man and his premature decease was much regretted all over Lancashire. rle dedicated one of his books to Mr. Cuisins, in which he said that he regarJecl , Mr. Cuisins as his prototype of a good schoolmaster. He thought that was a good indication that good work was done in school in those days. There were not many present who could recollect the Grammar School as it was then, and the number was dwindling sear by year. He would therefore describe in a few words, the head master's house. It was not built in a decorative style, for its architecture was plain enough. On the right hand side was a library, and on the other side were two capital rooms. They would .scarcely believe it, but the house was darkened by the numerous trees, some of which bore excellent fruit. Behind was a capital gar- den which supplied the house with vegetables for the whole year. The trustek s determined to rebuild the whole of the Grammar School with the result that the ! head master's quarters were not se convenient as of old, but the school was very much improved, the old garden becoming the play ground for the boys. The new master was the Rev. Carey who had been trained in the school , at Rugby, then in the zsnith of its fame and strong under • the prestige of the rule of Dr. Arnold. It was the ' centre of intellectual activity and many men went through it who became known to fame. Mr. Carey was devoted to his duties, and brought with him from Rugby the principle of Dr. Arnold of trusting to the honour of the boys for the management of the school. The excellent results showed that they were worthy of the trust. The school increased and prospered under Mr. Carey's rule. When he left the present master came, and at no time during the four centuries. which the school had flourished had the teaching ever been equal to the pres- ent time. When so much was said about education, lie could not help contributing his mite of commonplace to it. The first duty -was to teach the child how to learn, and to strengthen and develop the memory. It was all memory in the elementary stage. When they came into the secondary stage and tried to teach a boy to develop his power of thought, and to reason out a proislem to a logical conclusion without book or teacher, thee reached the crux of education. It was the duty for the teacher to endeavour to form the boy into a valuable and useful member of the commonwealth. If he were compelled to form a judicial position with regard to the school, they would be guided by the evidence of witnesses. With regard to the local adequacy of the school, he could not give a direct answer at first. There was so much" puff- ing "that ene could scarcely know what to believe. But when they looked at the successes of those who had been educated at the school not only at the old universi- ties of Oxford and Cambridge, but also at the Victoria University, they were bound to say that the education given at the school was thorough, and was net inferior to any provincial school in the kingdom. (Applause.) In giving the prizes he had not heard of any for the hest poem in English verse. They used to be accustomed at schools to have such competitions. He recollected them at his own school. He hoped after conferring with the headmaster. he would be allowed to off,r a prize for the 30, 40, or 50 best Enelish lines for the year. (Applause.) Those who attended the Speech day, would then have their ears charmed with the reeitation by the coming laureate of Warrington of the flowing lines of his own composition. (Laughter.) He had another suggestion to make, which did not affect school hours. He would like one of the under-masters to devote a little of his time in instructine the pupils in the geology and bottny of the surrounding district. The Rev. E. J. Wrr.cociss said that he would be very pleased to confer with Dr. White with regard to the Deg- lish prize poem. The boys then sang the Latin ode " Integer vita ," after which a French courd7 entitled " Apnartement meuble ft leuer,” in character was exceedingly well per- formed by some of the scholars. The folinwina were the dramatis persona! : Alphonse Duval (j-weller), R. D. Wileocics ; Edgar Colombe (draeer), J. S. Wallace ; Bond (American traveller), G. B. Flolford ; Madame Bonivard J. Richardson ; Rose Orr d itiehter). D. C. Wallace. A Latin sone. " Strenua nos exercet inertia," and an En 2.- 11811 song " Old Bell Buoy," was then given by the Loys,</p>
        <p>and afterwards a Greek play in character. The scenes were taken from " The Acharnians" of Aristophanes, and is a piece in which the Jingo, and the reveller are equally held up to ridicule. The parts were very ably taken as follows : Dicaeopolis, E. S. Kidd ; Boetian, S. Walton ; Nicarchus, W. T. 'Gornall ; Larnachus, C. P. C,Dugh ; servant, E. A. Davies. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NEW SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>We read much of "the New Woman," the "New Man," and so forth ; it is time a word was said for" the New Science." What the old science could not do the new science accomplishes. Diseases which baffled the old medicine, disappear before the new. An example is culled from the" Leeds Saturday Journal." A represen- tative of that enterprising paper relates a perfectly au- thenticated cure in the case of a railway, clearing-house man, Mr. Arthur Benn, living at 42, Domestic-street, Holbeck, Leeds.</p>
        <p>Mr. Benn had a stroke of paralysis in its most intract- able form—Locbmotor Ataxy—had been both an in and an out-patient at the Leeds Infirmary (where inquiries were made on the subject) and was pronounced not merely incurable, but doomed to a further development of living death. Paralysed in the lower limbs and utterly helpless, the palsy was creeping upwards to the arms, so that soon he. would be unable to move hand or foot. But Mr. Berm, the incurable, had been cured. The impossible had been achieved. Locomotor Ataxy has been cured. The reporter saw this cripple run up and down stairs, as if nothing had ever been the matter. This impossibility of the old scie nee has been accom- plished by the use of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, the remedy whose extraordinary effects fill the papers with" modern miracles."</p>
        <p>The reporter did not take the case on trust. He ob- tained confirmation from numerous impartial witnesses, including Mr. W. Benn, brother of the patient, who said that he had never expected him to recover from the fearful malady which for two years had rendered him helpless. Just before he commenced to take Dr. Williams' Pink Pills the doctor told him he never need expect to walk again. The paralysis was spreading to the upper parts of the body. The doctor c3uld not pre- vent the arms from becoming affected, and poor Benn had gloomy forebodings of spending the rest of his life in bed.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are an unfailing cure for rheumatism, sciatica, neuralgia, partial paralysis, loco- motor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, pros- tration, etc. They are a specific for troubles of the female sex, and in men effect a radical cure in eases arising from mental worry, overwork, or excess of any kind. They are sold by all chemists, or may be had direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are never sold loose ; any dealer who offers substitutes is trying to de- fraud the wooden box must be in a pink wrapper, bear. ing full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Not a purgative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>CURIOSITIES OF SCHOOL EXAMINA-</head>
        <p>CURIOSITIES OF SCHOOL EXAMINA-</p>
        <p>TIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry J. Barker, to whom we are indebted for a knowledge of many of the current curiosities of school examinations, relates the following instances that came under his notice :—" I remember a second standard boy giving me a reply during the reading lesson which puzzled me not a little at the time. In the piece chosen to be read, the word 'invention' occurred; and I asked one boy who had just read his prescribed portion, to try and tell me the meaning of it. The • child hesitated ; and, as I knew he was of a very nervous temperament, I did not hurry him ; but afforded him ample time to think. Presently I said to him' encouragingly. 'Well now, what is invention—eh?' Picture my amazement when I heard the boy falter out, Please, sir, it's either a little boy or a little girl.' I could not be in any way vexed with the lad, because I felt he had gravely given me an apparently irrelevant answer rather than not try at all. Little did I suspect at the time that the scholar's reply was really a thoughtful one, and the plain expres- sion of a fairly logical inference. A little later in the lesson I turned to the boy and said, Now let us know why you answered that the word 'invention' stood for either male or female?' Why, sir,' the scholar responded, I have read, or else have been told, that necessity is the mother of invention, and so I thought that it was sure to be either a boy or else a girl !* " In another case, the following naive reply of a little five-year-old girl was communicated by a schoolmistress to Mr. Barker, who relates the story in sue of his papers of amusing examples :—" A governess was delighting the children with the ever-welcome description of Eden and Paradise, with the ambrosial fruit and its music- murmuring streams, and its two joyous and privileged human occupants. She related to them how the man Adam was first created fresh from the hand of God, and how Eve was afterwards formed by the same Almighty Power. Presently she paused and asked the question "And why, children, did the good God create the woman Eve for the man?" "Please, ma'am," then answered the five-year-old, "to make Adam his coffee mornin's !" A parish minister was examining a class of small boys on their reiding exercises. The lesson was on the life of a pious man. The minister, as a test of their know- ledge, offered a penny to the first who should tell him what a pious man was. For a moment silence reigned supreme, and then an urchin at the bottom of the class, whose joyful visage proclaimed that he was is possession ol the grand secret, joyfully snapped his fingers in great glee, and cried out, "I can—l can, Mr. Minister ! " "Well, my boy, will you kindly tell us what it means ?" "It's a man that bakes pies, sir ! " _ .</p>
        <p>. . This is the latest of the many amusing stories anent the subject. In a St. Pancras school for little boys and girls, an examiner was questioning the pupils about cele- brated generals. "And now," said the examiner, "we come to the Duke of Wellington. Can any of you tell me who was the Duke of Wellington?" A pause en- sued, but at last a little girl held out her hand as a signal that she was prepared to answer. "That's a clever little girl," remarked the questioner approvingly. "Well, my dear, who was the Duke of Wellington?' "Mr. McGuire, sir," responded the little maid triumph- antly. The examiner retired to recover from his as- tonishment, and it was subsequently explained to him that Mr. McGuire was the landlord of the Duke of Wel- lington public house. to which the girl occasionally went for father's Sunday dinner beer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TERRIBLE MOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE TERRIBLE MOUSE.</p>
        <p>One of the standing jokes of the comic papers is the terror with which women are supposed to regard the harmless and timid mouse. It would seem, however, from a paper in " McClure's Magazine" on " Wild Beasts in Captivity," that t'i-e king of beasts and the elephant share this feminipe terror of the little rodent. The writer says :—One day, Philadelphia, wishing to test the affection popularly supposed to exist between a lion and is mouse, put a mouse in the c 3) of a full- grown Nubian lion. The lion saw the mouse before he was fairly through the bars, and was after him instantly. Away went the little fellow, scurrying across the floor and squeaking in fright. When he had gone about ten feet the lion sprang, lighting a little in front of him. Tito mouse turned, and the lion sprang again. This was repeated several times, the mouse traversing a shorter distance after each spring of the lion. It was demon- strated that a lion is too quick for a mouse, at least in a large cage. Finally the mouse stood still, squealing and trembling. The lion stood ever, studying him with in- terest. Presently he shot out his big paw and brought it down directly on the mouse, but so gently that the mouse was not injured in the least, though held fast be- tween the claws. Then the lion played with him in the most extraordinary way, now lifting his paw and letting the mouse run a few inches, and then stopping him , again as before. Suddenly the mouse changed his tac- tics, and instead of running when the lion lifted his paw, sprang into the air straight at the lion's head. The , lion, terrified, gave a great leap back, striking the bars with all his weight, and shaking the whole floor. Then he opened his great jaws and roared and roared again, while the little mouse, still squealing, made his escape. Of the two the lion was the more frightened. It is a fact well known in all menageries that a mouse will frighten an elephant more than will a locomotive. Let one appear in an elephant's stall, and the elephant, his mountain of fl-sh quivering, his trunk lashing the air, will trumpet in abject terror : and he will not recover for hours afterwards. The trainers say that what the elephant fears is that the mouse will run up his trunk. There is a tradition that a mouse really did this in one instance while an elephant was sleeping, and ca,ised the elephant such intense pain that he had to be killed.</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED WITHOUT OPERATION.—S. J. SHERMAN. Hernia Specialist, 64, Chancery-Lane, London ; 26, King. street, Manclieste-; Book on rupture and evil of trusses post free Li —A..vt.</p>
        <p>At Leeds Assizes on Monday, Albert Dews, aged 28, a moulder. was sentenced to death for the murder of his child at Alverthorpe, near Wakefield. The convict, who had committed the crime when frenzied with jealousy, was recommended to mercy. MEXICAN CURB. VNOWN AS REV. JOSEPH HOLMES' MEXI- Ix CAN Frescriptien, is the only guaranteed Remedy for all those who suffer from the errors of youth, nervous weakness, exhausted vitality, kidney, bladder, gravel. prost- ate and kindred complah.ts. and has stood the test for 20 years. Send a self-addressed stamped envelope to JosEeii HOLMES' Remedy Co., Bl:omsbury Mansions, Bloomsbury Square, London, and get full particulars of this great Remedy free of charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>PRIMROSE LEAGUE MEETING AT G RAPPENHALL B_EYES.</p>
        <p>A garden party was held on Saturday, at Grap- penhall Heyes, by the Grappenh all Habitation of the Primrose League, by permission of Mr. J. C. Parr J.P. The grounds were thrown open to the members and others from half-past two to eight p m., and during this time the number of persons who passed through the gates is estimated at from 600 to 700. A large number of visitors were allowed t 3 fish in the ornamental ponds, and one or two succeeded in bringing to land good catches, while others rambled about the gardens and grounds, which are just now at their best, or walked through the conservatories. Then excellent Punch and Judy shows were to be seen on the lawn near the house, and during the afternoon a varied and well played selection of music was submitted by St. Paul's Band, under the direction of Mr. Smith. A cricket match—St. Elphin's, Warrington, v. Grappenhall—was played in one of the adjoining fields, and from four to 5.30 an excellent tea was provided at a moderate charge in a large tent. The caterer was Mr. T. 0. Speakman, of the Saracen's Hotel, Wilderspool. Mr. Alan Lister Kaye, the hon. secretary, was responsible for the whole of the arrangements. Mr. and Mrs. Parr, the latter holding the position of Ruling Councillor of the Habita- tion, were present and manifested considerable interest in the proceedings, and they were visited during the afternoon by the Mayor and Mayoress of Warrington (Alderman and Mrs. Greenall), Mr. and Mrs. Wynne Jones, Mr. Roger Parr, Major Nicholson, J.P., and other ladies and gentlemen, several of whom presided over the tea tables.</p>
        <p>After the tea, a meeting was held, Mrs. Parr, ruling councillor, presiding. Mr. Parr in addressing the meet- ing bade them all welcome. Various events be said had happened since their last meeting. There were some events upon which they might congratulate themselves. To begin with he was sure they would all join with him in congratulating themselves and their country upon the birth of a sovereign Prince, which now gave them four generations of their Royal family. Let them wish them all a long life, and health and strength to enable them to do their duty to the country. There was another matter upon which he should like to congratulate them, and that was the finishing of the Ship Canal. There was also another matter upon which he might touch. It was not a subject of congratulation. He referred to the change of Ministry. They had changed a Salisbury for a Rosehery, and he was not sure whether the change had been a good one. For himself he thought they were very much better where they were. (Hear, hear.) Pro- bably, however, opportunities would occur before very long when the country would be again consulted as to whether it preferred a Salisbury or a Rosebery. He con- gratulated the members of the League for the work done during the past year. He named the wardens. Mr. Hankey and the Secretary in particular, to whom they were very much indebted for the success of the day's proceedings, as well as for the success of the many ether ga-herings which had been held. There were in every hive working bees and those who directed matters, but let them all endeavour to be workers and to make the League, what he was sure they all wished it to be, a great success.—Mrs. Parr then presented Mr. Hankey with the badge, expressing the pleasure which it afforded her to do so. She was very slSrry the other warden was unable to be present.—Mr. Hankey briefly returned thanks for the compliment which had been paid to him.—Major Nicholson said that on an occasion like the present, when there was dancing to follow, brevity was the soul of wit. They did not care to listen to long speeches, although there was one important duty which must be discharged before they separated,and that was a hearty vote of thanks, in which all would heartily join, to Mr. and Mrs. Parr for the •pleasure and enter- tainment which they had provided for them that day.— The motion was carried by acclamation, three cheers being given for Mr. and Mrs. Parr, Mr. Roger Parr and Mr. Hankey.—Mr. Parr, in returning thanks in the name of the Ruling Councillor and himself, said it had been a great pleasure to them to see such a large attendance and so many happy faces. There was one matter he had forgotten to mention, and that was that their mem- ber, the Hon. Alan de Tatton Egerton, was unfortun- ately unable to be with them, as he was busy at the House of Commons. They could readily understand that members were unable to spare even a few hours from their onerous duties, otherwise Mr. Egerton would have been with them that day. (Applause.) —The pro- ceedings on the lawn then terminated. The remainder of the evening was spent in an enjoyable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>REV. JOHN YONGE ON THE NEED OF THE RELIGION OF THE DAY.</head>
        <p>THE REV. JOHN YONGE ON THE NEED OF THE RELIGION OF THE DAY.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, the Rev. John Yonge took as his test the words "Why call ye Me, Lord! Lord! and do not the things that I say ?" Luke vi. 46, and referred to a sermon preached on the previous Sunday afternoon in Westminster Abbey by Archdeacon Farrar on "Reality, the need of the religion of the day." It seemed to him to be a great cause for thankfulness that in these days of religious formalism and indifference there should be men of God like Archdeacon Farrar who dared to proclaim from the high places of the Established Church, truths and doctrines which were by no means ordinarily promulgated from episcopal pulpits. If such preachers were many instead of few, and if Anglican pulpits did but generally instead of rarely thus boldly testify to the truth of Christ, there would be greater hope of the masses of Church-going Englishmen being really converted to God. For it was the kind of mess- age which the Archdeacon so fearlessly delivered to the Abbey congregation that church and chapel-going people needed supremelyto hear. And if they did not only hear but heeded, then the professing Christians of England would become the salt of England, the light of England, the saviours and sanctifiers of the nation. He then quoted from the report of Archdeacon Farrar's sermon, in which the rev, gentleman said "Cease to oppress or try to oppress our intellects and our consciences by teaching for doctrines the commandments of men ; fling your worthless idols to the bats; learn that the Gospel means to believe in the Lord Jesus Christ; and because you believe in Him rely not upon saying 'Lord! Lord!' but do the things which he says." Mr. Yonge said that kind of talk by a dignitary of the Eitablished Church,in the great historic abbey, before a vast congregation drawn from all parts of the metropolis, probably of the kingdem, and even of the world, merited the special report, and deserved to be echoed far and wide. It must, he thought, be their feeling that it was a testi- mony and a message of thrilling force and manifest timeliness. If, indeed, any justification could be found for a Church beim; established by the State it would be in the fact of God's truth being thus powerfully enforced by its preachers upon the national mind. Assuredly this kind of faithful preaching was infinitely more tend- ing towards the nation's spiritual betterment than when the established clergy preached sacerdotal doctrine or when they feebly attempted the beatification of million- aires who had been content to make their wealth from the degradation and misery of the people.</p>
        <p>In Persia, among the arist,)cra cy, a visitor sends notice an hour or two before calli i”.r, and gives a day's notice if the visit is one of great importance.</p>
      </div>
