      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT ON THE CHESHIRE LINES RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The inquest was held on Friday at the White Hart Inn, Sankey-street, by Mr. S. Brighouse. Mr, Simpson was present on behalf of the Cheshire Lines Company. Jos. Taylor, the husband, said that he was a foreman plate- layer and lived at Railway Cottages, Partington. His wife was 41 years of age. He was informed of the acci- dent to his wife on Saturday night by his son,and found her on the Partington side of the railway bridge. She was taken home, and next day was taken to the War- rington Infirmary. She crossed the railway bridge because the ferry closed at 8 o'clock, and the nearest way by the, highroad between Cadishead and Partington was round Warburton bridge which was a long distance. —Mr. Simpson said that there was a great number of persons trespassing on the line, and it would require a man night and day to keep them away.—Sybil Clarke, step-daughter to the deceased, said that she was with the deceased at the time of the accident. When they had got on the railway bridge over the river Mersey, a train was coining from Warrington,and the noise of that, pre- vented them from hearing one coming from the other direction. This train caught her mother and threw her a gainst the railway bridge. Assistance was procured and she was taken home. It was dark at the time the accident took place.—Alice Stocks Butler, a nurse at the Infirmary, said that the deceased died on Thursday at 545 p.m. She suffered from fractured thigh and inter- nal injuries. Her account of the accident agreed with the account of the last witness.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental Death."</p>
        <p>The inquest was fixed for six o'clock but Mr. John Toft, baker, did not arrive at the time, and Mr. Birtles had to be requkitioned in his place. Mr. Toft came near the close of the inquest, and after it was over, he was called into the room and asked to state the reason of his failing to attend at the right time. He said that he left home in time for the inquest, but on the way ho met some of his men, and in talking over business, the inquest slipped his memory. He was very sorry to have kept them waiting.—Mr. Toft's explanation was accepted as satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ALDERMANIC VACANCY IN THE WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE ALDERMANIC VACANCY IN THE WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives have decided to nominate Councillor Bolton for the vacancy in the Town Council caused by the death of Alderman Ashton. Mr. Bolton is the senior councillor, and is the chair- man of the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee, and is also a member of the Street Improvement and Lighting, Rivers, Water, and General Purposes Committees. He has been Li the Council for twelve years,having been elected for the first time in 1882. He at present sits for the Howley ward,and his election will therefore cause a vacancy in that ward, but as his term of office expires in November it is not pro- bable that the ward will be contested. This will be the third successive aldermanic elec- tion in which the principle of seniority has been recognized,indeed,it was principally on this ground that Councillor Bolton has been chosen. The other two elections were those of Alderman Ashton and Hutchinson. If this principle becomes the rule in ' aldermanic elections, it will remove much of the friction between the two political parties. It is already the practice in Manchester and many other boroughs to allow the oldest councillor to fill the vacant seat in the aldermanic bench unchallenged. The next senior councillor after Councillor Bolton is Councillor Roberts, who has already served ten years in the Council, and if the same arrangements were carried out he of course will be the next alder- man when there is a vacancy. The Liberals have not yet decided their course but it is not expected that they will effer any opposition to Councillor Bolton.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 21st July, 1894. During the week, which was the 29th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrington 33 14 Latchford 4 2</p>
        <p>Total 37 The excess of births over deaths was 21. The death- rate from all causes was equal to 14.8 per 1,000, against 20.1 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 46.2 the corresponding week of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 1.8 per 1,000, against 2.2 the average rate of the four previous weeks,and against 331 the cOrresponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were- -0-71 1-5 5-Ll5 15-25 25760 60 up. _</p>
        <p>2 0 2 3 1</p>
        <p>16 The deaths included-2 from scarlet fever, 1 from erysipelas, 4 from lung disease, 7 from brain disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 10 cases. Total number of patients in Hospital week ending 21st July, 34. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 58.4 Fah, and therefore about the same as the preceding week. The maximum temperature 72 F., was record..d on the 15th inst., and the minimum temperature 49 F., on the 20th inst.</p>
        <p>The rainfall measured 1.32 inch. The general direction was W., the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1260 miles. . JOHN H. GORNALL. Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
