        <p>TIIE POOR lAN'S FRIEND. SKIN VII.; INVALUABLE 0 i NTINIENT (as .1 iginally pre- oKIN tysEAsEs pare,-1 by G. L. 1om:v:1's, M.D.) ts uti.fAiling renie,ty for wounds z of every doscriptiim, Scorbutic, Q-KIN F'4°. Erti Discus, Inflamed Eyes, • SKIN DISEASES. DISEASES. Iu ZOBLIITS' ALTERATIVE PILLs, L,KIN DISEASES. For. the BLOOD and SKIN, 0 are very effectual in the cure of that form of skin disease which SKIN DISEASES. shows itself in painful cracks in his,l.&lt; in rindscsi v liseases, c; KIN DIEASE•3. t 'vile Pe:, pr.totairts, nriuport.auu Dy UlleMieta.</p>
        <p>Working-class THE proper housing of the working- Houses. classes in large and growing towns which was lately referred to in these columns —is now recognised in many quarters as one of</p>
        <p>the most vital and pressing problems of the dal. "Overcrowding is murder " says one writer, and</p>
        <p>_ this startling dictum is almost justified by some remarkable statistics which are given in the "Century "magazine by Dr. ALBERT SHAW. The statistics, which have been compiled with the usual German thoroughness and detail, relate to</p>
        <p>Berlin, but there can be little doubt that prettY much the same results follow wherever there is overcrowding. It has been found, says Dr.SHAW,</p>
        <p>that although one-room dwellers were only one- sixth as numerous as the three-room dwellers,</p>
        <p>their rate of mortality was about twenty-three</p>
        <p>times as high, and the actual number of -deaths among them was four times as great. Compared</p>
        <p>with dwellers in houses of more than four rooms:</p>
        <p>the mortality of the one-room dwellers was at s thirty times greater rate. The life-saving results</p>
        <p>of decent housing are demonstrated withindispg-</p>
        <p>table clearness. Crowd families into one room</p>
        <p>and the inevitable result is the appalling death - rate of over 160 per 1,000; or about one-sixth Of their entire number. Give them two rooms t° live in and there is a drop in the death-rate as IoW as 22.5 per 1,000. Three-room dwellers escaped with the marvellously low rate of 7-5, and the well-to-do people, who had four rooms or more for their household, suffered by death only at the marvellously low rate of 5.4 per 100 What more convincing argument for the decent housing of the poorer classes could be adduced than figures such as these ? The death-rate statistics for overcrowded English towns and cities are usually arrived at in regard to the tote' population, and we are as a rule contented if the rate does not rise much higher than twenty Per thousand of population. But, as Dr. SHAW pOintS out, if this twenty or twenty-five per thousand were minutely analysed it would probaW be found to vary for different classes from three or four per thousand to two hundred per thousand according to the house accommodation they biLa at command. If this be the case, it is clearly within the mark to say—leaving out of account for the time being the moral and intellectual sides of the question—that the sanitary reform that Is most urgently needed in these days is tile clearance of overcrowded areas and thef provision of improved dwellings. Maoris Od pounds are spent every year. in working nil perfecting sanitary methods and appliances, but e° long as blocks of. insanitary property are lust the breeding grounds of disease a considerable part of this money must be wasted. Forte 'lately, public opinion is ripening on this sulle(" and already some of the .most enlightened €lofs perations have hegun to move. Birmingham bits been the pioneer in many municipal reforT and in respect to • the provision of w°r men's cottages it has set a good e'samPlet; We are told in the " New Review" that wile° 11 street of insanitary "back-to-back " houses .fe into .the hands of the Birmingham CorporaPcgl under an irnrrcvernent cc Liis tlic!.&lt;) liun3c2.". , demolished; and twenty-two cottages contava</p>
      </div>
