        <p>rooms each, and all possible provision for the "• lath of the occupants erected in their place. The riew cottages were neatly and attractively built at cost of £4,000, and were quickly let to working- :au farnilies,at a weekly rental of 53.6 d. Although 7teee cottages are situated quite near the centre • }3lrmlngliam and are liberally provided with °I. 3" apace, it is estimated that, after making the ree4sary deductions in the shape of rates and ja4s, the rents yield a net income sufficient, when _lltftest and sinking fund are provided for, to pay b..' average ground rent of 11d. per square 'll per annum for seventy-five years. If this be done at Birmingham it should not be Possible to do something similar at smaller Phices.</p>
        <p>THE Parliamentary Opposition " W'n't Play." under the joint leadership of Mr. BALFOUR and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN 11;1111 to be almost on the point of demoralisation. th eir latest move is certainly about the worst could have made. The leaders of the coali- 1411°n have for months past encouraged—certainly ch„.? have not discouraged as they ought to have ;ue—obstruction of the most flagrant and time- ting character. As Sir Wm. HARCOURT said IN," admirable speech which he delivered on tc"uesday, they and their followers have opposed erything and protracted everything, and have 4'4l)loyed every artifice to delay when they could defeat. This being the case, the Government h'e had to-meet the emergency which has arisen. e Government are responsible for conducting business of the nation. They are pledged to ittl'aillote a large programme of important measures titt, elanY of these measures have, as yet, made 417 or no progress. They have the majority to lud them and the country expects the majority tiefule• hi view, there fore, of their responsibili- and of tl4e persistent obstruction against 1461 eh they have to contend, the Govern- gave notice of their intention to g the debate on the Irish Evicted Th4.ltlits Bill to a close at a fixed time. feelte2 ef course, cut the ground from under the , boe ef the professional obstructives who have Piling up amendments one upon another un- ' ti it is said, something like seventy stood in the sle of one person. The entirely justifiable action " Government was met by Mr. BALFOUR with NterY foolish move. "If," in effect said Mr. eatitP°l3l3,awe the Opposition—and the minority— have our own way overthis Bill we shall e from the debate altogether." Such a do- is not merely foolish, it displays sheer 18hness. It is bringing into the transaction pot atiOnal and imperial offairs the temper of the 11144)4nt juvenile who, because he cannot get all tiletZwn Way, declares he "won't play." Such a is reued as this of conducting any kind of business Neg,at'ded with disdain by every one who has 'led a state of mental maturity. And in the 464eliairit ease there was even less excuse for it than ror the very people who are so eager to it:448,, this Evicted Tenants Bill have been assert- ort:l' at the Bill will be thrown out by the House oke 4.lrcls. Why, therefore, may it not:be asked, e 37 so eager to discuss at inordinate length a Wurnre which is already doomed to extinction ? Qua ,a!3,Bvier to this question supplies the key of tinn:_uole situatioß. The Opposition have no giver!) desire to amend the Evicted Tenants Bill 11011" they believe will receive its quietus in the kabse of Lords. Their real object is to cause tettlin, to Waste time, and thereby render this hitlie-ti and, indeed, this Parliament as barren and tliq3 possible. For frustrating that design ~.71s'erlament are to be commended.</p>
        <p>ADDITIONAL PATRONS OF THE RUNCORN TONIC SOL FA</p>
        <p>CHOIR. A letter has very recently been received by the secre- tary of the above combination from the Dake of West- minster, consenting to become a patron of the ch)ir.</p>
        <p>SMALL-PDX IN RUNCORN. We regret to state that another man also of the boat- ing fraternity has been removed to the Runcorn Infecti- ous Diseases Hospital suffering from small-pox. The type of the disease is very mild in this case, and as the sufferer was expeditiously transferred from his place of abode to the hospital where he was immediately treated, it is expected that he will soon recover. The man who was first taken to the hospital a few weeks ago is now thoroughly convalescent.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SUNDAY SCHOOL SCHOLARS' TREAT. In connection with the Primitive Methodist Sunday School the annual outing of scholars took place on Wed- nesday, Overton Hills being the rendezvous. The new minister, the Rev. Thomas Braman, with the Rev. H. R. Didcock, and Messrs: Soyle and Hillidge, superinten- dents, accompanied the party. After indulging in different pastimes, a well-served tea was partaken of and enjoyed subsequent to which the scholars returned korne.</p>
        <p>HALTON-ROAD WESLEYAN CHAPEL, RUNCORN. It will be seen from an advertisement in our columns that the annual choir sermons in connection with this place of worship will be preached on Sunday morning and evening next. The Rev. W. Arrowsmith will occupy the pulpit in the morning and the Rev. J. Hanby in the eventne. Special hymns and anthems will be sung,Miss Clara Taylor, the young and accomplished daughter of Mr. S. Taylor. J.P., having consented to undertake th.e principal solos. The collections will be in aid of the choir fund, and it is to be hoped that on this occasion the worshippers will be more than usually liberal in their offerings. RUNCORN VICTORIA CRICKET CLEM : GENERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A -general -meeting of the members of this club was held on Wednesday evening, Mr. W. Bettley occupying the chair. After the treasurer, Mr. Leathwcod, had submitted his report, which was a very satisfactory one, the election of officers for the ensuing year was proceeded with. Mr. C. Burrows was appointed captain and Mr. Walter Howard, sub-captain of the Ist team ; Mr. H. Pendlebury, treasurer; Messrs. C. Burrows and F. Brad- ley, secretaries ; and Messrs. W. Bettley, Pennington and Leach, committee. Mr. H. W. Wright was chosen captain of the 2nd team, with Mr. H. Bettley, sub- captain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>It jStr,CCESS OF A RuNCORN MEDICAL STUDENT. 4(11 Pleasing to report that Mr. Henry Edward Annett, ,tzo:r.T. H. Annett, of Runcorn, received the de- , °I Bachelor of Medicine and of Surgery with 144 11 °ll Saturday last, at the Victoria University, PDOOI. BLACK BLENDED TEA, 2id.per quarter lb.—Advt -""` WESLEYAN CONFERENCE : LOCAL MINISTERIAL The CHANGES. Weal reVisiotl of the ministerial appointments at the 447151111 Conference were completed late on Saturday wY the Stationing committee,who decided that the 1.110tti,,141• George, of Lancaster, should take over the k.layej hdency of Runcorn, and not the Rev. Edward °lles. The Rev. W. Arrowsmith will leave Run- t. _"cupy the first position in the St. Helens Cir-</p>
