      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>LIGHTER SUCCESSFULLY LAUNCHED _AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>A LIGHTER SUCCESSFULLY LAUNCHED _AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Old Quay Yard, Runcorn, in the presence of many interested persons,a newly constructed lighter for use on the Ship Canal, was successfully launched. She has been built of English oak and pitch pine from the designs of Mr. F. Wiswall, the local engi- neer of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, her dimen- sions being 103 ft. 6ins.long, 21ft.31n.broacLand aft.depth in midship. She has a carrying capacity of 320 tons,at a draft of 7ft.; and is especially adapted for carrying a large amount at little cost. Her number is 201, and she is the first of several to be built by the Bridgewater under- taking for navigating the canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>DOG SAVES A DROWNING LID AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>A DOG SAVES A DROWNING LID AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At about 6.15 on Sunday evening a lad named Fred- erick Atkins, ten years of age, son of Mr. Frederick Atkins, Suffolk-street, was saved from drowning by a large dog belonging to Mr. Win. Taylor, of Collier-street. It appears that the boy by some means fell into the Francis Dock, in the Bridgewater Canal and the dog which is a large and powerful one, seeing the danger plunged into the water and succeeded in bringing the lad to the side of the dock. The dog was then relieved of his burden by Mr. Joseph Rose, dock master. This is the second life which this valuable dog has saved from drowning. DEATH OF A RUNCORN CAB-PROPRIETOR.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning about 1.30., Mr. Peter Brereton, of 4, Penketh-lane,Runcorn,cab-proprietor, breathed his last at the age of 46 years. The news of his demise was rather unexpected for although his health had been fail- ing for the last two or three years, yet he was seen walking in the neighbourhood of Penketh-lane on the alterßoon previous to the day of his death. The com- plaints from which he had suffered were pneumonia and dropsy ; and for these he had been treated by Dr. Robin- son, of Runcorn. The deceased was a well-known and respected man in Runcorn and district and was a mem- ber of the Star and Freedom Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows. His remains were interred on Thursday afternoon in the Runcorn Cemetery, amid many manifestations of grief and respect.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY: SIXTY-FOURTH HALF-YEARLY REPORT.</p>
        <p>This society, whose annual meeting will be held on Tuesday next, have just issued their half-yearly report and statement of accounts, which show that the sales have amounted to £44,304 63. 21.; an increase of £223 16s. 10d. on the half-year previous. The committee re- ports that "The amount disposable, after charging all expenses, including depreciation and interest on capital, deducting the cheques purchased, reducing Ship Canal shares £lOO, special depreciation land, buildings, and fixtures £lOO, leaves the nett amount £7,057 185.7 d. We recommend a dividend of 3s. in the 2 on £44,420 of cheques account books brought in,which will amount to £6,663, leaving a balance of 2394 18s. 7d. to the reserve fund."</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE ANNUAL EXCURSION TO</p>
        <p>G RAPPENuALL.</p>
        <p>By referring to our advertising columns it will be seen that this annual event, which always excited a great deal of interest takes place on Monday (Bulk Holiday). The boat will leave the Top Locks, Runcorn, at 8.30 in the morning and Delph Bridge at 9.0. A pleasant sail will then be commenced from this point'of the Bridge- water canal down to Grappenhall. The journey thither will be considerably enlivened by the Mission Drum and Fife Band, the members of which will play choice musi- cal selections. At Grappenhall every convenience will be provided for the excursionists in the nature of refresh- ments Szc. Tea will be served in the large room at a moderate charge, and those persons who travel by the excursion will have the privilege of freely entering and inspecting the beautiful paik belonging to Mr. John Nay- lor, which is only a short distance from Latchford rail- way station.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WIDNES WOUNDING CASE.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES WOUNDING CASE.</p>
        <p>Thomas Bulkoski (28), labourer, was indicted at Liverpool Assizes on IA ednesday, for having, at Widnes, on the 30th April, unlawfully and maliciously wounded Sylvester Oustra with intent to Eh him grievous bodily harm. Mr. Formby prosecuted, and Mr. Collingwood Hope defended. Prosecutor said he resided in Widnes. On Monday night, the 30th April, he was aroused by someone knocking at the door. He went downstairs and, opening the door, saw the prisoner and four other men. The prisoner had a penknife in his hand, and he asked the prisoner why he came knocking at so late an hour. The prisoner thereupon struck him on the head with the blade of the knife several times. Prosecutor fell down, and the prisoner and the other persons ran away.—For the defence, it was contended that the evi- dence for the prosecution was not reliable, and that it had not been conclusively shown that it was the prisoner who inflicted the injuries.—The jury found the prisoner guilty of unlawfully wounding, and he was sent to gaol for twelve mouths with hard labour.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S FRESH IRL9H DAIRY BUTTER, led, per lb. is excellent,—Adut.</p>
        <p>THE WORK OF TFIE RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE.</p>
        <p>According to the Runcorn Parish Church Magazine for August, twenty three cases have been visited by the purse from June 25th to luly 25th, viz.---eleven chronic cases, seven medical cases, and five surgical cases. Of these eight have been taken off the books, and eight are fresh cases. This leaves 23 cases on the books. Twenty nine patients have received occasional visits • eight of these have been taken off the books, leaving 21. Num- ber of visits paid to cases, 239; numbr of occasional visits, 105 ; total, 344. Nurse Potter has been succeeded by Nurse Buckenham, from the South London Nursing Institution, who took up the work on the 9th July. Doubtless under her care the work of the Nurse Com- mittee, which in October will have been carried on for five rears, will be mai itained at a very high standard of efficiency. It cannot be too widely known that the nurse mar be called in by any patient ; that she is strictly desired to carry out the injunctions of the medical attendant ; that she is not to be a district visi- tor or general almoner, but a surgical aril medical nurse whose chief work is to care For the bodies of the poor when sick and afflicted, but to do this of course from high Christian motives. COLLIER'S BREAD 11 the best money can buy.—Adet. MEETING OF RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE: THE FORTHCOMING DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this . Order was held on. Saturday evening, Nfr. George Sutton occupying the chair. A very interesting report, reviewing the position of the society, as well as a nuMber of valuable sugges- tions, were Diode by Mr. Samuel M. Tempnley (one of the auditors). Mr. Temperley stated that notwithstand- ing thefaet that they had paid £BB during the past sic months to members who had been ill, the society hal gained £l4 184. :Nine new members had been initiated.• making a total of 280 adnits, of whom ISO were married and 130 single. He proceeded to say that the cadets or :juveniles numbered 78 in good standing, and were worth .£B3 04. 10.1. The discussion which followed was parti- • cipated in by Messrs. Thomas J. Holt, . James Johnson, Thomas Clarke, 'Edward Key, John Jones, David Smith, Thomas Whyte, and Daniel Bisbrown. The laq mem- ber spoke at length on the coming temperance .demon stration on Bank holiday. 1k hoped it w!m1:1 be a rel- letter-dav in the history of Vie movement in Rencorn, and that the-members would redouble tlwir efforts to make it a success, both at the Sunday services; and the rrocession on Monday afternoon.—Additional particu- ' •la.r4 concerning the forthcoming demonstration will he found in our advertising c.ohimns.The anima, sermon will be delivered by the Rev. P. Pafford Williams,in the Green- way-road Chapel on Sunday afternoon next.. On the following day the demonstration will take plane, the Procession starting from the above-inentismed school at 1.30. Tea will aftlrward4 he provided and subse- quently a miscellaneous entertainment will be given, the programme being intersperged with 5p0r1.,‘,,,R bc W. Vaighttnan, P.M.W.P., of London ; Edward Halls- worth, M.W.A., Manchester; and C. H. Corrine, m.W.P., London, who will take the chair. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
