      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXA*MINEE, SATURDAY, AUGUST 4, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXA*MINEE, SATURDAY, AUGUST 4, 1894.</p>
        <p>TREAT TO PRE'iETTERIAN SCROLARS_AT RELICORS.</p>
        <p>- About 300 scholars, teacher 3, and friends of the John's Presbyterian School, Runcorn, left Runcorn on Monday afternoon for Overton Hills, there to partici- pate in their annual treat. The programme was a varied one, chiefly consisting of races and games of all descrip- tions, and a capital tea, which everyone enjoyed. Tlie return journey was completed at about half-past eight, after having spent an extremely pleasant day.</p>
        <p>OUTING OF SIIND&amp;Y SCHOOL CHILDREN. The annual outing given by the Rev. J. Simpson to the members of his children's class in connection with the Bethesda Congregation Church took place on Wednesday afternoon. The youthful scholars were conveyed to a field at Ashton, kindly lent for the occasion by Mr. J. Frodsham. Here they passed some time in playing at various games and running races for prizes. Subsc- quently they repaired to the Wesleyan School at Preston Brook, where they were regaled with an excellent tea. They returned home in good time in perfect safety.</p>
        <p>TETE MISSING ENGINEER DISCOVERED IN THE SHIP CANAL AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS COLLIERY DISPUTE SETTLED.</p>
        <p>The dispute at the Ashton's Green Colliery, St. Helens, where about 600 men had sent in notices, has been settled, and the notices, which would have expired on Monday morning, have been withdrawn.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>In the St. Helens district no trouble whatever is anti- cipated with the miners as regards . the 10 per cent. re- duction, which under the conciliation settlement, took effect on Wednesday. The whole of the miners were work- ing at the reduction for the first time on Wednesday, Tuesday being the makinz-up day throughout the district. At the different collieries separate scales of paying wages exist,and consequently the redaction will be taken off in different ways. It will, however, be taken off in the same way as advences have been put on, and will simply be the taking away of the last two 5 per cent. advances received by the men. At Messrs. Pilkington's pits the reduction will mean about 7 per cent. off the total wages, while at Ashton's Green,where a new scale of field prices has only been fixed this week, the 10 per cent, only amounts to id. per ton reduction. Mr. Thomas Glover, miners' agent for the district, and a member of the Conciliation Board, has during the past few days been holding meetings of the men at the several lodges which form the district,and has explained fully the terms of the settlement. The men in each case have unanimously decided to accept the reduction. LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE MINERS' FEDERATION. LOCAL DISPUTES.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation was held on Saturday, at the Miners' Hall, Wigan. The chief item of business was the discussion nf a special report sent out to all the members by the Lancashire representatives on the Board of Conciliation, in which they fully explain the reasons which actuated them in agreeing to the terms for a general settlement of the wages question. The Confer- ence, which was exceptionally well attended, commenced soon after ten o'clock, on Saturday morning, and the proceedings did not close till about half-paat four. Mr. S. Woods, M.P., the president, occupied the chair. The Conference first received reports from delegates with regard to colliery and district grie- vances. It was reported that at the Haydock col- lieries, disputes had arisen with regard to several local grievances, chiefly in connection with wages. A meeting had, however, been held between Mr. Woods, M.P., Mr. Aspinwall, Mr. Ashton, and Mr. Greenall, representing the Haydock district of the Miners' Federa- tion, together with a deputation of the men from the collieries affected, and Mr. Kellett, Mr. T. R. Ellis, Mr. Robinson, and Mr. James, representing the Haydeck Colliery Company. At this meeting all the grievances which had arisen at the Haydock collieries were fully discussed, and after a consultation extending over five hours. the Conference was adjourned for a fortnight so that Mr. Wocds, M.P., and Mr. Greenall might hold a meeting of the men and go into various points which were raised at the joint meeting of the coalowners and miners' representatives. The Haydock Colliery Company employ altogether about 4,000 men, so that it is of great importance that the dispute should be settled without a strike. It is expected that at the adjourned meeting to be held in a fortnight some amicable arrangements will be come to. About 200 men had given in notices which expire t3-day, but these will now be adjourned for a fortnight. Another matter brought befote the meeting was with reference to the Pcarson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company. At the collieries belonging to the above Company, a dispute arose some weeks ago, affect- ing sonic 2,060 men. An agreement was, however, arrived at between the employers and the men which would, it was then considered, put an end to the griev- ances complained of. It has since been reported that the employers are not carrying out their part of the agreement, and the men were therefore advised to send in fourteen days' notice to cease work. It was also re- ported that at Ashton's Green, St. Helens' where a dis- pute had arisen, a settlement was now likely, as an arrangement had been come to at all the mines except one. A meeting with the coalowners will be held to- day, when it is expected that a satisfactory arrange- ment will be made.</p>
        <p>The meeting then proceeded to consider the report touching the wages settlement. The President and Secretary made a statement of their reasons for accept- ing the wages reduction. The bulk of the delegates at the meeting expressed themselves in favour of the terms of settlement ; and those disrricrs which had been ad- verse to the agreement, were, after the explanations, unanimous in also deciding to remain loyal to the Federa- tion of Great Britain. After considerable discussion.the following resolutions was unanimously passed :—" That this Conference of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners Federation desires to express its entire confidence in our leaders on the Conciliation Board, and after the full ex- planation by the President and Secretary, we agree to recommend the acceptance of the settlement to the miners in Lancashire and Cheshire." Toe remaining business of the Conference was the election of officials for the ensuing half year. Mr. S. Woods, M.P., was re- elected president, Mr. Thomas Ashton v,ecretary, and and Mr. R. Isherwood J.P., tre 'surer ; Messrs. F. Malli- son, J. Pearson, Edwin Rickman, were elected members of the Committee, and Mr. G. Worthington auditor of the Federation.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>WHISTON COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The polling in the Whiston Division for the election of a county councillor resulted on Tuesday in the return of Mr. Wm. Gamble by a majority of 1,030 over Mr. Wm. Ciibbings, who polled only 217 votes. ST. HELENS ENGINEERS.</p>
        <p>Intimation has been received in St. Helens that Second Lieutenants Bertram Brewis, and Watkin Oppenheim have passed the examination, and have been granted certificates of proficiency. Both men are connected with the 2nd Lancashire Engineers (St. Helens), and the examination was held at St. Helens on the 14th, the board consisting of Colonel Slazke,C.R.E., Major Shute, R.E., Crewe ; Captain Littledale,R.E., Birkenhead ; and Captain Turton, R.E., St. Helens. - TRAP Accinerr. -</p>
        <p>On Sunday, Mr. George Nixon, of the Run- ning Horses Hot;11, St. Helens, with his niece, Miss Sissie Jenkins, were driving near Eccleston-lane Ends, when the pony shied. Mr. Thomas Milne, of the Nag's Head Hotel, and Mrs. Milne, were following in another trap, when both vehicles got locked. The occupants of Nixon's trap were thrown nut; Mr. Nixon sustained severe injuries to his head, Miss Jenkins was badly bruised, and the trap was smashed. The pony bolted but was caught half a mile away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MEETING OF THE RUNCORN GAS COMPANY.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF THE RUNCORN GAS COMPANY.</p>
        <p>On Tl.;;;daY morning the ordinary half-yearly meet- ing of this company was held in the offices, Mr. Fred Rigby presiding. There were also present Major Hazle- burst, Messrs. W. Handley, T. Handley, J. Pritchard, W. Whittaker, G. Frith, J. Handley and J. R. Frith, secretary. The recommendation of the directors to pay a dividend of 10 per cent.for the half year was accepted. It was stated that the new works werestarted on the 6:h April, and the working was very satisfactory. Mr. W. Whittaker, the retiring director, and Mr. W. Handley, retiring auditor, were both re-elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>– THE PROPOSED GR&amp;VING DOCK AT .RIINCORN.</head>
        <p>- THE PROPOSED GR&amp;VING DOCK AT .RIINCORN.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee of the Runcorn Im- provement Commissioners, who have this question in hand met in the Town Hall on Wednesday evening, Mr. Pritchard occupying the chair. A letter from Messrs. Grundy and Co., solicitors, on behalf of the Ship Canal Company was read, stating that they had no funds for the construction of the graving dock, or for the carrying out of any alternative schetne, for the provision of in- creased facilities for dealing with traffic. The committee expressed themselves as disappointed with this informa- tion, and finally it was resolved to instruct Mr. Lake, law clerk, to reply saying that the Commissioners intended to hold the Canal Company to their agreement. DEPARTURE OF THE RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS FOR BLACKPOOL.</p>
        <p>The following are extracts from the detachment orders En the week ending August 4th,referring to the F and G Companies of the 2nd V.B.C.R. stationed at Runcorn: —2. The detachment will parade in complete marching order, viz., helmets, kerseys, leggings, valises, great coats, mess tins, haversacks, and water bottles, at 3.45 p.m. on Saturday August 4th, for the purpose of pro- ceeding to camp. Officers and staff sergeants will carry theirgreat coats rolled and slung over the right shoulder. 3. Volunteers going into camp on Monday and Tuesday will procede in complete marching order. 4. It must be clearly understood that volunteers will not be allowed to attend camp after TueEd ay, August 7th, (except for the inspection). 5. The inspection of the Battalion will take plaee in camp on Friday August 10th. Volunteers attending for the inspection only will do so in complete marching order (without valises), and must leave Runcorn by the first morning train. Those who cannot attend must obtain leave in writing, in compliance with the standing orders of the battalion.</p>
        <p>BOWLING MATCH. About 40 members of the bowling club held at the Bowling Green inn, Birkhall-lane, Westleigh, were driven in waggonettes to the Horse Shoe Inn, Croft, on Saturday. On their arrival they adjourned to the bowling green and contested for three money prizes, J. Wilman gaining the first prize, S. Ripley the second, and John Buckley the third. At five o'clock they partook of tea, and spent the remainder of the evening in a convivial manner. Mr. J. H. Hayes, president, occupied the chair. GENEROUS GIFT BY Mr. W. H. LEVER.</p>
        <p>An intimation has been received from Mr. W. H. Lever, of Thornhill Hall, Bebington, that he will subscribe £5OO to the National Society for the Pre- vention of Cruelty to Children. This is owing to the good and valuable work being accomplished by that institution throughout the country, and especially in Warrington, Widnes, and neighbour- hood, where of late several prosecutions of negli- ent parents have taken place. SEWING PLizsiNks Han I£2 18.6 d. Treadle £4 4s.witt• handsome wtlnut cever.—J and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, Sankey-ot., Warrington.—Advt. COUNTY COUNCIL SCHOLARSHIPS. . _</p>
        <p>The Technical Instruction Committee ot the Lan- cashire County Council have decided to recommend the awarding of the following, among a host of other scholarships : Science exhibition of £lO each to C. J. Barkla, Ditton, Widnes; T. A. Bottomley, Widnes; T. B. Franklin, Widnes; W. H. Nickson, Widnes; and E. Tebbutt, Ditton, Widnes. An art exhibition of £lO to 11. Pierpoint, Warrington. A commercial exhibition of £lO to J. P. Hughes, Ap- pleton, Widnes; and J. Williams, Widnes. A exhi- bition of £lO for proficiency in hygiene to T. Wright Prescot. The scholarships and exhibitions repre- sent a total grant of £7,030. WILL OF THE LATE COLONEL J. IRELAND BLACKBURNE.</p>
        <p>Probate of the will (dated February 11, 1891) of Colonel John Ireland Blackburne. of Hale Hall, Liverpool, 'LP., M.P. for South-west Lancashire from 1875 to 1885, who died on the sth September, 1893, aged 76 years, has been granted to his son, Robert Ireland Blackburne, of Hale Hall, and his son-in- law, Robert Crawford Antrobus, of 27, Eccleston- square, loy whom the gross value of the testator's personal estate has been sworn at £106,235 Os. 21., and the net value at nil. Having appointed in favour of his daughters Dorothea Ann Bright,E mily Antrobus, and Madame Margaret Everilda Edwards- Moss, settled charges of .£lO,OOO and .£4,000, and a settled trust fund of £7,000 in consols, the testator charges his estate with a further £8,539 16s. 9d. in favour of his said daughters in order to make up their portions to £lO,OOO each. He be- queaths the plate presented to his grandfather by the county, the jewellery which belonged to his grandmother, and the furniture and effects at Hale Hall, to devolve as heirlooms therewith, and be leaves all the residue of his property to his son, Robert Ireland Blackburne. SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 188. 6d. Treadle £4 41. with handsome walnut oover.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 13, Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt. MAN FOUND DROWNED IN THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The body of a man was seen floating in the Ship Canal near the swing bridge, Latchford, by some workman on Saturday. The body was pulled out and was found to be that of a rather young man, and appeared to have been in the water for some time. Letters and papers were found upon him about a fortnight old. Mr. Worsley conducted an inquiry at the Red Lion on Monday morning, touching the death of the deceased, who had been identified as William Richardson, 40 years of age, who lived in Charles-street, Warrington. Cather- ine Richardson, wife of the deceased, said that she last saw him alive on the 21st. July, when he left home to go to Dallam Forge for his wages. He had bcen low-spirited for some time, but that caused her no alarm. Joseph Worsley, Turner-street, said that he saw the deceased on Bank Quay Station on the 21st of July. Deceased said he was going to Liverpool. Robert Stephens said he saw the body floating in the canal at Latchford on Saturday. He obtained a rope and brought the body to the bank. P.C. Brew having stated that there were no marks of violence on the body, the jury returned an open verdict of "Found drowned. WARRINGTON CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL UNION QUARTERLY MEETING. There was a large number present at the quar- terly meeting of the Warrington Sunday School Association which was held at Padgate on Saturday. Tea was served in the schoolroom, o which about 150 sat down. A service was held in the church at ! 7.30, conducted by the Rev. J. J. Langham, vicar of Padgate, the pulpit being occupied by the Rev. G. 11. Spotoner, rector of Woolton, who took for his text the words, " And I .saw another mighty angel come down from heaven, clothed with a cloud ; and a rainbow was upon his head, and his face was as it were the sun, and his feet as pillars of fire." He said he considered it a privilege to be permitted to deliver alew Words of encouragement and help to those presnt. He had chosen the passage partly because the book from which it was taken was so deeply interestimg, and more eSpßeially because the verse itself seemed to him to be so eminently sug- gestive of the thoughts intimately connected with their work in the Master's name among the young. In order to take Cbrist'a message to others they must be intimately acquainted with. •.Him themselves. What be wanted to impress upon them was the dignity of their' office. There was no work which had so fully the approval of their master Christ as that wonk. It might be humble and unostentatious. but it was a 1-ery noble and eXcellent work. His. believed that there were many Sunday school teachers who, if hey went through an exemination, might not take a high place ietelleet wally, but who Were doing a mighty ! work for We Wister. If they felt that the work ! had come direct. from God it would malce it diff,r- ent than it otherwise would he. There eught to he a seriousness about the way in which they carried out their work. He somekimes noticed a c-rtaio amount of frivolity in the demeanour of some Sun- day school teachers when they entered the school. !At the. seine in they need not -carry a sail face to their scholars, hut rather let the children realise that the principles which they brought to them had made them themselves happy awl hriet Christians.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>FOUND AFTER THE EXPIRATION OF SEVEN MONTHS.</head>
        <p>FOUND AFTER THE EXPIRATION OF SEVEN MONTHS.</p>
        <p>Whilst the dredger " Barry '' was engaged in its operations near the Lay-By, Runcorn, at about 10.40 on Tuesday night, the body of a man was brought up in one of the buckets. Thomas Evans, of Pen- nington's Place, Runcorn, who was aboard the dredger, was the first to observe the body, and at once gave information of the discovery to the police authori- ties. The clothing found upon it consisted of a short blue flannel jacket, black cloth vest and trousers, drawers and Blucher boots. The body is supposed to be that of Samuel Tilsley, chief engineer of the steam ship " Albertina," who, during his life lived with his wife at Cryes-road, Cardiff. It will probably be remembered by some persons that the man named above came ashore from the " Albertina " in the company of two other sea- men named William Pearson and Augustus Guise, on the 9th January last and visited the town. They afterwards returned to their vessel, and Tilsley whilst endea- vouring to board the craft slipped from the ladder into the water,and was lost to sight owing to the intense darkness of the night. Since this unfortunate occurrence efforts have daily been made by Mr. Win. Shaw, the local missionary at the Seamen's. Institute, and Mr. By- field, to recover the body by means of grappling irons, but without success. Considerable interest in these operations has been evinced by the missing man's em- ployers, Messrs, Fisher, Renwick, and Co., shipowners, of London and Manchester. The body which was in an advanced stage of decomposition was taken to the mor- tuary where an inquest Will probably be held relating to it to-day (Friday). This makes the fourth marina engineer who has been -recovered from the Ship Canal since its first opening in January.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>"MY GREYT AMBITION ! "</head>
        <p>"MY GREYT AMBITION ! "</p>
        <p>Wen aw wur a lad, my ambition Wur allus to be up at th' top; An' neaw, in a owder condition, Aw connot this same feelin' stop. Aw'm brunnin' to be a Commissioner, An' appinted fer Cheermon ez weel ; Wen yo'd see heaw a fause owd Practitioner Cud make sum o'th' ratepayers squeal ! Aw shud want, fer mi job, most o'th others, To do wot aw said, beaut demur; An' to wark my sweet will like true brothers, An' ne'er need a whip er a spur! Ehl then wot grand things we'd be seein ' ! Me skamin' eaut fads bi the score, Wile yo—not one sewl disagreelif— Wad rake up the shekkels galore! Aw'd hey a Zoologikin Garden Per monkeys—that's summat that pays ; Aw cud go then an' smook mi' 'Churchwarden," An' study mi ancestors' ways! Aw wud build a magnificent fountain, In front ov eaur wonuerful Hall ; An' ez hee ez a Switzerland mountain, To squirt o'er the opposite wall! Aw shud think, too, a Stachoo i' marble Wud noan look so bad o' mysel' ; An' th' childer cud daunce reaund, an' warble Sum tunes on a rate-raisin' swell ! Aw wud raise a six-storied Theayter, An' shud never consider the cost ; If one grumbled, awd just stop.his wayter, An' smile wile tt e chap :did his wost ! Aw wud go theer an' practice mi speeches, Tho' sum foak met think it look'd strange; An' fer theme a surveyor's new breeches Wud do very weel fer a change! In short aw wud work moor then gladly, To double at least every rate ; Till the ratepayers, lookin' quite sadly, Wud aw run away fro ther fate! Thoose eautlyin wards met be axin' Wheer aw ther good money hed gone; But the answer is plain—we'd bin taxin' To benefit but" Number One ! " Aw shad allus believe aw'r promoted To keep that one object i' view; An' ez lung ez aw' wern't ovvervoted, Aw' shud not go) an' act like a foo ! • Thay met pray that aw'd dee er retire, Er met threaten to smash mi owd nob; But aw'd see aw the lot i -- fire, Befoor aw wud chuck up mi job! Yore's titles, 717.\-1 0' PEGS Rose Bonk, Aiigust 2, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>THE DAY SCHOOL.—It 13 extremely pleasant to record that Her Majesty's Inspector of Schools reports that— "the school does much excellent work." This news must be very satisfactory to the managers and warm congratulations are certainly due to Mr. Wade, Miss Marlow,and the remainder of the staff. The children are also worthy of praise, not one of them having failed. The school will re-open on the 20th August. Tim BAZAAR FUND.—With a view to augmenting the fund for the forthcoming bazaar, in aid of the proposed new church, the following ladies have given teas in their houses :—Mrs. Bankes, Mrs. Sill, Mrs. Kenwright, Mrs. Robinson, Mrs. Goddard, and the Rev. W. H. Stables. Sewing meeting teas will in future be given every week, Mrs. Mather, Mrs. Guest, Mrs. Pendlebury, Mrs. T. Ford having already promised to entertain the workers.</p>
        <p>CHORISTERS EXCURSION TO LLANDUDNO —The juvenile members of the choir enjoyed the luxury of a saloon to Uandudno on Friday, July 27th. They assembled on the green at Weston about seven o'clock, and walked down to the station followed by a hamper which was emptied of its contents on the outward journey—early breakfasts create a ten o'clock appetite. Llandudno was reached at 10.27, and after a very light meal the boys enjoyed themselves in boating, donkey riding, &amp;c. The niggers were visited in the "Happy Valley " ; the Great Orme was climbed with great fun; and the boys tried their vocal powers by singing a hymn in the old church. After running along the pier a very substantial meal was served at the" Promenade Hotel. An excursion was then made to Conway, where the fine old castle was visited and after various other pastimes were ifidulged in the homeward journey was begun and completed in perfect safety, all very pleased with the day's outing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LOCAL VETO ACT IN NEW ZEALAND.</head>
        <p>THE LOCAL VETO ACT IN NEW ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>A few wceks ago we gave an account of the working of the Local Veto Act, which was passed by the New Zealand Legislature last March. In that report we gave the results of the elections of the licensing magistrates in the different electoral districts, which were co-equal with those for the Colonial Parliament, the electors hav- ing the choice of magistrates who would deal with the licenses according to the wishes of the inhabitants. In some districts magistrates were elected who would adhere to things as they were ; in others, the electors went for a reduction of 25 per cent. of licensed liouses ; while in other localities magistrates were selected who would refuse all renewals, and so put the closure on the sale of all alcoholic drink in those districts.</p>
        <p>Be the mail just to hand we are able to give our readers an account of the first sittings of the veto-elected licensing benches in that far-off colony, which may not only be of interest to our teet;tal,but also to our licensed victualler, readers; and it may be a guide as to the working of such an Act if it ever should come into force in this country. In the city of Dunedin, forty-five pub- licans have obtained renewals of their licenses, but the hour for closing has been fixed at 10 p.m., instead of 11 p.m. as previously. In forty-three other cases the deci- sion of the bench had ben reserved, and the applicants are to be given an opportunity of showing cause why their , licences should not be included in the 25 per cent. re- duction of licensed houses. In the Chalmers district, one licence had been refused ansolutely, and the apPli- cations of four other hotel proprietors had, when the tri.t,il left, been adjourned ' • but of those four two must perforce be refused. Theeaversham r Bench have three hotel keepers in suspense, and of these three two must be forfeited. lii the Clutha district, .where the electors .voted for entiie prohibition, much excitemeAt prevails; because, while the votes went, for entire closing of all public-houses-. and bottling businesses, the stipendiary ma,istrate, as chairman of the licensing bench, and by virtue of the powers vested in him granted three whole- sale licences. In the city of Wellington/ three houses have been closed,while in the city of Christchurch twelve hotels have been shut, and 10 p.m. fixed ,as the hourfor the closing of those whoss licences have been renewed.. r In Invercargill, where the Bench were bound to restrict the licensed, houses by 25 per ceoe., this licensing tnagi- ' trAtes selected the names of the doomed seven in num- ber by ballot. . . • there is no " compensatinn for. dieochance " so in !he case where 110,3SV‘: have lin shot up the tenahts have simply to close up and hear their losses in a heroic 'fashion. . . . _ _ _</p>
        <p>In addition to the working of tit. Act,. the Peehibition League in Duna:lin had a meeting.atter the li‘lences -had been dea,th Lk by th,• e'ected committee when it was advocated that. AS the Ltague had succeeded so far. there was fear of anl thy. They had had only the tint round in the great ft ht between society and vested ini• cinitics, and it would ,be dizastrona ;e they famie:l that the foe was dead. They advocated, "Register, register." To sum up the whole is to say that New Zealand means to go in for more than the well-known " Main Liquor •</p>
        <p>.P1.11D:3, NI :11 illf4 AND FISH STUFFED, and 11 mounted in Glass Cases of any desic,rn. HEADS anl. HORNS mounted .10 for Sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or kire Screens. Dealer in British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins, Glass Shades, Stags' Heads. Cabinets, Store Boxes, Nets, &amp;c., for Butterfly and Egg Collectors. —B. Cooxr, Naturalist and Furrier, 21, Renshaw-street, LI. —1,•.01</p>
        <p>FORS JACKETS AND FURS SFALSKIN of every dese ipi ion CLEANED- or RE-DYED and ALTERED. FUR SKINS dressed and made up into rugs, COOKE, Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper Newing- ton, and 21, Rensiviw-str'At, Liverpool. Established 20 2 carp,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Fr, HELENS TOWN cOUNCTL,</head>
        <p>Fr, HELENS TOWN cOUNCTL,</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the St. lielens Towil Council was held on Wednesday, the mayor (Alder- man A. Sinclair) presiding. A letter was read from Mr. J. Dearden, secretary of the St. Helens Trades Council, asking the corporation to give all the work at their disposal to firms who conformed to trade union terms and paid trade union wages. A letter was also read from the St. Helens and District Cottage Property Owners' Association, protesting against corpor- ate officials being paid El Us. 6d. per day and at the same time receiving their respective salaries when away on corporation business—The Mayor remarked that attention could be paid to the communications when thought necessary hereafter. The health committee recommended that the Rabies Order be enforced in the borough as in the surrounding county districts, and that the owners of all unmuzzled dogs be summoned.—Councillor Mearns. on behalf of the St. Helens Canine Society, protested against the adop- tion of the recommendation, and said there was abso- lutely no necessity for the enforcement of the order in St. Helens.—Alderman Gamble, C. 8., said the watch committee had received a telegram from the chief con- stable of the county expressing a hope that the order would be put in force in St. Helens in order that the county might be freed from rabies. There had been 26 cases of rabies in the county during the last twelve months.—Several members considered that dogs under the proper control of their owners should be exempted ' from the order, and an amendment to that effect was moved by Councillor Brook. Sixteen voted fsr the amendment, and a similar number against. The mayor gave his casting vote in favour of the recommendation of the committee, and the minutes were declared passed. When the minutes of the parliamentary committee came forward for confirmation, Alderman Pilkington said the committee had been in communication with the London and North-Western Railway Company in refer- ence to the unsatisfactory passenger train servine to and from St. Helens. An intimation had been received from the company that instructions had been given to the stationmaster at St. Helens that whenever a passenger train from the North was ten minutes late a special relief train had to be made up and despatched frpm St. Helens to Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PENKETII.</head>
        <p>PENKETII.</p>
        <p>SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD.—The monthly meeting of the Sankey School Board was held in the schoolroom,S,Ankey on Tuesday afternoon. There were present : Major Ridgway (chairtnan),Mr. W. P. Skelton (rice--thairman), the Rev. E. S. Jackson, and Mr. Basil Shearer (school attendance officer),representiu,g the clerk,Mr. S. Sutton. Cheques were passed. for payments amounting to £53. The schoolmaster (Mr. George Clarke) reported that the average attendance during the t Ns° weeks, since the holidays, had been 280. There was no other business of interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SUTTON WEAVER.</head>
        <p>SUTTON WEAVER.</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED BURGLAR CAUGHT WHILST Doz Whilst P. Sergeant Benfield, of. Sutton Weaver, was passing along Chester-road in that locality on Monday afternoon he espied a 3 ming man lying asleep by the roadside. After eyeing him over the appearance Of this victim of Morpheus aroused the suspicion of the " cute" representative of the law who forthwith -proceeded to rudely disturb the slumberer. The la,.ter,w !tan thoroughly awakened, was submitted to a rigid examination, the result of which was the finding on his person of a bunch of fourteen keys, a railway ,signalman's guide book, a clasp knife with several blades, a razor, • a new Vest., and a, partly worn railway servant's coat. Wing in- terrogated by thc. ofii...er he stated that he had broken 'into the cabin of a railway man, a shirt: dis!ance from Welshpool. Ho was thereupon taken to Runcorn and lodged in the police cell on suspicion. Enquiries were instituted, the outcome of which was the receipt of . tolegrarn. by the Runcorn Police staling.. that a man, ' whose appearance was identical with that of the prisoner rThomas Edwards, was " wante.i." by the Welshpool police for having broken into a house in that town on the ' previous Friday night from which the articles found in his possession had been stolen. On Tuesday afternoon, a constable from Wolshpoal arrived in Runcorn and took ehargo of Edwards whom he conveyed back to Welslipool on a charg.e of house breaking.</p>
      </div>
