      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DOGS OR PUPS.</head>
        <p>DOGS OR PUPS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday, before Mr. W. Tyrer, Alderman M'Kechnie, and Mr. Dromgoole, Robert Brogden, a lodging-house keeper, of 2, Tontine-street, was summoned for allowing two dogs to be at large unmuzzled and having no collar on. Defendant said the dogs were not his, but, having admitted they were in his possession, the clerk told him he was liable. He then said they, were puppies, only eight weeks old, and were in charge of his housekeeper. Eventually the house- keeper claimed the dogs or puppies as her own. Defend- ant was fined is. and costs in respect of each dog.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ABSTRACTING CREAM FROM MILK.</head>
        <p>ABSTRACTING CREAM FROM MILK.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Monday,before Mr. Tyrer (chairman), Alderman M'Kechnie, and Dr. Dromgoole, Mary Lally, milk dealer, 29, Tontine-street, was summoned for sell- ing to donstable James Hughes a pint of milk, which the public analyst (Dr. Robertson) certified to have had 8 per cent, of fat (cream) abstracted.—Defendant said she told the con- stable it was warm, having just come from the shippon, and contended that she sold the milk as she received it from the cowkeeper.—ln reply to the magistrates,Police Constable Hughes said he took six samples from different dealers that morning, and paid the standard price for each of them. The magistrates said it was clear that the milk was very deficient in cream, and as defendant sold it she was rePponsible. If it was true she sold it as she received it, the cowkeeper ought to recompense her. Under the circumstances the small fine of 2s. 6d. and costs would be imposed. T. HELENS AND ITS RAILWAY CONNECTIONS. _</p>
        <p>_ The Parliamentary Committee of the St. Helens Cor- poration have again had under consideration the un- satisfactory service of passenger trains to and from the borough, and the Town Clerk has been instructed to communicate with the London and North-Western Rail- way Company upon the subject,.requesting their atten- tion to (1) the long time which passengers from St. Helens frequently have to wait at Warrington, and the delays in the journey between the two towns and between St. Helens and London, and the consequent unfairness of the extra charge for the latter journey via Edgehill ; (2) the need of a train to Liverpool, via Pres- cot, about 9.50 a.m. and of a train from Liverpool, via Prescot, at 8.30 p.m. and the inconvenience caused by the cessation of the running of the relief train to Liver- pool on Saturdays at about 1..15 p.m.; and (3) the ex- ceedingly inconvenient and unsatisfactory train service between St. Helens and Southport, and the need of alteration in the 2.42 p.m. train so as to make a conne3- tion with the 2.38 p.m. train to Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WATER POLO MATCH.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, the Warrington first and reserve teams journed to St. Helens, where two matches were play&amp;l at the baths in Boundary-road There wal a crowed attendance, and the good form diAplayed by Warrington in the few league matches in which they were allowed to take part., gave one the opinion that a good game was in store.</p>
        <p>In the matches between the reserve teams, Warrington had choice of the shallow end, and although Russell gained possession at the start, fouls against the visitors led up to D. Sharrock notching a goal, the result of a clinking shrst. fhe Saints claimed the upper hand on restarting, but Walsh proved an excellent custodian, fisting shots out in smart style, and in turn Warrington took up the aftack, and after Clough had had a point disallowed success attended their second venture, Crigh- ton equalizing after a general males in close quarters. The Warringtonians showed smart form, but in point of , combination they compared poorly with St. Helens, and but for the accurate play of Walsh, the Warrington goal must have fallen many times. At half-time the scores were equal, but during the second half the Saints were almost cmtinually attacking, and showing smart form throughout ultimately won by eight goals to one. After the reserve match, the first teams met and were loudly cheered on entering the water. The teams were as foii:.:Tl: —St. Helens : Goal, J. Aspinall ; full backs, S. Sherwooff, .;, Mirtlew ; half back, D. W. Holland ; forwards, J. mcgtf, j, Sharrocks, A. Maraden (cap- tain). Warrington: Goal, T. rDixnn : full backs, W. D. Sutton, S. Beaman ; half backs. Fair. Turner (cap- tain) ; forwards, G. A. Scotson T. Locker, A. Jones. Referee, A. Thompson, Liverpool. St. Helens had choke of the shallow end, and Sharrock early gaining posses- sion tracks were speedily made for Dixon's charge. A capital save sent the invaders back. and Aspinall was te.,ted with futile result. Fouls were given against each side, and then came a neat piece of passing by Holland and Shm-rock, the latter shooting a capital goal. Dixon was again troubled on the re-start, but Locker and Scot- son dribbled well, only to be beaten back by Sherwood and Martlew. and Holland giving Sharrock another chance this placer availed himself of the opportunity by addinc, a second. Warrington next had a chance through the good play of Turner and Locker, but failed at close quarters, and the Saints taking up the attack again a third goal was registered, quickly followed by one from Marsden. Dribbling by Locker and passing by Sutton, Turner, and Jones raised the hopes of the visitors, but the defence was good, and St. Helens again showing capital combination Sharrock put on a fifth just before the interval, the locals thus leading by five goals to none. The Warringtonians showed better form in the opening of the second half, and after Dixon had saved well Turner initiated a bout of passing, which Locker finally turned to good account, Aspinall having little chance with the shot. However, a I,,ng shot by Sharrock again lowered the Warrington goal, but Warrington per- severed, and sear the close Sutton adding cleverly to the score, the final issue read —St. Helens, eight goats; War- rington, two goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BANQUET TO SIR WILLIAM HARCOURT.</head>
        <p>BANQUET TO SIR WILLIAM HARCOURT.</p>
        <p>Sir William Harcourt was entertained at a ban- quet on Wednesday night in London by many Liberal members of Parliament in recognition of his success in passing the Budget Bill through the House. Mr. Jacob Bright, M.P., presided, and in proposing the hmlzh of the Chancellor of the Exchequer spoke sd his tact and skill as a statesman, and of the great merit of his Budget, which showed sympathy with the struggling poor. Sir W. Harcourt, in responding, said with regard to the rumours as to his probable re- tirement that whatever remained to him of the future belonged to the Literal party. He was to old to secede, and he hoped to continue a Liberal without having any adjective added to his name. He expressed his grati- tude for the hearty support he had obtained in piloting the Budget Bill. No measure passed into law had been less chanced in its leading principles,notwithstanding the remarkable opposition. He referred to the utterances of the Marquis of Salisbury and the Duke of Devonshire on the horrors of graduation, and said, amid laughter that the land was not to pay because it was too poor and the brewers were not to pay because they were too rich. Now, however, the fight was over, and the bill had . received the " blessing" of the House of Lords. He failed to see why the will of the people should not be final and supreme on other questions besides that of finance. They had a still -Teat and important chapter of their Multifarious prpgramme to finish before they asked the judgment of the nation.. There was no reason why in the time which lay before them they should not do as much and more than they hid alrendr amrn- plished. They had no reascm to be ashatuad of the work thpr had dnne. and tep hy step and bill by bill they intended to aohieve the rest of their %Cork.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Ylfm,</head>
        <p>Ylfm,</p>
        <p>RE-CPENINO OF THZ PARTSI.I P IS.II Church wa4 re•oncoe‘i on Sunday, tylv,u special services were conducted. Holy Coannunion was cmehratm at Og,hto'clock in the morning., and at the half-past ten service thc pulnit was occupied by tlw. -Rector, the Rev. G.W. Guest, M.A. The preacher in the evening was the Rev. J. S. Collins, R.A., curate, The anthem was "The Wilderness," Sir J. Goss. There wAs a large con- gregation at each service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
