      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>FOUND DROWNED IN THE BRIDGE. WATER CANAL AT RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SUPPOSED SUICIDE OF A LIVERPOOL</head>
        <p>SUPPOSED SUICIDE OF A LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>WOMAN.</p>
        <p>James Lloyd, fitter, of 18, Norton-st., whilst walk- ing alcing the bank of the Bridgewater Canal, oppo- site the Soapery, Runcorn, on Monday morning last at about half-past ten observed the body of a woman in the water. He pulled the body to the side of the canal with the assistance of a young man named Thomas Goodier, and then communicated with the police, who, npon arriving, identified it as that of Arm Moran, 33 years of age, of Liverpool. The body was then conveyed on an ambulance to the mortuary. The deceased, who was known as an unfortunate, had been seen alive and intoxicated late on the previous Saturday night. A fortnight earlier to that date she had been found in the Ship Canal at Big Pool by P.S. Lee, who suddeeded in rescuing her from the water. Subse- quently, on the same day, she was taken into custody on a charge of drunkenness and sent to prison on the follow- ing Monday morning at the Petty Sessions f.pr seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>TUE INVTEST.</head>
        <p>TUE INVTEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Worsley, coronet:, investigated the circum- stances attending the death of the unfortunate woman at the Cemetery Offices, Greenway-road, on Tuesday afternoon.—Thomas Murphy, Mersey-street, deposed to the deceased having lodged at his halm for about a week. Owing to her having stayed out two or three nights he forbade her the house. He saw her come out of Knutsford gaol on the previous Saturday morning where she had been for seven days for being drunk. Witness was also coming out of gaol at the time he hav- ing been incarcerated for a similar period for a like offence. He saw her again that day when she called at his house in Mersey-street and expressed a wish to lodge there but be refused her and she was ordered away from the house. At that time she was evidently under the influence of drink.—ln answer to a juryman the witness stated that he only knew the deceased by the name of Ann Mitchell, which was the name she had always given him.—George Sant, who stated that he lodged at the Blue Ball, Fryer-street, Runcorn, and was by occupation a carter, said he had known the deceased three weeks. He was ignorant of her name, however. The last time he saw her alive was six o'clock on Satur- day night when she and himself had a glass of beer toge- ther in the Boathouse hotel. Having drank his beer, witness went out of the public-house and left her there. She was not quite sober at the time of his leaving her neither was he himself. He knew the husband of Cath- erine Bowden, but did not know her. After leaving the deceased he went to his lodgings, had his tea and came out again. He spent the evening with several persons, and at various places, buthe could not remember every- thing. At ten o'clock he was in the" Glass Barrel,"and left at eleven o'clock. When he went to the" Blue Ball "it was locked up and he was forced to sleep in a stable belonging to Mrs. Fieldes, in Irwell-lane, from whom he obtained permission. fie was convinced that he did not see the deceased after six o'clock that night. —James Lloyd testified to the discovery of the body, after which P.C. Richardson deposed to having examined the body but found no marks of violence upon it. There were a few cuts on the face but they had apparently been caused after death by a passing boat. He saw the deceased several times on the Saturday, always alone, and the last time was 10.20 p.m., when she was siting on some steps in Mersey-st. At that time she was very drunk. The body was found some 500 yards distant from these steps. Witness continued that the statement of George Sant to the Coroner was not identical with the one he had made to him (witness), being in some instances contradictory. He had been told by Catherine Bowden that Sant was seen with the deceased late on Saturday night, but although he (witness) had told Bowden to appear as a witness at the inquiry, she had not yet appeared,and was not to be found probably'that day.—George Tufft, barman at the Commercial Hotel (" Glass Barrel"), said he knew the man Sant,hut could. not remember seeing him in the house on Saturday night at eleven o'clock. Sant came in at about five o'clock on Saturday evening, but witness could not say at what time he went out. Ann Moran was in the hotel on Saturday morning, but not in the evening.—The Coroner thought the evidence they possessed was not satisfactory, and several of the jurymen were of the Isame opinion. Consequently, it was decided to adjourn the inquiry until Friday afternoon, the police being or- dered, in the meantime, to make further inquiries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>HAYbOCK.</head>
        <p>HAYbOCK.</p>
        <p>EXCURSION TO BLACKPOOL.—The annual excursion of the employes of Messrs. R. Evans and Co., of Haydock, took place on Saturday to Blackpool. Soon after five o'clock in the morning large numbers of people were astir, wending their way in the direction of Messrs. Evans's sidings, in Haydock, where, the empty coaches were drawn up. By about seven o'clock. between 3,000 and 4,000 people had left Haydock in four long trains, which were handed over by the firm's own engines to the L. and N.-W. Co. at Earlestown. Blackpool was reached at various times, and the day being fine, the trippers were enabled to enjoy themselves to the fullest extent, Of course the majority swarmed to the beach, and indulged in the annual dip. Others visited the gar- dens, pier, tower, and other places of interest. Two trains left for home about seven o'clock, but the remain- der did not leave until about ten o'clock, reaching Hay- dock about midnight. _</p>
        <p>HAYDOCI COUNTY COURT DISTRTCT.—The follewiog ter was on Wednesday morning received by Mr. J. W. Green, solicitor, of St. Helens, from the Hon. T. W. Legh,M.P., regarding the agitation for a transfer of the Havdock County Court district from Warrington to St. Helens :—" House of Commons, S. W. July 31, 1894. Sir?—I had an interview some days ago with the Lord Chanoelion cretary with reference to the pro. posed transfer of flaydock io the St. Helens County Court district, and pointed out to the best of my ability the desirability of the change, more especially as affect- ing the convenience of the Haydock residents. While declining to pledge myself to comply with the request of the Haydock Local Bnard,the Lord Chancellor's secre- tary stated that he would cause further inquiries to be made into the matter. Lord Cross who is interested in County Court jurisdiction, and who is also acquainted with the district, has at my request also placed the facts fully before this gentleman.-1 am, sir, yours truly, T. W. Legh." _ _</p>
        <p>Form) DROWNlll).—James Lawrensoti, 28 years of age, a collier. of Jucifield-street, Haydock, was found drowned on Saturday. Some children playing near the Slitting Mill Dans at Blackbrook saw a coat and hat on the grass, They reported their discovery, and the water was dragged, with the result that deceased's body was found. Lawrenson was last seen alive on Friday night. On Tuesday Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner. held an inquest at the Huntsman Inn, Haydock,near St. Helens, on the body.—Joseph Lawrenson, brother, identified the body, and said he did not know anything of his brother's trouble about a girl. The deceased left home on Friday night, presumably to go for a walk.—Jonathan Lester, of Havdock, deposed that it came to his knowledge on Mondic week that the deceased had assaulted his (wit- ness's) daughter. He saw the deceased on Thursday. and told bin, that if he would come to the house and apologize on the Friday he would not put the matter in the hands of the police. The deceased did not come to the house, however.—John Mason, collier, said he met the deceased in Chain-lane about half-past one on Satur- day morning, and on speaking to him deceased said he had been for a walk round the town. He did not notice anything strange about Lawrenson, except that he was slightly in drink.—The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>STRANGE WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>STRANGE WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>A peculiar marriage,and one not without its droll side took place not long ago at the Registrar's Office, Bristol An immense crowd of spectators were attracted thereto. The bride, a cpinster, fair and forty, pushed into the office of the astonished registrar a family perambulator, in which was the bridegroom, a man of forty-five, so *rippled in both legs, that from birth he had been unable to walk. He was permitted to remain in the perambula- tor during the customary declaration of the civil contract, and when the bride again appeared, pushing the perambulator containing her husband, the crowd cheered lustily, wished them joy, and showered them with rice.</p>
        <p>Another very odd wedding was, some time back, solemnized in New Jersey, U.S.A. The bride was Mii,'S Violet Mascotte,of the Violet Mascotte British Burlesque Company, and the bridegroom, Mr. Wilfred Lasemore, the manager of the company. The interesting ceremony took place on the stage of the Grand Opera House, Newark, in the State named. lc is this circumstance , which lends a 'Wore than usual interest to the matter ; for, although playgoers are familiar with the orthodox stage weddings, the perform ri nce of a " real "ceremony upon the boards is Anmethine unique. The wedding took place during the last act of the performanee of " Sinbati ; or the Lass that Loy:4l a Sailor." ,The enter- tainment was temporarily dis:lOntinued, and, as a pre- liminary, the comedian of the company, Mr. Charles A. 1 Pusses', came forward and announced to the audience that the weddinr, was about to .take place. - - .</p>
        <p>The bride, in her costume of Sinbad, stepped from the wings, draped in a -cloak. She was followed and sur- rounded ,by the chorus. Then the bridegroom, in a dress suit., made his a pprars ner. accompani,d by a justice of the peace. The chorus acted as hride.,tnairls, while the male members of the company supportea the bridegroom ;Is " heat men." N&amp;th;.r of the contracting parties it will he seen, lacked sunport. It might have. been well to have called in an assistant for the justice, who per- formed the cf-remony. Ile had a had. attack of stage fright. His voics quart-red and his knees knocked together as he spoke the fatal words that united the pair.</p>
        <p>• ROYAL COLLEGE PHYSICIANS AND SURGEONS, LONDON. Among tho.ze who have succes4ully cornpl?ted the firAt of to tt-iree examinations for the above qualification recently held in London,we are pleased to notice the name of Mr. James Bennett, of Victoria Univerity, son of Mr. .Tni,n Bennett, of Bew,iea</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
