      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING COMPANY.</head>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING COMPANY.</p>
        <p>LIMITED. LLANELLY. SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>THEY TOOK THE CANE. On Wednesday afternoon two boys named James Coyne and Griffith Roberts, were going through the market place and when passing Mr. Emerson's stall of confectionary, they could not resist the temptation, but seized a cake each and ran off. A man who had seen the act promptly gave chase, and gave them in charge of P.C. Barlow who was near to. P.C. Barlow upon searching them not only found the two cakes upon them but in Robert's pocket a rat-trap was found which it was discovered had been taken from the stall of John Lae. They were brought before the magistrates on Thursday morning, when it was stated by various members of the police force that Roberts was utterly neglected by his father. The boys were discharged, but Roberts senior was told that he would have the eyes of the N.S.P.C.C. officer upon him in the future.</p>
        <p>OVERCROWDING AT WARRINGTON. On Friday,at the Borough Police Court,Mary Baguley, Dial-court, was summoned at the instance of Inspector Norman, charged with keeping lodgers on the 24th July without being registered to do so. Inspector Powell stated that on the date named he visited the defendant's house. He found it without any through ventilation,and it was occupied by six persons, whilst only adapted for four. There were two lodgers in addition to the family. The floors of the house were very defective. Defendant was fined &amp;including costs.—Chas. Hankinson,Wood's- court, was charged with a similar offence on the same date. Inspector Powell stated that the defendant had been served with a notice for overcrowding previous to the day of his visit. On the 2441 inst. when he visited the house he found it was occupied by a family of six in addition to one lodger. The house was only adapted for three persons. He found it in a very dirty condition, and with no through ventilation. A fine of 53. was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>THEFT FROM DALLAM FORGE.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM DALLAM FORGE.</p>
        <p>Michael Logan. -36 Siamfora-street, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday, with steal- ing from the joiners' shop at Dallam forge, on July 11th, a plane valued 10s. the property of Henry Atherton,also two chisels and a hand saw valued 63. the property of Frederick Cooper, and also on June 13, a clock valued ss. the property of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. The defendant was employed at the forge as an ash wheeler. On July 11th, the joiners in the works locked up their tools, but when they returned the following morning, they found that the box had been opened, and the arti- cles enumerated above were missing. Information was given to the police, and a description of the articles was given to the various second-hand shops of the town. Some days. afterwards Mr. Leslie, Pinners Brow, noticed some of the tools in his shop answering the description, and he gave information to the police,and Logan's house was searched. In it was found a clock which had been missing from the lodge at Dallain forge for some time. Logan was then brought to the police station.—The prisoner said that he had stolen the things in order to get bread for his family, as work had been so bad of late.— P.C. Lawrence said that it was correct that there was very little work and the prisoner had borne a very good character up to the present.—The bench decided to send the prisoner to gaol for one month for stealing the clock, and recorded a conviction against him for the other two offences. ANOTHER BATCH OF CASES OF CRUELTY TO HORSES. At the Borough Police Court, on Friday, a number of cases of cruelty to horses was heard. The first case dealt with was that of James Timmins, 292, Winwick- road, who was charged with having illtreated a horse by working it in an unfit state on the 17th nit, at IN ar- rington, and William Timmins was charged with work- ing the samo. Inspector Luff R.S.P.C.A. stated that he was willing to withdraw the case on payment of costs as the defendant had since slaughtered the horse. The defendants were therefore discharged on payment of s. costs.—Bichard Boscow, Turner-stiett, was sum- moned for illtreating a horse by beating it on the 18'h July. Inspector Luff said he was at the horse fair in the town on the date named when he F w the defendant riding a horse up Winwick-road. He had a thick stick in his hand with which he was beating the horse up and down the street. This went on for about 20 minutes. When witness examined the horse he found that it was covered with marks. Every horse the defendant had ridden that day had been similarly treated. He was fined 10s. and costs.—Fred Hall, hawker, 3, Brown-street Wigan, and James Thomas Rudd, hawker, Hilton's-yard, Low Murray-street, Wigan, were both charged with cruelly illtreating a horse by beating it on tire 18th ult.—lnspector Luckings, R.S.P.C.A., informed the bench that at 2.30 on Wednesday last, he was at the horse fair, Winwick-st., when he saw the defendant Rudd, running behind an old brown horse, beating it unmercifully with the lash of a whip. Witness got D.S. Monaghan and Inspector Luff to go with him, and they followed the two defendants into Tanner's-lane. Hill then to( k the whip from Rudd and commenced thrashing the horse with it. Witness went to the horse and found it covered with wails, some of them about two feet long which had been done by the whip. He spoke to the defendants about their conduct and they said they were very sorry. The only excuse they gave was that they wanted to make it go.—lnspector Luff corroborated. Each defendant was fined ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>AN IMPUDENT BEGGAR. Thomas Npwton, of no settled abode, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, with steal- ing from Bank House, Sankey-st., sometime on Wednes- day July 25th, two umbrellas valued 325., the property of Alice and Francis Reynolds. lie was also charged with stealing from Messrs. Eddowes and Co.,Scotland- road, on the same date, two bottles of mineral water. The prisoner had been brought before the magistratss on Thursday charged with being in possessioa of an um- brella without being able to give any satisfactory account of how he obtained it, and was remanded for further inquiries to be made. During the course of Thursday P.C. Barnett in making inquiries discovered that the prsoner had attempted to take two bottles from the yard of Mr. Eddowes, mineral water merchant, and had also an umbrella, which Mr. Maccabe who was em- ployed by Mr. Eddowes thouht belonged to a person who had" put up " in the yard. Maccabe took the umbrella off him, and afterwards handed it to the officer In the meantime, publicity having been given by the press of the fact that the initials F. A. B. were upon the umbrella originally found upon him, Mrs. Reynolds, of Bank House, and her daughter came to the policestation and identified the two umbrellas as their property. They had missed them since about Wednesday. Mrs. Rey- nolds said that she had not seen the prisonerat her house during the past week, but a week Wore that, he had walked into the hall for the purpose of begging.—The prisoner said that the umbrellas were given to him by a traveller, and the mineral water bad been given to him by " Sam'l " (pointing to Maccabe). Maccabe however stepped f, rward and said that his name was not " Saml." but Sohn, and he had never given him any mineral water.—The Chief Constable said that the pris- oner was a pest to the tradesmen of Warrington as he</p>
        <p>Th..ltovai Laundresses say UritehiAy's 3TAtiCEI ULU S is unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched linen, ac., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Com. wall, says My wife will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss." From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sit.— lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-ealled Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 31., and 63., hy most stands :miles. It not in stock will procure it. '1 CIUTCAL.AY. BLeekburn. P:oprietor.—itdgl.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>was continually stopping them. The magistrates decided to send the prisoner to gaol for two months for the first offence, and one month for the second. A POLICEMAN'S HELMET BATTERED TO PIECES. John Miller was charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, with being drunk and disorderly on Sunday morning, and also with assaulting P.C. 's Cooper and Marsh at the same time and place. P.C. Cooper (20) said that on Saturday night he saw a number of men making a great noise in Fennel-st. He told them to "get along "but prisoner said that he u ould murder him. They however, went away. At one o'clock on Sunday morning, he heard a noise again in the same place. He went there and ordered them away, but the prisoner refused to go. He became very rough, and in the struggle witness lost his helmet. He found it the next morninc, in Cockhedge-lane, " and here," said he (lilting up a'bundle of pieces of cloth and leather), " is all that is left of it "(great laughter). Alderman Elarri- son :" I suppose it is almost new." P.C. Cooper :" 0 yes, it is pretty new. It is only two years old." (Re- newed laughter). Continuing the officer stated that the prisoner was so rough that he was obliged to go and obtain the assistance of P.C. Marsh, and they two received numerous blows and kicks in various parts of the body while bringing him to the police station.—P.C. Marsh gave corroborative evidence and P.C. Baxendale said that when brought into the police office,the prisoner pulled off his coakto him and he was obliged to knock him down.—A witness named Shepherd now came for- ward and accused the police of dragging the prisoner along, but he said that he did not see the commence- ment of the disturbance.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for one month.</p>
        <p>YOUTHFUL BURGLARS. At the Warrington Borough Court on Thursday, John Maguire (13), 10 Stoney-yard, Bank-st., and James Con- way (14), 28 Dolman's-lane, were each charged with stealing from the shop of William Isaac, Buttermarket- stree, on the previous night, 5d., his property. Maguire was further charged with stealing 3d. on July 31. The Chief Constable stated that Mr. Isaac came to him on Wednesday saying that he was suspicious that there were systematic robberies on a small scale, going on in his premises. A short time ago, a square of glass was broken in Clothall-yard, at the back of the premises of Mr. lasac. There was also a bakery at the back of the premises. The jagged pieces of glass in the window pane had been taken away, and it was suspecred that the thieves came that way. On Wednesday night, Detective Monaghan and P.C. Bruen secreted themselves upon the premises. After the baker and everyode employ ed on the premises had left, the watchers noticed the two boys roll a barrel to the wall and while Conway kept "nix," Maguire quickly slipped through the opening in the window,and in a very short time, he was out again. He was so quick that Detective Monaghan had difficulty in catching him. Bruen had also a chase after Conway-, but he was successful in capturing him. They were taken to the police station, and sd. was found upon Maguire. The money was marked. Maguire also ad- mitted taking 31 the previous night hut said thatiCon- way was not with him on that occasion. Maguire had been an errand boy for Mr. Isaac about a month. It was Maguire's brother who had broken the window pane. The magistrates ordered the boys to be kept in custody for two days,and hoped that their parents would chastise them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Wright (chairman), Coun- cillor H. Roberts and Mr. S. Wright. _ _</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF A SAW.—Samuel Heath, 3, Erwood- street, Warrington, was charged with stealing from a house in course of erection in Watkin-street, one saw • value 55., the property of Arthur Mounfield. Inspector Spinks stated that from information received the prev- ious night he went to the shop of Mr. Leslie of Pinners- Brow and there found the saw produced which had since been identified by Mr. A. Mounfield as his property. It had been missing since the first of April last. The defendant had offered the saw in pledge, but Mr. Leslie refused to accept it, as he found on (xan4ning it, that a name had been scratched off both sides of the saw. Mr. Leslie asked the defendant to go to the Police Station, but he would not. Witness visited the defendant's house and found another saw there. On being shown the saw, which the defendant had offered in pledge, he said" I never saw it before in my life." On being charged with the theft afterwards he said he did not steal it but he knew it was Mr. A. Mounfield's saw. A remand was asked for until Monday, in order that further inquiries might be made respecting the other saw. The magis- trates acceded to the application for remand. KEEPING A DOG WITHOUT A LICENCE.—Charles Newton was charged with keeping a dog without a licence on the 13th June. Mr. W. H. Cogman prosecuted on behalf of the Commissioners to the Inland Revenue, and called Mr. Price, an Inland Revenue officer, who proved the case. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed. , COWARDLY ASSAULT ON A Boy.—Herbert Sanders, a young man. St. Mary-st., Latchford, was charged with assaulting and beating Frederick Stockdale,a boy, living at 65, New-road, Latchford, on the 19th inst. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, who represented the complainant, stated that previous to the date named ,the complainant had been ill at home for a fortnight. It was the boy's first time out after his illness, and he was with two others named James Wells and John Eckersley. Tile defendant came to them and said that they had been on his fence. They denied it, whereupon the defendant put his knee behind the complainant, and threw him to the ground, and also struck him with his fist. The lad got up, and he was again thrown to the ground by the defendant, who also got hold of the lad's throat. He afterwards dragged the lad on the- ground about 100 yards, to the defendant's house, where he again struck him. A young man named Pemberton came to the assistance of the lad. The defendant had since been written to but he had taken no notice of the letters. Evidence in corroboration of Mr. Browne's statement was given by the complainant, and James WE Ils, John Eckersley, and Jos. Pemberton. The defendant admit- ted putting the lad to the ground twice, but denied either striking or dragging him on the ground. His mother told him that she had seen the b3ys on the fence Evidence for the defence was also given by the defend- ant's father, and Mr. Miller. The chairman said there was no doubt that the defendant had received some pro- vocation but he should not have abused the complain- ant. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed £1 94. in all. ASSAULT ON A WOMAN.—Margaret String.er, 5 Guar- dian-street, a young woman, was fined 74. 6 I. including costs for assaulting Sarah Ann Wright, 28, Aikin-street, en the 21st inst. The evidence showed that the de rend- ant struck the complainant several times across the head. MONDAY.—Before Mr: I% Monks (chairman), A ldermar jjarrison, Mr, J. W. Hayward; And Mr. S. Wright. "</p>
        <p>STIIANOC CONbCCT OF A MANCHESTER WOMN. Elizabeth Spencer was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Church-st., and alto with wilfully damag- ing a window valued 103., the property of Josiah Whittle, on Saturday night. P.C.Leonard said that Mr. Whittle's son came to him on Saturday night, and asked him to come and remove the .woman who was sitting on his step drunk. When the officer came in B:gilt of the woman she got up and pulled off her shoe,and after waving it in the air she smashed two large panes 6f glass with it. Before witness could get to her, she put her hands through the—broken window and nicked up a fancy flower pot and smashed it on the floor. Witness then came up and locked her up. She had come from Manchester that day, and did not know where she got drunk. The magistrates fined her 53., and also ordered her to pay the cost of the window. DRUNKENNESS. —F(,/. this offonce Martin LT-.ns. 20, Bank-st., was fined 2s. 61.; Bridget McMullen ,last from Preston, ordered to leave the town ; Margaret Smith, last frpm Birkenhead, similarly treated ; Catherine Logan, James-st.,2A. 61. to the police poor box. A DISHONEST WORKMAN.—SIMueI Heath, 3. Erwood- st., was to sent to gaol for 14 days for stealing from some houses in course of erection in Watkins-st., a s-4 w, valued ss. the property of Arthur Mounfield. The prisoner had been employed by Mr. Mouofield, and had taken the saw home on April Ist. On Saturday night he went to sell it at Mr. Leslie's second hand shop, but Mr. Leslie noticed that an erasure of the name had been made upon t'.e handle. He at once told the prisoner that the saw had been stolen, whereupon the prisoner said that he could keep the saw, arid walked out of the house. Leslie followed and, the prisoner went into Bevvsey forge. Leslie then told P.C. Lawrence and the Prisoner was then arrested and taken to the Police Station. THEFT BY A LODGER. Florence Henderson was charged with stealing from the dwelling-house of Elizabeth Williams, 573, Knutsforthroad, on May 30, a sheet valued 25., her property, and also a shirt valued 34., the property of Tom Proctor, a lodger. Detective Monaghan stated that the prisoner was a lodger at the house' and had taken the articles from a box. The Bench inflicted a fine of 103., including costs for each offence.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY To tloesEs.—Patrick Hanlon. 8. Halifa was charged with working a horse while in an unfit state, and John Cook was also charged with causing it to be worked on July 11. P.C. Almon said that he warned Hanlon not to take the horse out as it was too lame for work,but he went out nevertheless. Inspector Luff said that he would withdraw the case on payment of costs against Cook, as the horse had been slaughtered since. Cook was accordingly fined in the costs, and Haulm fined 10s. including costs. Michael McConville, Simm's Cross, Widnes,was fined sa. and costs for working a horse while in an unfit state in Winwick-at., on July 17. John Sampson ,28, School-brow,and Patrick McLough- lin, 19, School-brow, were also ordered t-) pay costs for committing a similar offence in Horsemarket-st., on the 12th July.</p>
        <p>DESERTERS.-Wm. Coyne,a °rummer boy, and George Payne were each remanded to Strangeways, on the charge of deserting, the former from Her Majesty's Second King's Regiment stationed at Manchester, and the latter from H.M.First Battalion Connaught Rangers, stationed at Portsmouth.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Mr. J. Platt (chairman):and Mr. E. J. Guest.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL CASES.—Mr. G. Riley prosecuted in the fol- lowing cases for irregular attendance at school :—Wm. Mustoe, Bewsey-road, is.; George Rowlands, Winwick- road, 23. 6LI. ; Moses Williams, Winwick-road, ss. ; Rich. Booth, Warburton-st., 53. ; Mary Johnson,Bewsey-road, 53. ; Walter Lunn, Bewsey-road, 53. A FONDNESS F JR PERAMBULATORS.—MiIIicent Sullivan, daughter of Sergeant-Major ullivan, was charged with stealing on July 23, a perambulator, the property of Clara Smith, Pierpoint-st., and also a mail cart, the pro- perty ot Je,hn Porter, Queen-st. The Chief Constable said that Clara Smith used the perambulator for the pur- pse of carrying various articles about. On the above date she went to Jackson's pawnshop with some cloth- ing. On coming out, she met the prisoner who went with her along Queen-st. After they had gone some dis- tance Clara Smith suddenly remembered that she had left a bundle in the shop and went back for it. When she returned, both the prisoner and the perambulst)r were missing, and she could find them nowhere. She went home and told her mother, who then came out along with her to look for it. They met the prisoner, who said that she did not know who had taken the perambulator, and whoever had done so, "must be a big thief." Failing to find it, the matter was reported to the police, and Detective Monaghan, on Sunday,men- tioned the matter to Sergeant-Major Sullivan, who at once said that his daughter had brought a perambulator and also a mail cart home,saying that she had found the former,and paid a halfpenny for the latter. The peram- bulator was at once identified by Mrs. Smith, and Mrs. Porter also identified the mail cart. As the prisoner was of respectable family, she was not locked up during the night, her father undertaking that she should be present that morning.—Sergeant-Major Sullivan said that he thought that the two vehicles were only old lumber or he would not have allowed his daughter to have brought them into the house.—The Bench considered that the father had not done as he ought to have done in the matter, as he ought to have reported it to the police as nine persons out of ten would have done. They would leave the punishment of the girl with him, but would mark their sense of his carelessness by inflicting a fine of ss. in each case.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Mr. S. Cbandley (chairman), Alder- man Sutton and Councillor J. Fairclough. _</p>
        <p>DESERTEIL—WiIIiam Fletcher was remanded until Monday on the charge of deserting trom Her Majesty's Ship Ganges at Falmouth. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Owen Smith was discharged on promising to leave the town. _ . _</p>
        <p>A SUSPICIOUS CHLRACTER.—Catherine Bowden. well known to the police, was charged that she being a con- victed thief was found frequenting Hill-st. on Wednes- day evening.—lnspector Spinks said that he saw the woman in company with a man, who afterwards said that he had lost 7s. &amp;I.—P.C. Corbett having corrobo- rated, prisoner was sent to gaol for six week.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT. MONDAY.—Before Mr. James Tinsley. AN OLD OFFENDER.—EIizt Smith and Emma Edwards were chaned with being drunk at Rixton n Sunday. Both prisoners pleaded guilty. Smith, who had 61 prev- ious convictions recorded against her, was fined 103. and costs, or in default 14 days' imprisonment, and the other prisoner was fined 53. and costs,or seven days' imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before:Major Pickme.r.(chairman), Mr. J.</p>
        <p>Crbsfield and Mr. J. White.</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—Charles Wilson was discharged with a caution for begging iia Orford on that morning. AN UNREGISTERED CANAL BOAT.—Joseph Gordon was charged that on June 21, in Sankey Canal, he allowed a boat to be used as a dwelling without being registered, and Robert Fiawes was charged with using it. Inspector Kerfoot said that lie saw the defendant Flawes on the boat which was not marked and registered as required by the Canal Boats Act. He went on board and dis- covered that Gordon was Flavres' master. He accord- ingly summoned the two defendants. The beach inflicted a fine of 103. and costs.</p>
        <p>SHOOTING A PlGEON.—Richard Roberts was charged with killing a homing pigeon valued 203., the property, of John Hill, at Sankey, on July 17. Mr. Longton pros- ecuted, and Mr. P. Davies defended. Mr. Longton said that Hill resided at 95, Cartwright-street, and on the morning of the above date, he sent a man named Bradbury to Sankey with a pigeon in order to liberate it there. Bradbury liberated it, and after making two or three circles in the air, it alighted upon the house of Mr. James Fairclough, a farmer. It had not been there a minute, when it was shot dead and rolled over into the garden. Bradbury went to the hedge, and there saw Roberts with a double barrelled gun in his hand, looking for the pigeon. He told him that he would have to pay for it. Bradbury went again with Hill to Sankey in the afternoon and saw Mr. Fairclough, Roberts' employer. Fairclough said that he was sorry that the bird had been killed, and went behind the house and fetched the pigeon. A summons was then taken out. The bird was a short distance strain,and was valued at 20s. The con - plainant had paid 203. for it.—Evidence was given by -Hill arid Bradbury in support of Mr. Longton's state- ment.—Mr. Davies for the defence said that his client had had a good character previously and never had been summoned before. He said that he was working in the afternoon of the day in question and Bradbury was seen by a man named Collins, who was working on a stack, to throw up a pigeon, and immediately afterwards a report was heard and the pigeon dropped. During this time, the defendant was working in the garden, and never had a gun in his hand. Bradbury at once charged the defend- ant with shooting the bird, but the defendant denied having shot it. He never expected to be summoned for the shooting of a bird. They did not know who hail shot the bird. The defendant had not had a gun for over twenty yehrs.—The witness Collins was then called, after which the bench said that they considered the case proved, and fined 203., the cost of the pigeon, and also the costs. _ _ _</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Martin Comber, Golborn, was fined 21. GI for this offence. CYCLIST IN TROUBLE.—Wm. Henry Haves was fiued 6. 6J. for riding on the footpath in Sankey.</p>
        <p>Funious DRIVING. —Thomas Boardman Newton, was fined 10s. and costs for furiously driving his horse and trap along 13Iackbrook-rcad, on July 18. P.C. Gresty proved the case. Patrick O'Neil was fined 20s. and costs for the same offence on July 6th. Inspectors Luff and Luckings and Sergeant Barnes gave evidence to seeing the defendant driving the h,orse and a waggonette containing twelve permons. It was totally unfit for work as it was in a most exhausted condition.</p>
      </div>
