      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 4, 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>S:A.MTITr7IDTAy, .Beilfloerseijr:l;nlerl. N4l(:.Ccor.qllTedcaolercp(ocdhallierm,aa!):(,,i _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Colonel J. L. WOod. DRUNKENNESS.—Tho following Were summoned for this offence: William Downes, Newton. fined 2s. 61. and costs; 'Thomas Lloyd. Haydock, 53. and costs. ALLOWING THEIR DOGS TO BE AT LARGE.—The foil .W- -ing were.fined for this ()Since. George Wavwell,.Ne ton ; Henry Picktbn, Haydock ; Benjamin -Wadswor: Haydock. A CYCLIST FlNED.—Henry Shargold was fined 23. 61. and costs for riding a bicycle without a light in Morl-y- -lane, Newtan. at 10 10 p.m. July 11, without a light. R.S.P.C.A.CASEi.—Jeseph Robinson was charged with working his horse while in an unfit state at Newton on July 6th, and Joseph Reynolds was charged with cans. ing it to be so worked. Inspector Luff R S.P.C.A. grid that he was at Newton racecourse en the above date aiid saw the defendant R dainson driving a horse attached t acart. The horse was continuallyllinching, and witn-ss upon examining it found it to be unfit for work that day. He also discovered that the owner was Rylands. He ordered them to t .ke the horse away Which they 'The magistrates-fined Rylands 103. and Costs, and Robin- son 53. and costs. William ll.idd lak was charged with overcirivinfr and torturing his horse on July the 6th. Inspector Luckings R.S.P.C.A. said that on the above date, he was at, the racecourse at Newton when lie saw the defendant drivini, the horse attached to a waggonette. 'The horse was in an emaciated condition and was almost droppingto the ground from-e'xhaustion. It was totally unfit for work. Defendant was fined 20s. and costs. . . Edwin Bickerstaffe was also fined 203. and cost for beating and overdriving his horse on the same date. Inspector Luckings and Sergeant Barnes each gave °yid- enc2 to the effect that the defendant was driving the horse near Newton racecourse. and had beaten it so much that it was covered with " white lather," from head to foot and was in a meet exhausted condition. His excuse WAS that he was late for the races. Robert Farnworth was charged with illtreating his horse at Newton racecourse on July 7th. Inspector Luckings and a. witness named Gregory'each gave evid- ence to the effect that the defendant was violently beat- ing his horse' and the off .side was literally covered with " whails "arid a mark of a knot in the whip was also • distinctly visible. The bench considered this to be the worst case of the lot, and fined the defendant 40g. and costs. William Bdl Was fined 54. and coats by working his n horse while in a unfit state at Newton on July 6th. The evidence of Inspectors Luff and Luekings showed that the horse flinched considerably, and when examined a wound of the size of a 23. piece was discovered. He took the horse home when told by the inspectors. William Bennett was charged with a similar offence at Newton racecourse on July 7th, and at Golhorne on July 11th. Inspectors Luff and 'Thorne each gave evid- ence to seeing the horse walk very lame on the two dal es. and the defendant was fined 20s. and costs on each charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
