      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Alderman H. Neild (in the chair), and Messrs. C. Marson, H. Talbot, and W. C. Jones. SELLING WITHOUT SCALES.—Lowe and Monks, bakers, Runcorn, was summoned for belling bread without the necessary scales.—The case having been proved, the defendant Lowe pleaded that the orders for the bread had been previously received, when the bread was weighed. They did not think it necessary to send scales with the cart.—The Chairman said there seemed to be some little misunderstanding. A fine of 103. would be imposed. DRUNKENNESS.—Thomas Hatton, John King,and Sarah Johnson were fined for drunkenness. KEEPING A DOG WITHOUT A LICENCE.—WaIter Starkey, was fined 103. and costs for having on the 19tb June, at Dutton, kept a dog without a licence. Defendant pleaded that he picked up the dog on the canal bank. AN UNMUZZLED DoG.—Peter Robinson was summoned for having on the 13th July at Walton had a dog at large unmuzzled.—P.C. Robinson said he saw the doc, following defendant's cart.—Defendant pleaded that the dog was not his. He believed that it belonged to John Fleming of Warrington.—The case was dismissed. SLEEPY DRIVERS.—SamueI Lunt was fined ss. and costs for having been found fast asleep in charge of a horse and cart on the 3rd July.—George True, was fined 53. including costs for a similar offence. TRACTION ENGINE DRIVER FlNED.—Charles Woodward was fined ilk and costs for driving a traction engine on the road at Stretton on the 19:.h July, between the hours of 7 p.m. and 3.30 a.m. TRESPASSERS.—John Allen, Henry Musker, and Henry Birtles were charged with having on the 15th July, at Dutton, unlawfully committed trespass in pursuit of game on land bel inging to Thomas Baxter.—John Wil- liams, gamekeeper, said that he saw the defendants with five dogs ranging the field.—Benjamin Lyon, another gamekeeper, corroborated.—Allen was fined 10s. and costs, and the other defendants, against whom there were previous convictions, were fined 20s. and costs each.</p>
        <p>A BOATMAN CHARGED WITH THEFT.—George Dawson, boatman, Runcorn, was summoned for having on the 28th June, at Preston Brook, stolen a scythe, the pro- perty of Mr. J. B. Taylor.—P.C. Griffiths said that from information he received he went to the defendant's house at Runcorn and found a scythe. He took defendant into custody and, on the way to the police station, he admitted that he had sold the scythe for 2s.—William Royle, of Dunham Massey, deposed to having bought the scythe from the defendant.—Thomas Smith, a farm labourer in the employ of Mr. Taylor, said he left the scythe about 100 yards from the canal bank on the 28th June.—Defendant pleaded that he found the scythe in the canal, and was supported in his statement by his son. —The Bench decided to dismiss the case.</p>
        <p>iHE LATE SIR GILBERT GREENALL.—AIderman Neild (chairman) referred to the loss sustained by the death of Sir Gilbert Greenall. No doubt they had all heard with revet of his death. Sir Gilbert was the oldest magis- trate in the division. Mr. Arthur Browne, Warrington, on behalf of the local solicitors, sppke in kindly terms of the late baronet, referring to his kindness and courtesy on all occasions.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PErIY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Befere Messrs. Henry. Reynolds, S. Taylor,</p>
        <p>and T. Handley.</p>
        <p>RENEWAL OF LICENSES.—The magistrates granted the renewals of game licenses to the following applicants : Mrs. Brimelow, 25, Bridge-st., and Mrs. Hardley, 51, Regent-st. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY.—Peter Riley was granted per- mission to sell at the Royal Oak, Weston, lately occupied by Alice Mary Green ; and a similar authority was granted to Thomas Dyer, at the Albion Inn, Nelson-st., recently kept by Henry Hill. OBSCENITY.—For making use of obscene and profane language in Nelson-street on the 20th July, Elizabeth Santley Smith was fined ss. or an alternative of seven days in gaol. THE EFFECTS OF ALCOHOL.—The following persons were each tined 53. including costs, or seven days Im- prisonment for being drunk and disorderly :—Henry Cooke. 40, Robert-street; Christopher Connolly, and John Hannon, both of Water-street.</p>
        <p>KEEPING A DOG WITHOUT A LICENSE.—Henry Grounds of Higher Runcorn, was fined 53. and Bs. 6d, costs for having kept a dog on the 14th July without possessing a license for the same.</p>
        <p>BEGGING Auds.—John Jordan, an old man, of no fixed residence, was sent to prison for seven days' hard labour for having begged alms on the 28th July in Lord-street. Evidence in support of the charge was tendered by P.C. Wright who said that when the prisoner was refused alms by certain persons he became very disorderly and made use of abusive language towards them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CARDINAL LEDOCHOWSKI.</head>
        <p>CARDINAL LEDOCHOWSKI.</p>
        <p>Cardinal Ledoehowski, whose death was wrongly announced at the beginning of the week,was,twenty years ago, the leading spirit of the fight between Church and Stats in Prussia. He too, was the first notable sufferer in the cause,for he was imprisoned in 1874 for contumacy against the statutes known as the May Laws. Bismarck has said that one of the gravest mistakes he has ever made was his estimation of the character of the Archbishop of Gnesen and Posen—as Ledoehowski then was. Bismarck had chosen him for this position as the best man to prevent the Polish clergy from indulg- ing in political agitation—but Ledochowski went in the directly opposite direction. The Chancellor was more surprised then ever to find that the Pope had made him Primate of Poland—a title which assumed that country's independence. A great stir was caused throughout Germany when Ledochow- ski was thrown into prison—where he was kept for two years. But, in the end, he had his triumph. He was a great favourite with the Pope Pius IX. and with his successor. When the Kaiser went recently to Rome he made, 'a point of being espe- cially gracious to the victorious Cardinal, whom his ex-Chancellor had imprisoned,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>TEMPERANCE IN THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE IN THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>Sir George White, our Commander-in-Chief in India has been giving in an address some remarkable facts ' and figures bearing upon the progress of the temperance movement among the British troops in that part of the world. Over 22,000 men now belong to the Army Tem- perance Society, that number being equal to one-third of the entire strength of the troops in the country. In 1890, the number was little more than 13,00). The ad- va,nta!,c to discipline of the great change thus rapidly taking plaCe is almost self-evident, hut statistics bring out the fact still more clearly. The Court-Martial return for 1893 shows that there were eight general courts-martial held for the trial of British soldiers, and of those tried at them not one was an abstainer. Out of 1,100 regimental courts-martial, only 39 sat to try members of the Temperance Association, and at the 2,680 courts-martial of all classes held during the year, only 73 members of the same Association appeared in answer to charges of a more or less serious character. Had they had their fair share of prisoners at these 2,680 trials, the temperance men would have contributed about 869 prisoners, or nearly 800 more than was actu- ally the ease. Experience had taught him, said Sir George White, that nearly all the crime in the British army is directly or indirectly traceable to drinking to excess.</p>
        <p>WORTH KNOWIN'T.—WOODEND'S FAMOUS NINE OILS. is A boon to sufferers and ucts like magic in all cases of RHEUMATISM, SPRAINS, BRUISES, and STIFF JOINTS. Pri.e Is. 41. Der bottle, carriage paId.—J.WOODEND. chemist. 22. Pitt-street. Liverpool.—Advt.</p>
        <p>China's natimal hymn is so long that people take half a day to listen to it. The area of the Czar's individual possessions pf land is greater than the entire extent of France. The oldest epitaph in England is found 'dated 1370. Its obsolete language is almost unintelligible.</p>
