      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CRI CKET.</head>
        <p>CRI CKET.</p>
        <p>SHOW THIS TO SOME WOMAN.</p>
        <p>I will send a valuable, instructive, and highly interesting book which eve-y woman would be glad to have Contains important and confidential advice and tells both married and single how to keep healthy and avoid the anxieties so distressing to the sex, tent free, securely sealed, for one stamp to cover postoge. Aranasa Professor E. LA SALLE,l7,Eastbourae 1 errace, Paddington, London. W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BOWLING</head>
        <p>BOWLING</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOWLING LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB V. HORSE AND JOCKEY . These teams met on the green at the Liberal Club on Saturday to play off their first fixture in the Warrington Bowling League. Some good games were witnessed, and the match resulted in a win for the club by 153 to 145. Score : • LIBERAL CLUB, • HORSE AND JOCKEY. Taylor 7 Hardman Banner Stevenson Jones Lowe __ __</p>
        <p>Jennings • Langley 15 Hughes Strange J fArker l2 Wells J Starkey Bennett Mynekyne</p>
        <p>Total 153</p>
        <p>Total 145</p>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS.</p>
        <p>WINNINGTON PARK V. HALTON (RUNCORN).—A match between these ttvons was on Saturday afternoon decided at Winnington Park, the visitors being rather heavily defeated. Score :</p>
        <p>WINSINGTON. MILTON. Sing. D'ble. Sing. D'hle. J. ic W. Snate ‘i m .NlNa'llialtl, y l.3i 1 '" J. lloston 10 T. Tomlinson... 13 } 15 T. Wilkinson G. Starkey l3 It. Potter ..... .... 2 1 F.StTingfellow l3 t 15 11. Johnson J. Cooper . l2 1 J. Has9all H. Reeves T. Andrews ... 13 11. Barker T. Holmes l3 }.15 W. Bowden W. Unsworth . 13 J. Carter W. Oakes J. Hughes l3 S. Dawson 5 W. Hughes ... 13 1. 1, B. Dutton S. Forster - '' H. Clarke 2 1 C.Stringfellow. 13 1 15 W. Ray C. Hinton - W. Hassall - 4 1</p>
        <p>Totals 197</p>
        <p>Totals lO6</p>
        <p>•-•--• 317 153 Majority Winnington, 164 points.</p>
        <p>Ernest Longfellow, a son of the poet, who resides in Manchester, Massachussetts, has no literary ability. but is an extraordinary good painter. The present executioner in Germany, Herr Reindel, performed his one, hundreth execution last month, add numerous telegrams of congratulation were sent to him from various parts of the kingdom. An hour before the execution he received as a token of felicitation and good wishes a superb basket ot Malezhal Niel roses. An enterprising firm in New York has converted the roof of its restaurant into a garden with two promenades, plants, and arches of coloured lights. One end is occu- pied by a small stage, on which a musical entertain- ment is given. Small tables, on which refreshments are served, are placed here and there, with chairs for the accommodation of visitors, who ply a small admission fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MEErING OF RAILWAY MEN AT WARRINGTON. THE EMPLOYERS' LIABILITY BILL.</head>
        <p>MEErING OF RAILWAY MEN AT WARRINGTON. THE EMPLOYERS' LIABILITY BILL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning, a church parade in connec- tion with the Warrington branch of the Amalgana- ated Society of Railway Servants took place, When the men, to the number of about 150, assembled in front of the Town Hall at half-past nine,and shortly 'afterwards marched in processional order to the Parish Church, headed by a band under the direction of Mr. T. Armstrong. The procession was witnessed by a large number of persons. Seats were allotted to the men on their arrival at the church. The officiating clergymen' were the Revs. G. B. Thurston, E. J. Willcocks, and A. A. Markham. The sermon was preached by the Rev. A. A. Markham, who selected for his text the words " as Paul reasoned of righteousness, temperance, and judgment to come, Felix trembled and answered, GO thy way for this time, and when I have a convenient season I will call for thee." A public meeting of the railway men was held at the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, in the afternoon, when there was a crowded audience. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Birchall, J. P., and he was supported on the platform by Mr. E. Harford (general secretary of the Railway Servants Amalgamated Society), Mr. T. Malkin (president of the Warrington Trades Council), Mr. Battersby and various officials of the Warrington branch of the Society. The Chairman in his opening address said that Sunday afternoon was not a favourable time to hold meetings. of that description. There were people who took exceplon to holding meetings for purposes of that kind on Sun' days, and one gentleman had said to him that he v73_l going to break the Sabbath. To such people he coulu only say in the language of an Authority, which even they recognised, "The Sabbath was made for man, nn,t man for the Sabbath." (Applause.) In fact, he hai yet to learn that any day was too sacred to redress a wrong. He took it that their object that afternoon wa9 to popularise their organisation, which was a very admirable thing. There was no mistake about one thing. that no good could be accomplished in this direction without organisation and combination. (Applause.) The single individual was powerless against the great interests with which he had to contend. The aim of the great railway companys was chiefly to make dividends. The object of the Society was to keep tiv.,se railwaV companies out of the temptation of making their work- men keep unduly long hours,and of giving them the least possible wages. He had heard of some of the wages which the railway companies gave,and he did not knoW how they could, for shame, offer such wages to any man' It was to keep the companies out of temptation of thig kind that the Society was formed. Th4y nad, been told a great deal about the condition 0' America. He considered that a great deal of folls!? things had been done by some of the American unions ot late, but he had more .faith in the common sense of hils fellow countrymen. (Applause.) They had learned 110 W to act. Less than a hundred years ago, they saw Men breaking machinery and doing all sorts of things. NOW they had a better way of doing things by organizatien• The railway companies were in a favourable positioo compared with ordinary manufacturers. They were Ilov subjected to such fierce competition. He, himself, wal a tradesunionist at 15, and was one for many years, an. he had many awkward things to do in the way of meet' ing unreasonable employers. He believed in the On- ciple upon which the best of the English tradesunions were based—that of justice between an sters and met?. He was glad that the railwaymen recognized thei.r moral obligation by going to church that morning. did not mean to say that they were taking part in ahY particular doctrinal organisation, but that they recog: nised a great Ruler of the universe, and that they recW nised the principle taught 1,800 years ago. .• Do unto others as ye would be done unto." With regard to the Employers' Liability Bill, he thought it was a dangerout thing to allow the railway companies to contract out a measure such as that. (Applause.) Mr. J. ELLISON, president of the local branch, theh proposed the following resolution : " That this meeting expresses its hearty sympathy with the prolonged an resolute attack maintained by the Amalgamated SocietY of Railway-Servants upon the adverse condition of eir ployment obtaining in the railway service, and cordial 9 wishes them a speedy and successful issue to their ardtr ous struggle,and earnestly urges upon the local railway' men the duty of streng thing the organisation by benol33; ing members of the War rington branch without delakt He said that the local society was 84 strong. It ollPt to be 284. (Hear, hear.) He therefore hoped that a.,e the close of the meeting those railway men who were present and were not members would give in tile</p>
        <p>names. Mr. OGDEN seconded the resolution. He said thacit labour ought to be united. The Trades Council ha chosen three candidates for the next election of tbe„ Board of Guardians. They also recpiired members u13(37: all the various public bodies. He therefore hoped thhave the railway men would do their duty by j )ioing</p>
        <p>society. 'ed The resolution was put to the meeting, and carrl unanimously. Mr. HARFORD thanked the meeting for th unaninlitYt with which they had passed the resolution in favour oex combination, as it was by combination they becarlio powerful. He had seen the time when the railway 0124 were the most over-worked people in the country'. u; now through the persistent agitation of the Anialg mated Society of Ihilway St•rvants, they had a 11114 less time to work. He, himsr•lf, had had to work %hours for a single day's pay. After referring to Tar measures which had been passed by the society's eff,7lo he referred to the evidewce of the late Sir Gee. Fir 17,45 before a committee,in which he slid that Trades U' 11l had done a great deal for the working-classes generafar- in the country in improving their position, 'out the Ile • tiler they kept ti.em from the railways the better. d • (the speaker) thought that if it had dune so much 02, for the other trades of the country, it would also do tuea. same for the railway servants. He, therefore, h,e13140 that they would combine for their own sakes ana a— , for the sakes of their families. Mr. J.CLARRE then proposed the following resolution: —"That this meeting cordially approves of the ployers' Liability Bill passed by the-House of C0mm0.110,.. n.;and marks its strong disapproval of the hostile o of the House of Lords in the insertion of the clause gi ing power t) contract out of the Bill. It further 1: proves of the action of the Home Secretary in withdra" ing the Bill under such circumstances, and respect.f.° requests him Co reintroduce it at the first opportunlLY;r, (Applause.) He said th,t the resolution was ill Ilte mony with the policy of the society for the last twen</p>
        <p>years. Mr. LEVI JAMES seconded the resolution. St Mr. FlaPForip said that he had the pleasur3 at the -3,0 Trades Unionist Congress at Belfast of moving a res%; tion to the same effect. He believed the Bill as it 'yea introduced was one of the best measures ever introdu%o into any legislative assembly. It abolished the d()ctrad of common employment, and its action would havecrilorti the effect of making wotk in Great Britain as secure Yiole, accident as possible, as it made the employer respe„,re Coming to the contracting out clause, he said that tio were only three railway companies which adopteJ v„d insurance system, viz., L. and N.-W., Brighton ile South Coast, and North Eastern. - whether they were likely to receive adequate cornflet,?, Ue tion from that Rystetn. He held that it etight;',;.tits made illegal for men to contract out of their own r"" fOO or rather the rights of the widow and children. aft asked whether it was adequate compensatian to gis'ecei, engine driver, say £1 per week when disablzd hY dent, and when he came back to his work to he be foreman over the cleaning shed at El per week, ari,,i-get e asked ts; condemned to night work all his life. • They e()'11-„;j15T almost as much from being in sick clubs or frieo societies. He instanced the case of an engitle-driver was killed while working on the South Eistern ''j which did not adopt this system. He left two He (Mr. Harford) undertook the duty of " nest fr.en-arir'' and made a claim for compensation against the C_`l,,4oe pany. Without any dispute, the company sent 3 Che for .-C4OO which had been invested for the children. old this had taken place on the L. and N -W. they simply have obtained £lOO, as insurance money, PiV which, the deceased man had paid himself. also remember that it was the weekly contribution wtheo was taken out of the men's wages w filch cans 00 to be backward in joining the rail way unioting therefore urged upon them the necessity of surl'i use, the Bill, and of opposing the contracting 00t ' which placed them in the power of the 'railcva) panies. trie3 The resolution was then put to the meeting and ca unanimously. Were flarford Votes of thanks to the Chairman and Mr. passed on the"motion of Mr. Malkin seconded Vll‘jr/,, Battersby, the latter remarking that he and Mr. -1340 at" were two of the Trades Council candidates for the eta of Guardians. Mr. 13ircliall. and Mr. Harford replied, the meeting terminated.</p>
