      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>TYNWALD DAY. A DRIVE FROXI DOUGLAS TO ST. JOHN'S</head>
        <p>TYNWALD DAY. A DRIVE FROXI DOUGLAS TO ST. JOHN'S</p>
        <p>BY A CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>I I suppose it is not everyone that can make a visit to the Isle of Man,and yet, many hear from time to time, about some of its ancient places and customs. Being on the Island at the time when the celebra- tion of one of its ancient rights and customs was taking place, perhaps a short sketch of it wili not be out of place to the readers of the " Examiner." The little village of St. John's is but a few miles drive from Douglas, and a most pleasant and inter- esting drive it will prove to all who take an interest in ancient remains, legends and stories, of such places, and who wish to see Nature in all her gran- deur and beauty which can be seen along the whole drive. For at every turn there is something to attract the attention of the visitor, in the ancient ruin with its ivy-clad walls giving forth some token of its worth and usefulness in the past, pointing our memory back to those far off times when they were living and thriving institutions with living souls within them. But that has all passed and as I .have said, one can only see a few remains of what was once of so great an interest to those of earlier days. The day,sth of July,began very hopefully,the sun shone out beautifullysupon the island,it seemed as if it had reserved its beautiful and bright rays for that. day, brightening up the horizon, and filling every heart with gladness upon this special occasion. 'After passing many places of interest described to us by our driver, who like many others was ready and eager to give such information as was possible to give in passing, we arrived safely through a double line of naval and military detachments, stationed in the island. We reached St. John's only to find that dozens more of cars which had preceded us, and that hundreds of spectators eagerly waiting for the ancient ceremony to begin. This year it is exactly what it was last year, and what it has been from time immemerial, in fact it never changes. The weather is the only thing that presents any variation worth mentioning. Those who go year by year always know exactly what they are going to see ; the difference this time being a larger concourse of people, which was spoken of as 10,000. I suppose the centre of attraction was the new governor, Sir West Ridgway, who, by the by, seems to begin in earnest with the work entrusted to him. At St. John's, the annual promulgation from Tynwald Hill, of the laws passed during the year by the Manx Legislature. Why? we naturally ask. Well, it is a quaint ceremony,.prac- tised for generations—that no laws can be enforced until they have been read out to the whole people assembled there to hear such, and passed by them. And for such reading of the Laws, as one looks in the distance you are pointed to a mound, or as it is called, a hill," Tynwald Hill." Now this Tynwald Hill was, so the story runs, formed with sods brought from every parish in the Island and the tradition was that no mason's trowel should touch it. Agriculture was the ancient, the first business of man, to till the ground whence he was taken, and in representation of that ancient business, this mound was made entirely of sods, and the way of ascending the hill was, as the steps show, by circling the hill, and in that way arriving at the top. But, this year it has been modernised a little by the wish of the new governor,and much against the wish of the natives. There have been stone steps put up the hill, which 'to give it an ancient look, were covered by long grass, and rushes. So instead of going round the hill until they reached the top, they went as the stone steps led, straight up,to the top of the hill. I had a little time to wait before the ceremony began, so I took a stroll round as it is commonly called to eee what there was going on in the fair, for fair there is, at you are made aware of bv the call of She car driver, "are you going to Tyn- wald, and the Fair?" As you look down the road leading to Tynwald you see what seems to be a continual stream of cars laden with happy people with smiling faces. The railway company were taxed to their utmost, as load after load was emptied at St. John's, of gallant and sturdy northsiders attracted either by the ceremony or the fair. For the fair was an attraction, and right merrily it went. There were the usual exhibitions which are to be seen and found at such places and times, including large and small shows, The fair differed a little from the olden times, as large numbers of cattle used to he sold, but this time but a few changed hands. The Legislators assembled at St. John's Church at 11 o'clock. In the roadway close to the church were drawn up in line the men of the Royal Naval Reserves at present training at Peel, number- ing about 120. They wore their cutlasses and carried rifles. Chief Inspector W. T. Davey, R.V. was in command. Just after half past eleven, Sir Joseph West Ridgeway K.C.8., K.C.S.J., arrived from Douglas in a carriage drawn by a pair of horses, and was accompanied by Lady Ridgway and also Miss Ridgway. He wore his official dress, and was adorned with his orders of Bath and Star of Julia. He also wore two war medals. As he alighted at the gate of the church, the men of the Royal Naval Reserve gave a royal salute by presenting arms. His Excellency walked to- wards the church, the path being accord- ing to ancient custom, strewn with rushes. One side was kept by a detachment of the East York Regiment. The other side was kept by the 7th Volu- nteer Battalion King's Liverpool Regiment. As his Excellency the governor passed between the ranks, the men presented arms, while the band of the 7th played the National Anthem. And as His Excel- lency entered the church the choir sang the National Anthem, the congregation standing. There was also in attendance a goodly number of the lead- ing officers, friends, and also a number of members of the Council, and captains of parishes. Divine service was_ held by the Reverend John Corlett, (Government Chaplain) read the prayers, while the Reverend E. Ferrier also (Government Chap- lain) read the appointed lessons. At the conclusion of the service, the usual proces- sion to Tynwald Hill followed. The order was, four sergeants of police, the coroners, the translator of the Acts of Tynwald into the Manx language, captains of parishes, the clergy in file, high bailiffs, ! members of the House of Keys, clerk to Council, members of the Council, the Lord Bishop, the sword bearer, His Excellency the Lieutenant Governor, Government chaplains, surgeons of the Household and Chief Constable of the Island. The naval and military forces were drawn up the whole line of the procession, and presented arms as His Excellency passed. Having arrived at the summit of the hill His Excellency seated himself beneath the pavilion upon which the Union Jack floated. The chair of state as in days of old was over- spread with a crimson cloth. The Coroner of Glenfoba (Mr. Wm. Kermode) then addressed the court in ancient form charging in the name of the Queen that no person should brawl, quarrel, or make any disturbance. The scene around and about the hill was a very imposing one. Thousands of all grades thronged the hill around, and while the ceremony proceeded the noise we had heard but a few minutes before was hushed. The retiring coroners give up their wands of office to his Excellencywhichwere handed by himto others who kneeling took the oaths of office and were duly elected coroners for the year. The Laws that had been passed during the year were read out aloud. Deemster Drinkwater read the marginal notes and the royal assents in English, after which the coroner of Glentoba read them in Manse, the whole ceremony lasted about an hour, the people listening attentively through the whole, and at the conclusion three hearty cheers were given for Her Majesty,at the call of Deetneter Drinkwater, the band of the 7th playing "God save the Queen." The procession was then reformed in the same order, and the legislature returned to St. John's Chapel for the transaction of Government business, after which we drove off to Peel, and Glen Helen, and spent the rerrainder of the day enjoying ourselves as holiday makers can do with grand and glorious weather to help them.</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SURGICAL LAWSUIT.</head>
        <p>A SURGICAL LAWSUIT.</p>
        <p>THE DOCTOR AND TII;;', PARENT.</p>
        <p>An interesting surgical lawsuit lately took place in Germany. A girl, seven years old, was taken to a hospital affected with tuberculosis in the foot, and the directing physician, after vainly trying in other ways to prevent the spread of the disease, proceeded to amputate the root. The father of the child had previ- ously declared he would not permit the operation, and the little patient was already lyit.g on the operation- table under ether or chloroform when the father appeared at the hospital to t al-ie the child away. But the physi- cian,saying"lt is too latej will speak to the father after- wards," proceeded with and completed the operation. After this no new tuberculosis appeared, the child regained its ,trerigth, and lia.l !-:one develorilm*nrirmailv. The tailor tauught a case ot"wounding, by Ineani of a dangerous instrument" against the doctor, but the agnitt,d. The case is to be tried again, I however.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>- ' • • • ' , • - , , ' _ „ _„ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER, SAIURDAY AUGUST 4, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SAIURDAY AUGUST 4, 1894.</p>
        <p>speaking at Golborne asked what good the people had received from the Parish Councils Bill, lie evidently appears to be under the im- pression that the councils are already elected, for they surely cannot do either good or evil before they come into existence.</p>
        <p>A remarkable case of forgetfulness, I hear, took place at a village just outside Warrington. A per- son who resided there became the proud father of a bonny little son. There are duties to be performed when this event happens, and one of them is to in- terview the registrar to give him the name &amp;c., of the yuungster. The father accordingly trudged off to the registrar who lived about three miles away, and when he arrived there, the official commenced asking the necessary questions. " What is the child's name?" was a very simple question, but it proved to be a poser this time. " Wed, I've quite forgotten," was the reply,'but I'll go home and tell my wife to send ic on a piece of paper." The regis- trar informed the forgetful fellow that that would not do. He would have to come back with it him- self. He accordingly had to walk the three miles back to his house again, and then return with the name written on a piece of paper. He will perhaps be more careful the next time he has to go on sim- ilar business.</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonians will have noticed the rapid' progress which is being made inthe erection of the Parr Hall in Museum-street. The building is now almost ready for the roof and it may be confidently expected that the Hall will be ready for use during the winter season. The position and size oi the building should make the hall a most popular one for concerts and meetings. It is to be hoped that in future it will not be necessary for those inhabi- tants of the town who are desirous of hearing a first class concert to be obliged to travel to Manches- ter or Liverpool in order to do so. When possessed of a good hall, I should think, that the population of the town and neighbourhood is now sufficiently large, to secure an audience which would more than clear expenses for the providing of concerts by leading artistes.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Parr's Banking Company and Alliance Bank Ltd. had an excellent result of their labours to show at their meeting on Thursday, last week. A dividend of 19 per cent is one to make one's mouth water in the present time of depression. Large dividends are very rate, as I have noticed several large concerns in the country which have no divi- dend at all to give to their shareholders. It is quite clear that the Parr's Banking Company are upon a firm and substantial basis, the only failure thus far which they have to point to being their long name. AS this is to be shortened, that will soon be recti- fied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>TLHN GS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>TLHN GS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Owen Watkins of the Warrington Wesleyan circuit must be very careful or he will have the thunderbolts of the women's suffragists hurled at his devoted head. The Third London District had sent a lady—Miss Dawson—as a repre- sentative at the Wesleyan Conference held at Birmingham, last week, and it was to this fact that Mr. Watkins drew attention," and moved that a committee be formed to consider the validity of the election. This resolution at once brought the Rev. Hugh Price Hughes to his feet, he being a great advocate of Women's Suffrage. He contended that the question should be settled at once, and said that he considered women had every right to sit in con- ference. Mr. Watkins, however, persisted in his motion, and although defeated, another motion of a sightly different nature was carried. Thus the question of the right of women to sit in conference has been postponed for another year, when there will no doubt be a strong tight between the two opposing parties.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding that the novelty of the P.S.A. societies has to some extent gone their popu- larity does not seem to decline. On Sunday last week, there was a large assembly at Wycliffe Church while at the same time an equally large audience was present at Bold-street chapel in connection with the P.S.A. at the Y.M.C.A. The popularity of the P.S.A's. is marvellous when the failures of so many other schemes for the reformation of the people are considered and it is evident that they have a strong vitality. A very prominent feature of the meetings of the P.S.A. is the regularity with which the programme is gone through. Each item is timed for so long, so that the audience know when and what is coming next, which is an excel- lent arrangement and might be copied with advan- sage at other places,</p>
        <p>There is a great cry at the present time for economy in the rates, and in one respect, the officials at the workhouse would do well to practice a little of this desirable virtue. Notwithstanding the hot weather we have had—and Wed- nesday last week was hot enough for anything—at their Board meeting, which is held in a small room, they had a hot fie, and thus made the room frightfully close. They either ought to put the fire out altogether or go to the other extreme, and transform the room into a Turkish bath. It is very uncomfortable to be in the intermediate state as it is at most of the Board meetings.</p>
        <p>Councillor Hill is. " pu's about," with very small things at times. He is a member of the Board of Guardians, and at their last meeting he askad to see the bills of the house. He could not see them upon the table. Mr. Ward, the workhouse master, it ap- pears,had been on his holidays ; and, therefore, had not had time to prepare them for that day. Mr. Hill thought he had discovered something wrong, and he at once proceeded with great vehemence to com- plain that that was not the first time he had looked for the bills and could not find them. It happened repeatedly,and it was not fair to the members of the Board that they had no opportunity of examining them. Mr. Ward and the Clerk,hdwever,interrupted Councillor Hill's torrent of wrath by stating that never before in their recollection had the bills not been placed on the table at the meetings of the Board, and that if Mr. Hill could not find them, some other Guardian must have had them in his pos- session. This made Mr. Hill look very foolish, and in future he should take care that he has sufficient grounds for complaint before he "launches out" as lie did on that occasion.</p>
        <p>The Parish Councils Bill will soon come into operation, and already public attention is being drawn to it. The Rev. J. Kirtlan gave an address at the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan- chapel upon it last week and urged upon his auditors to be in readiness for the election so that they may have a fair representation upon the first Parish Council. The rev. gentleman who is well acquainted with village life believes that the bill will make great and beneficial changes in village life, and is heartily in sympathy with the Bill. lie views it in quite a different spirit to the lion. I'. W. Legh M.P. who,</p>
        <p>sLou. L E</p>
        <p>ery f rses</p>
