      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WILL.</head>
        <p>WILL.</p>
        <p>The will of the late Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart., of Walton Hall, is dated the 14th March, 1893, and appoints Lady Greenall, Robert Davies, John White, his son Sir Gilbert Greenall, and his two daughters, executors; and appoints Lady Greenall, Robert Davies, and John White, trustees of his estates. The Mansion House of Walton Hall with its grounds and parks is given to Lady Greenall for life, with an annuity of £B,OOO and an immediate legacy of £2,000. Portions of £lOO,OOO are provided for each of the daughter. Subject to the foregoing provisions, the estates in Cheshire and Lancashire, and all other the testator's freehold property are settled upon the present Baronet, and his issue in tail, and in default of such issue, upon the testator's daughters in succession, and their issue in tail. The testator gives five shares in Greenall, Whitley and Co., Limited to Lady Greenall, and one share to his three children. The remainder of his shares in Greenall, Whitley and Co. Limited, are given to his son, Sir Gilbert Greenall, absolutely. After bequests of certain legacies to old servants and others, the testator directs that the residue of his personal estate shall be held upon similar trusts to those declared of his freehold property. The net value of the personal estate is £1,018,375.</p>
