      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WIDNES STARVATION CASK</head>
        <p>THE WIDNES STARVATION CASK</p>
        <p>DEATH OF THE CHILD IN WHISTON WORKHOUSE. The police have reported the death at Whiston work- lsouse, near St. Helens, of a girl named Elizabeth Mercer, aged ten years, which tonic place under circumstances pointing to starvation. It appeared that on the 6th of July, Samuel Mercer, a labourer, of Widnes, his wife, Ann Mercer, and his five children, were admitted into the workhouse in a destitute condition. Elizabeth was in a very bad condition, and bad to go into the hospital under the care of Dr. Hall. At that time a summons WAS pending against the parents for wilfully neglecting Elizabeth, and the cases were heard at Widnes on the 32th of July. It was then stated that although Mercer WaB in constant employment, earning 190. per week, be had no home whatever, but appeared to sleep in differ- ent lodging-houses. All the troubles were due to drink. The child (Elizabeth) was in a most emaciated condition, and weighed only 371 b. instead of 501 b. 130 z., the stan- dard weight of a girl ten years old. Mercer was then committed for three months, and his wife for one month. Meanwhile, the child remained in the hospital, but did Riot make any improvement, and she died last week. At the Whiston workhouse on Monday, Mr. Samuel Brighouse, county coroner, and a jury. held an inquiry as to the death of the girl. Inspector WAteer repre- sented the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Chil- dren, and Mr. A. F. Mann watched the proceedings on behalf of the workhouse authorities; Superintendent Keighley appearing for the Widnes police.—The Coroner said that it seemed to him that, if after hearing the evi- dence of the medical man there seemed to be a prima fade case against the parents it would be far better to adjourn the inquiry until they were out of prison and could attend it.—Mary Jane Mercer,l, Nortia-st.Widnes, said her husband was uncle of the deceased girl. About six weeks before the parents were sent to prison the girl and the other four children were brought to her house. The girl Elizabeth Ann was never a healthy child. All the money that the mother could get was spent in food for the children. The deceased girl was taken to the workhouse hospital on the 6th of July.— Dr. O'Keeffe deposed that on the 21st June he was re- quested by the inspector for the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children to examine the de- ceased. He found her to be a mere living skeleton. The thighs and legs were absolutely without flesh, and 80 were the arms and forearms. The girl was also in an advanced stage of pulmonary consumption. The normal weight of a child ten years of age (which was the age given of the girl at the time) was 501bs. 130 z., but she only weighed 37Ibs. when he examined her. On Satur- day afternoon he made an examination of the body of the child. He found the lungs to be in a state of absolute putrefaction. The immediate cause of death was pul- monary consumption,and he had no hesitation in saying that the disease was brought about by starvation earned on systematically for over two years. When he saw the girl she was past all hope of recovery, however skilfully treated. Whilst she was in the workhouse she lost Vbs. —After hearing this evidence the Coroner said the mother of the girl would be out of prison on the 14th prox.,and an application in the meantime might be made to the Home Office asking that the father might be brought from prison to the inquiry.—The inquiry was then adjourned till the 14th of August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WIDNES FOOTBALL FIXTURES, 1894-5,</head>
        <p>WIDNES FOOTBALL FIXTURES, 1894-5,</p>
        <p>The Widnes Football Club have completed a capital fixture list for the coming season. They open at home with Runcorn, the enemy whom they have not yet managed to beat, and this is followed by a match with Wigan, also at Widnes. The following week they are due at Salford to meet the premier club of that town. After these three" trial matches "the club will for six Saturdays be engaged in the championship matches (seccnd division) followed by the return with ,Salford and their doughty antagonists from Warrington, against whom they generally play an excellent game. Leigh, who are now in the first division, will be encountered, and the follswing clubs will also try conclusions with Widnes I—Crompton, Werneth, Kendal Hornets, St. Helens, and Barton. The following is a full list of the ixtures : FIRST TEAM.</p>
        <p>" A " TEAM.</p>
        <p>- Rep. I Runcorn H Runcorn A 8 Wigan H Wigan A 15 Salford A Salford H 2E *Blackley Rangers A Widnes, St. Mary's H 29 *Broughton</p>
        <p>Oct. - 6 *Lancaster A Ditton Recreation H 13 *Mickley A Warrington St. Mary's H 20 *flochdale St.Clements.H Stand. South End A 27 *Barrow A Latchford H Nov. 3 Salford H Salford A 10 Warrington H Warrington Rangers A 17 Leigh A Leigh H 24 Crompton H Warring' on A Dec. 1 *Aspull A Aspull H 8 *l[llverston A Stand. South End H 15 Werneth A Sunlight H 22 Wigan A Wigan H 26 Kendal Hornets H 29 St. Helens H St. Helens A. Jan. 1 Warrington H 6 Leigh H Leigh rr t t/ti ? A 12 Werneth H Runcorn Rees . A 19 *Aspull H Aspull A i,-; nylverston li</p>
        <p>Feb. a Parton A 9 *Blackley Rangers H Ditton Recg A 16 *Walkden A Widnes Rovers H 23 *Lancaster, H Latchford A</p>
        <p>Mar. 2 Cfnmpton A Warrington Rangers M 9 Walkden H 16 *Rochdale St. Clements A Widnes St. Mary's H 23 *Barrow H Warrington St. Mary's A 30 *Broughton A Broughton H April 6 *Blackley H Sunligkt A 12 Barton H 13 Kendal Hornets A. Runcorn Recs H 15 Runcorn A Runcorn H 20 Warrington A 27 St. Helens A St. Helen A * Championship matches (second division).</p>
        <p>LOCAL BREACH OF PROMISE CASE.</p>
        <p>A SIX WEEKS' COURTSHIP AT PENKETH.</p>
        <p>-- --- On Wednesday, Mr. F. H. Mellor, barrister-at-law, and a jury at in the Sheriff's Jury Court, St. George's Hall, Liverpool, for the purpose of assess- ing damages in a case in which Ann Skelhorn, aged twenty-three, daughter of a florist., residing at renketh, near Warrington, sought substantial damages from James Edgerby, cabinetmaker, of Penketh. Mr. Horridge (instructed by Mr. A. Browne) was counsel for the plaintiff, and the defendant was not represented by counsel. Mr. Hor ridge said that the defendant was a master builder and cabinetmaker in a good way of business,who was living in his own house, and who had other houQe property, so that the match would have been in every way an advantageous one for plaintiff. The plaintiff had known him slightly for eighteen months. His wife died in May of last year, leaving him a widower with three young childten. On a Saturday evening in November last while plaintiff was travelling home on a bus from Warrington, the defendant came and sat beside her. Subsequently he came frequently to see her at her father's house, and plaintiff and her sister went on his invitation to have tea with him. During their six wreks' acquaintance she became very fond of him, and in addi- tion to paying for her £4 which she owed on a sewing machine, he gave her a wedding ring and a keeper, a small gold watch, and £2 103. for a wedding dress. Their wedding was fixed forlast Christmas Day, and the event was well known as a fixture in the neight3Aurhood In December, however, he suddenly and wiihiut warn- ing ceased to visit her, and when she called upon him to find out the cause he told her what had since been proved to be a deliberate falsehood, that he had got another girl into trouble, and must marry her. He had since marriee, not the girl referred to, but another one and for the breach of promise, substantial damages wer asked. Mr. Browne, the solicitor, stated that, in answer to the letter threatening proceedings if he did not make a substantial offer to plaintiff, defendant called at his (Mr. Browne's) office, and said that Mr. Browne might do what he liked, as he (defendant) had transferred his property to his present wife. Evidence was called to show that defendant was a man id means. The jury assessed the damages at £5O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Calcium Warrington were unable to keep up their form of the last two weeks, and again had to accept defeat on Sat- urday at the hands of Sale. The match in question was played in splendid weather, and as a result, the Sale ground was well attended, the number being the largest of the season, Warrington were minus S. M. and J. J. Crosfield, and R. Smith, so that they were materially weakened both in batting and bowling. The Warring- ton innings commenced most inauspiciously for two of their best bats, Bates and Mortimer were out in the first two °vers. Wordsworth and G.Clarke fared much better for they carried the score to 31 when Wordsworth came to grief. Clarke continued his innings in brilliant style and his wicket was not oaptured until he had contri- buted 38. The only other players who could cope with the bowling were F. Williamson and Clark, who each scored 14, the latter being not out. The total of the Warrington innings only realized 93, and Sale were able to score double this number and three more besides. The odd 87 of the Sale innings was made by R. Cookson, he going in first and being eighth out.</p>
        <p>Bates was the most successful of the Warrington bowlers his five wickets costing 63 runs, although Mortimer was able to take one for seven. The Warring- tonians had unfortunately to play against the Sale um- pire as well as the Sale men, for he appeared to have great difficulty at times in making up his mind. For instance Whittle was bowled after only two rums had been scored,but the umpire allowed him to keep inon the ground that he was not ready. Again at 3.9, R. Cook- son was bowled by Bates, and the umpire at first gave him "out," but he changed his mind again and Cookson was allowed to continue, the umpire agreeing with the batsman that the ball rebounded frem the wicket keeper's pads. This caused the game to be delayed for some minutes, but at length G. Clarke consented to continue, but the visitors did not play with much heart, and were very easily beaten. E. Connor did not come off as usual with the ball, but of course this cannot be expected every time.</p>
        <p>I notice that Connor has been chosen in the match between Lancashire second eleven and sixteen colts of the County, in which he will figure as a colt, along with his friend" Jimmy" Ross of Preston North End. The match will be played at the commencement of August, and every Warringtenian will wish Connor every suc- cess, and higher honours still for the county. •</p>
        <p>The Sankey Bridges Wealeyans had to submit to de- feat for the first time to the Liverpool WindsorWesleyans on Saturday'. They have always previously come out very strong against the Liverpool team, but on Satur- day they shaped very badly. The Liverpool bowlers had a good time of it at their expense, for only Taylor was able to make any headway against them, and he played very well for his 25. Excluding Taylor, Clare was the longest stayer, for he went in at 15 and came out at 34, his contribution, however, being only three The innings closed for 48, and it soon appeared. when the Windsor Wesleyans went in, that it was only a question as to whether they had time to secure the runs. The end of the game was very exciting, for the Liverpool team scored one run more than their oppe- nents just "on time," for the loss of eight wickets. The fielding of the Sankey Bridges team was very loose, and numerous chances were thrown away, but the Liverpool team fully deserved their victory ,which was the first they had gained this season.</p>
        <p>The Bewsey Wesleyans were successful over a selected team on Saturday by 48 to 29. It is very peculiar how the selected teams almost invariably get the worst of it. The St. Mary's also gained an easy victory over the San- key Wesleyans second by 61 to 39.</p>
        <p>A peculiar match was played at Howley on Saturday, between Cairo-st. Unity and Bank-st. Mission. Duck- worth for Bank-st. scored no less than 52 not out, and captured half the Unity wickets. Notwithstanding his high score, the Mission could only realise 85. On the other hand, White, Shaw, and "Extras " scored 36 out of a total of 43. It is not, therefore, surprising to find that eleven out of the 22 players obtained " ducks."</p>
        <p>Low scoring was the rule at the match between Fair- field and Bank Quay Mission, Mounfield scoring 12 out of 24 for Fairfield, and Wilcock with two being the highest scorer for the Mission, who could only manage nine. The match was a good one for the bowlers, who had excellent averages.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wire Mills team tasted the sweets of ! revenge on Saturday when they encountered Walkers' ' Brewery. The teams had met before this season, and on that occasion the brewerymen proved too good for the wiremen. The wiremen were accordingly looking for- ward to last Saturday to prove that the first match was a mistake. They did I,eat the Brewery but only by eight runs which however is much more satisfactory to the Sankey team than the result of the last match. There were two who obtained double figures on each side. Williams went right through the Wire Mill innings for 30, and Warburton scored half that number. Arber with 14 and E. Allen with 12 were the best on the oppos- ing side. T. Allen and Todd shared the Sankey wickets between them, and the Brewery were dismissed by Simpson, Marshall, Hobson, and Warburton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>The successes which the Warrington water polo team have achieved previous to the present week,led everyone to believe that a capital match was in store at St. Helens on Tuesday. The result of the match came as a rude shock to the supporters of the Warrington team, for a defeat of eight goals to two, was never anticipated. However, as this is the first season in which Warrington have_had regular fixtures, they must not expect to carry all before them. They have performed remarkably well up to the present, for such a young club, and must not be disheartened,by their defeat of Tuesday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>BASEBP.LL.</head>
        <p>BASEBP.LL.</p>
        <p>In-reply to " Cne Interested," I am glad to inform him that there is still a fJecretary to the Warrington ' baseball club. As " One Interested '' ba. cuch a fatherly interest in the club, I have made inquiries and have dis- covered that although it has not been so successful of late, its name stands fourth in the league, and the mem- bers have every reason to expect that they will finish third, which is not a position to be ashamed of. The table with the names, etc., of the clubs in the League cannot be supplied, because owing to a change in the secretaryship of the League, affairs have got slightly mixed, and there are no official tables extant. The matches have not been supplied to the" Exatniner " for various reasons, but not, I am assured, because of recent defeats. I think I have supplied " One, etc." with all he requires, and hope he will be satisfied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>The fixture for the Warrington Cycling Club on Saturday was an inter-club run to Delarnere with the Bowdon C.C. The Davenham Sports, however, took place on that day, and arrangements were made with the Bowdon Club by which the fixture was cancelled, and about 40 members proceeded to Davenhain to wit- ness the sports, and a most enjoyable run was the re- sult. Another run was made on Tuesday night to Bowdon. During the next two or three weeks thlhy members of the club will be on their liolidays,and will be far away from Warrington in Wales and various picturesque portions of the country.</p>
        <p>A correspondent writes :—Dear Will yell kindly allow a small space in your paper to open up the above subject of obtaining a cycle and running path for War- rington. I think it is time nor town d.d something in this direction and may I be allowed to suggest one thing and that is: Can the Warringtcn Cricket Club see their way clear to form a cinder path round their field which is an excellent one and very suitable for it? lam sure their efforts would be well responded to by racers and public alike and would well repay them. At present we are far behind other smaller towns who make their tracks pay. Perhaps some one with an abler pen than mine will take the matter up and make more sugges- tions.—Yours, &amp;C., ENTHUSIAST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>In the quarter mile obstacle race at the Leigh A.C. Sports, on Saturday, D. Gibson, of Warrington, obtained a second place, thc first prize being captured by T.Platt, Leigh A.C., who started off the same mark as the local runner.</p>
        <p>rrHE SUB-CAPTAIN of the Warrington Cycling Club.—Mr. TED ProcAvascE, writes May 2'3, 1891.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l have given your DRINKS lONE EMBROCA- TION an exhaustive trial and can safely recomcsend it as a good reliable article. A little of it rub oed int') the muscles after a long ride gives a wonderfully soothing effect ; while for a Sprain I know of nothing better. lam confident that when its mPrits are known amongst Cyclists, and Athletes generally it will command a great Sale. Yours truly, E. PICKAVANC E . _</p>
        <p>To be obi- tined only from the Proprietor, HENRY M. ASHTON, 63, Sankey-st., (opposite Legh-st.), Warrington.</p>
        <p>F 1? ODSIIAM.</p>
        <p>WORKPEOPLE'S Picsic.—On Friday afternoon of last week a number • f workmen employed by D. Steele, of Frodshatu, accompanied by their wives were entertained to tea by the doctor at Mersey View, Overton Hills. Dancing and other pastimes were participated in and extremely enjoyed. PAROCHIAL NURSE FUND.—The accounts of thiq fund which have just been published show that during the year 1893-4 the subscriptions and donations received amounted to £7O 6s. Id.' which, with the sum of £l6 18s. op., balance in hand &amp;c., made £B7 4s. lid. Of this total the following items were paid :—Salary to nurse, £7O ; nurse's account for supplies, 23. 2d.; ma- terials for soup,&amp;c., £2 16s. 9d. ; Mr. Dunsford's account £39s. 81. ; printing and pozitages, 183. 6d. ; towards keep of pony, £4 ; which leaves a balance in hand of £5 17s. Old. 'The rec, rd of the past year's work shows the number of patients as 318 ; number of visits paid, 3,082, which is much larger than heretokre,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY AUGUST 4, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY AUGUST 4, 1894.</p>
        <p>man's removal, maintenance, medical attention,and bur- ial. In answer to this the Commissioners received a letter from the Local Government Board written on the 17th May, enclosing for the information of the Commissioners a copy of a letter on the subject which the Board had received from the Guardians, and which has already appeared at length in the columns of the "Examiner."— The Health Committee replied to this letter on the 15th June as follows :—" . . . . The opening sentence of Mr. Cooke's statement as set forth in the letter of the Runcorn Guardians contains the substance of the whole matter in dispute, viz.: ' That infectious cases which were not paupers at the time of being reported to him were not to be made such for the purpose of throwing certain liabilities on the Board of Guardians which ought to be borne by the Board of Commissioners.' The Guar- dians' contention is that if a person is suffering from an infectious disease and makes application to their reliev- ing officer for a medical order that irrespective of the person's needs or inability to pay, their officer is justified in declining to grant such application (unless the applicant already appears on his books as a pauper) and is entitled to refer the applicant to this authority for admission to our hospital ,and the medical attendance which naturally follows the admission of patients into that institution My Committee consider that if your lordships will be good enough to give an authoritative opinion as to whether the Guardians' intrepretation of their duties, as mentioned in.the above paragraph, is correct,and one to be maintained, it will remove a matter which has caused considerable friction between the two authorities. It will not be understood that we accept the version given by the Guardians as representing the facts of the case; but we do recognize the futility of attempting to recon- cile conflicting statements which depend entirely upon the individual ideas of what constitutes destitution held by the officers of the authorities concerned."—Continu- ing, Mr. Imison remarked that in reply to the last letter they received one from the Local Government Board enclosing a copy of the letter sent to the Guardians on the 26th January 1893, and which appeared in the above minutes. From that letter it would be seen that the relieving officer's idea that he was entitled to make a distinction between applications from persons suffering from infectious disease, and per- sons suffering from other causes which were not paupers, and the Guardians' instructions that infectious cases which were not paupers at the time of being reported to the relieving officer for removal were not to be made such for the purpose of throwing certain liabilities upon the Guardians which ought to he borne by the Commis- sioners, was an idea and an instruction which could not be maintained. The letter also clearly stated that it was the relieving officer's duty in the interval to give an , order for the admission of a destitute patient into a hospital. The agreement, which they would consider later on, stipulated for the receipt of notice from the Guardians in the cases which had occurred previonsly. Where the relieving officer had acted upon his own responsibility—and from this letter he was clearly entitled to do so—the Guardians had repudiated the action of their own officer, and had declined to pay the Commissioners for costs incurred on behalf of patients admitted into the hospital at his request. The agreement which the committee asked the Board to terminate on the ground, among others,that the prices arranged were inadequate to cover the cost of maintenance and nursing of patients when there were only a few. The first payment agreed upon was ss. for the removal of the sufferer. They could not get this work done for less than 15s. The second payment was one of 21s. per week for maintenance and nursing of each patient detained. When he told them that nursing alone usually costs them 30s. per week, exclusive of patients maintenance and other inevitable expenses, they would readily see that a fresh arrangement was absolutely ne- cessary if they were to get anything like their own money back. Mr. Imison went on to compare the cost of stamping out the epidemic of smallpox in the Runcorn Rural Sanitary district, and the Commissioners district, and showed that whereas it cost the Sanitary Authority £l3 is. sd. per head on the average, it had cost the Commissioners £3 13s. si. lie would not say that the respective sums covered the same items of expense, but it gave unmistakable evidence of the economical working of the Commissioners hospital. He concluded by hoping that the friction which had prevailed between the Guar- dians and Commissioners would cease, and that an amicable arrangement would be come to in the near future. He had pleasure in moving the adoption of the minutes.—Mr. Bisbrown seconded and after Messrs. Lightburn and Meadowaroft had spoken at some length on the subject, the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTER.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. J. W. Lightburn the reports of this committee were approved, and also recommenda- tions that £lO be expended in ventilating the Board- room.</p>
        <p>THE BATHS QUESTION AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collier's name was Clown on the agenda paper to move the following resolution of which he gave notice at the last meeting—" That a poll of the town be taken to I ascertain the decision of the ratepayers as to the adop- tion of cne of the following schemes for baths. The enclos- ing of the pond on the Common adjoining the Runcorn waterworks or the provision of first-class baths in a position as central as possible in the town." Mr. Collier now stated that since the notice had been placed upon the agenda paper he had been advised that such could not be done. He begged to withdraw the notice and expressed his intention of resorting to other means to carry out his object.—This was accordingly agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>RUNCORN IMPROVEMENT COMMISSIONERS. HOW PUBLIC AUTHORITIES SHOULD DEAL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN IMPROVEMENT COMMIS- SIONERS. HOW PUBLIC AUTHORITIES SHOULD DEAL</p>
        <p>WITH INFECTIOUS DISEASES.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held on Thursday morning in the Town Hall, Runcorn, Mr. S. Taylor presiding. There were also present—Dr Mangan, Messrs. J. Meadowcroft, W. Collier, J. W. Lightburn, H. J. Once' Wm. Hankinson, R. Abel, D. Bisbrown, Wm. Leach, F. A. Lake, law clerk; E. Marshall, secretary; and M.Barker, surveyor. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT. . . .</p>
        <p>Dr. McDougall, Medical officer of health reported that during the month just ended, twenty-five deaths had been registered from various causes making a death-rate per thousand of the estimated population of fifteen. The number of deaths registered in the corresponding period of last year was fifty-eight.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE. The recommendations of this committee were that 50 tons of limestone chippings be purchased from Messrs. Kneeshaw, Lupton and Co. at 7s. 9d. per ton, delivered; that Messrs. E. Timmins and Sons prices for man hole frames, covers, and grids be accepted; that a brake be put on the iron watering cart ; that a urinal be erected at the south end of Mereey-st. at an estimated cost of £45, and that application be made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to a loan for that amount for 30 years. The Committee reported that the estimated cost of constructing a footbridge across the Bridgewater Canal at the Halton Road Crane was £150; recommended the Board to construct a footbridge at the place indi- cated, the Board to pay two-thirds and the promoters one-third of such expense, the iron girder bridge which the promoters propose delivering at the aforesaid site ; being taken as representing the contribution to be madel by the promoters. It is further recommended that ap- plication be made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to a loan ef £lOO for 60 years. That the following plans be approved :—Mr. J. Bell and Mr. Peers for the erection of two sculleries at the rear of two cot- tages in Union-st. ; Mr. J. Williamson for the erection cf a small scullery at the rear of house situated in Irwell ' Lane; the managers of the Runcorn National Schools for the erection of two class rooms and a cloak room. Mr. D. Bisbrown in moving the adoption of the minutes, said that amongst the matters of importance, in the minutes was the provision of a feotbridge in Halton-road. The subject had been before the public for a number of years, and had been an election cry on many occasions. The committee which had been ap- pointed to deal with the matter had been beset with the difficulty that before the bridge could be erected, the property owners would have to give a contribution to- wards the construction. This difficulty had now,he was pleased to say, been surmounted, inasmuch as they had provided the bridge, and it was for the Commissioners to put it in position. He hoped that recommendation would meet with the Board's approval.—The Clerk drew Mr. Bisbrown's attention to the fact that as there were only ten members present, the minute referring to the bridge could not be adopted, as it required a majority of two-thirds of the Board in favour of it. They were short of two members to make up the number required. He asked the mover to withdraw that particular minute from his proposition for the time being.—Mr. Bisbrown consented.—Mr. Grice seconded, and the minutes as amended were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Committee recommended that four 6ft. 9in. bed- steads be purchased for the Hospital ; that the Nuisance Inspector be instructed to take proceedinis and appear at prosecutions on behalf of the AuthDrity under section 253 of the Public Health Act, 1875, and the Town Police Clauses Act, 1847, in cases of firing or allowing chimney to catch lire ; that the tender of the Municipal Appli- ances Co. for the supply of a 1000 gallon flushing van for the sum of £6O delivered be accepted, and that application be made to the Local Government Board to sanction a loan of the aforesaid amount for 30 years. The committee reported that a letter had been received from the Local Government- Board in reply to an en- quiry of the committee as to the interpretation of the duties of the Guardians of the Runcorn Union in cases of infectious disease, enclosing an extract from a letter I addressed to the Guardians, of which the following was</p>
        <p>_ a copy :—Extract from the Local Government Bard's letter to the Guardians of the Runcorn Union on the 26th</p>
        <p>January, 1893. "The Board direct me to stete that, where a person suffering from illness, including infecti- ous disease, is destitute, it is the duty of the Guardians, or in the intervals between their meetings, of the reliev- ing officer, to give such relief as the case may require, and if necessary to give an order for the admission of the patient to a hospital in which he can be properly treated. Where, however, the removal of the patient to a hospital is only required for the purpose of isolation. and the person to be removed is not destitute,the Guard- ans have no duty in the case,and the necessary provision should be made by the Sanitary Authority of the dis- trict. The test of the Guardians' duty in the matter is the destitution of the patient, and this will not neces- sarily depend upon his being in the actual receipt of poor relief, but may consist in his being unable to ob- tain at his own cost the requisite medical attention, nursing and accommodation. Where it devolves upon Guardians to deal with eases of infectious disease which require hospital accommodation, they are not restricted to providing for the treatment of such cases in the workhouse. On the contrary, the Board think that the hospital accommodation required in any locality should be provided by the Sanitary Authority,who should agree with the Guardians to receive their pauper infectious cases."—The committee recommend that three months notice be given to terminate the agreement made on the '3rd July, 1882, between the Commissioners and the Guardians of the Poor of the Runcorn Union for the reception of pauper patients into the hospital. Mr. Irnison moved the confirmation of the minutes saying that the Board would remember that in April last it was resolved that full particulars of Curzon's case should be referred to the Local Government Board, ane that they be asked to say whether it was not a case in which the Guardians ought to have made good the ex- penses incurred by the Commissioners on behalf of the Man in the hospital. The above letter was really a continua- tion of a correspondence which had been going on since April 1893, and which he intended to read in order that they might have all the particulars fresh in their mind. Mr. Liaison then read the following letters :- 12th April, 1893.—My Lords and Gentlemen,—l am directed by my Health Committee to ask if you will be good enough to say whether it is the duty of this Auth- ority to maintain destitute persons in their hospittil whilst suffering from infectious disease. The Guardian: of the poor for the Runcorn Union are under agreetnere with this Board to pay for the treatment of pauper case. in such hospital, but they do not see their way any longer to pay for the maintenance of persons, who prev ious 'to being received into the hospital were not in receipt of parish relief. It is the opinion of my Commit- tee that when persons are found to be suffering from any infectious disease, and are quite destitute if is the duty of the Guardians to make good the cost incurred in treating and maintainine such persons in the hospital. Will your lordships be good enough to say if their view is correct in this respect.—E. Marshall, secretary to the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners,: Replying on the 26th April the Local Government Board said that" although the hospital referred to is provided for the use of the inhabitants of the district,any particu- lar inhabitant is not empowered to claim admission to it as of right. The management of the hospital is vested in the Improvement Commissioners, and the Board think it would be competent to the Commissioners to refuse to admit pauper patients into the hospital, except unct ran arraneetnent with the Guardians providing for the cost of the maintenance of the patient in the hospital. Upon the general questiou as to the authority by whom the cost of maintenance should be defrayed, I am to observe thee in the opinion of the Board, where a person is not .destitute, and his removal to the hospital is required for the purpose of isolation' the Sanitary Authority, and not the Guardians should deal with the case under the powers conferred upon them by the Public Health Act, 1875. If however, a person attacked by infectioes disease he deatittite, either wholly, or in part of the means of obtaining all such necessaeies (including medical attendance, nursing and aceommoda- im) it seems to the Board that it is the duty of the Guardians to give the relief that is :edd. That letter was followed by ore from the Commissioners on the 28th November saying that the latter had pressed the Guardians for payment of the account £B3 which had been rendered but which the Guardians had persist- ently refused to pay. The letter was accompanied by a resolution passed by the Guardians referring to the account, and which ran as follows :—" That taking into consideration that it does not appear to be the practice in other Unions to pay for patients taken into an infect- ious diseases hospital, and that the removal thereto of such patients is for the purpose of stamping out disease and the general good of the district within which it has broken out, and that in the cases charged for the pati- ents were not taken into the hoseet il at the request of the Guardians, or under an order of justices, the Guar- dians feel they would not be justified in paying the account."—The letter concluded by the Health *Commit- tee of the Commissioners asking for an inquiry to be instituted by the Local Government Board inte the nptt- ter in order that it might be definitely settled as to wlti k Authority was to hear the expenses referred to In reply, the Local Government Board wrote on the 7th December, saying that " it would be competent to the Runcorn Commissioners to refuse to admit pauper pa- tients into their hospital except under an arrangement with the Guardians providing i for the cost of the mainten- ance, &amp;c., of the patient n hospital, but if no such arrangement has been entered into, and pauper patients have nevertheless been received, it does not seem to the Board that any liability attaches to the Guardians. The Board suggest,as regards the futtne, that an agreement should be entered into ferthwith, einhodyirg such an arrangement." On the lith April. 1894, the Health Committee again wrote to the Local Government hoard with reterence to a man named James Cut zon. who was rerneved a short time before to the hospital. This letter contained the Commissioners Sanitary Inspector's report upon the case, a text of which has been given before in these columns, and coneeided by asking the Local Government Board if in their oninion it was not a case tech the relieving officereheuld have dealt with,and also as to whether the Guardians ought not to pay for the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>RE-APPOINTMENT OF THE NUISANCE INSPECTOR.</head>
        <p>RE-APPOINTMENT OF THE NUISANCE INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that the sanction of the Local Government Board to the re-appointment of Mr. James Percival as Sanitary Inspector had come to hand.</p>
        <p>THE HALTON-ROAD FOOTBRIDGE.</p>
        <p>In consequence of a sufficient number Of members not having assembled at the close of the meeting, the recom- mendation contained in the Buildings and Highways Committee's minutes having reference to the proposed bridge over the Bridgewater canal in Halton-road, the matter was left over either for the next ordigary meet- ing or a special meeting of the Board, whichever the committee thought fit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The annual flower service in connection with the Bold- street Sunday School, was held in the chapel on Sunday atfernoon. The service was conducted by the Rev.G.D. Mason who took as the basis of his remarks " Coasider the lilies of the field how they grow." Special hymns were given by the chair, and Miss Wheatley made a dis- tinctly favourable impression by a solo which she rendered. During the service, the scholars presented flowers which were arranged artistically round the pulpit and contained the text " God is love." A collec- tion was made on behalf of Dr. Stevenson's home which amounted to nearly £4. The evening service was con- ducted by Mr.W. C. Nicholson when Miss IVheatley and Miss Bennett contributed a solo, and the choir sang Stainer's well known anthem " What are these which are arrayed in white robes ?" sr. JAMES' CHURCH, LATCHFORD. Sunday, was the day in the octave of the feast of the patron saint of St. James' Church, and special services were held. Four services were conducted in the morn- ing, when the preacher at the second celebration was the Rev. F. W. Willis, M.A., rector of Warrington. At a flower service in the afternoon an address was given by the Vicar. The flowers were subscqoently sent to the Infirmary, a number being also distributed among the sick of the parish. There was a large congregation in the evening, when the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. P. H. Leary, M.A., vicar of St. Catherine's, Aber- cromby-square, Liverpool,whose eloquent discourse was attentively listened to. Mr. F. Booth presided at the organ, and a band accompaniment, under the superin- tendence of Mr. T. Armstrong, was also given. The ()fret tories will be devoted to church expenses. BUCKLEY STREET ME I'HODIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Special services were held at the Chapel on Sunday when the pulpit in the afternoon was occupied by Mr. Thomas Morris, F.G.S., who has recently returned from a trip to Belgium, where, he said, he had been travelling amongst the ironworkers to ascertain the number of hours they worked and other particulars. He said that the Sunday there was a curse instead of a blessing. The men worked 12 hours every day, and the "night-turn" man had to work until six o'clock on Sunday morning every week of his life. There was hardly a place of wor- ship to be.seen in many places; in Jae district he visited the only religious building was a Roman Catholic Church The men had no time to attend a place Of worship. It seemed to him to be a case of " weary week in, weary week out."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>INGENIOUS DAIRYMAN.</head>
        <p>THE INGENIOUS DAIRYMAN.</p>
        <p>BOW MILK IS ADULTERATED.</p>
        <p>Dairymen who are in the habit of diluting their milk with water have found out an ingenious plan of circum- vening the public analysts. Under the Act of Parlia- ment they can request that their milk shall be analysed by the chemists at Somerset House, and this they now do. Their object is to gain a little time, for milk quickly begins to ferment, and it is not possible to test with accuracy a decomposed fluid, and say what it con- Listed of before decomposition set in. Dr. Wynter Blyth instances a recent case of a dealer who sold milk diluted with at least 8 per cent, of water. He appealed to Somerset Hou,,e, and after a little delay Somerset House declared that there was no evidence of the addition of watAr,so that the case was dismissed. That he had nevertheless made no mistake in the matter Dr. Blyth is certain, as Vie milk had been subjected to an independent analysis by Mr. Colwell, who agreed with him. The only way out of the difficulty would be for each sanitary' authority to have a freezing chamber, in which reference samples of milk, &amp;c., could be frozen and preserved. Such a chamber would also be found useful for preserving meat supposed to be diseased until the evidence on both sides could be heard by a court of •justice.</p>
        <p>SOARING RAIN PRoOF OVERCOATS, yet porous, 556., C. JACKSON HOLMEQ, Gel era! Outfitter, blarket Qate, Warring- tut. .—Acirt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>------ - In the House of Commons on Monday evening Mr. MORLEY, answering a question, said that Lord CLANRI- CARDE had had removed 29 of the huts erected for his evicted tenants, and the estate bailiff appeared to have warned some of the tenants not to give shelter to evicted tenants.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH, replying to Mr. BowLEs, said the pro- posal to number cycles with a view to their more easy identification in cases of accident was worthy of COR- sideration, but from the growing number of this class of vehicles and the rapidity with which they changed hands the question was surrounded with practical diffi- culties.</p>
        <p>The House went into Committee of Supply on the Civil Service and Revenue Departments estimates, and considered a further vote on account. Mr. BARTLEY moved to reduce the Salary of the first Lord of the Trea- sury, for the purpose of calling into question his Lord- ship's exercise of patronage in the matter of the pension granted to Mr. RHYS-DAVIDS, the Oriental scholar, the hon. member declaring that the grant was a political job. Sir. J. T. HIBBERT vindicated the action of the PREMIER and on a division Mr. BARTLEY'S motion was rejected by 156 to 73. A reference to the administration of the Fac- tories and Workshops Acts led, Mr. ASCTITH to express regret that over a large portion of the country local authorities were very remiss in putting the Workshops Act hat° force, and to hint at the probability of the powers entrusted to them being taken away.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, the third reading of the Budget Bill being moved, the Marquis of SALISBURY repeated his contention that the long abstention of the House from interference with Finance Bills did not in any way viti- ate or weaken their constitutional right to interfere, and argued that the moral force of the third reading in the House of Commons was not sufficient to command re- spect as an expression of the national will, as the bill was only passed by a majority of 14. In that majority were six members who were only returned by aggregate majorities amounting to 150, and he held that the moral authority of those 150 householders could not exceed the moral authority of 150 members of the House of Lords who might think that the Budget Bill was not worthy of their support. His Lordship, in conclusion, protested against the principle of graduation. The LORD CHAN- CELLOR said the principle of graduation was introduced by .Sir STAFFORD NORTHCOTE with reference to the house duty. The bill was then read a third time and passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening. Sir Wm. j FlancouaT moved his resolution for the closure at speci- fied intervals at the debates on the remaining stages of the Evicted Tenants (Ireland) Bill. Mr. BALFOUR pro- tested against the House being gagged. The Govern- ment by the course they were taking rAade the fate of the bill when it reached the House of Lords almost certain. Mr. JOHN MORLEY said Mr. BALFOUR could not get over the fact that a similar measure of closure was applied to the bill constituting the Parnell Commission. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said the House was called on to closure the bill simply in order that the holiday of the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER and his friends might not be shortened. It would not be for the dignity of the Opposition to continue the discussion through the Com- mittee stage or the report stage, and they would accordingly leave it entirely to the Government and the Irish members. Mr. LABOUCHERE said when they were told that the House of Lords would throw out this bill it was an utter waste of time to discuss it. He hoped the Liberal party would go as speedily as possible for the abolition of the House of Lords. Mr. COURTNEY said it was urgently necessary to deal in some way with the question of the evicted tenants. He asked whether it was not possible for the Government to withdraw their closure resolution and see if both sides could agree that in a reasonable time the hill should pass through all its stages. Sir WM. Beam= said he still hoped an arrangement might be made for fair discussion. Mr. BALFOUR declared that the Opposition had not desired to discuss the bill at any unreasonable length. Mr. T. W. RUSSELL said if the bill was lost it would be because of the extremists on both sides of the question. On a division Mr. BALFOUR'S amendment was rejected by 217 to 174. and the closure resolution was then passed with- out a division.</p>
        <p>In the House of L9rds the Royal assent was given to 33 bills which had passed both Houses—including the Parochial Electors (Registration Acceleration) Bill—and the Bill for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children was read a third time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House or Commons on Wednesday the formal I resolution relating to the guarantee and expenses under the Evicted Tenants Bill was agreed to. The House then went into Committee on the Bill. At this time there was not a member of the Opposition on the front Bench. Two amendments moved by Captain NAYLOR-LEYLAND and Mr. DARLING were moved out of order. The Chair- man (Mr. MELLOR) called on Mr. CansoN and other Opposition members to move the amendments standing in their names, but there was no response except from Mr. BANBURY, who said, amid laughter, "I do not move." An amendment standing in Mr. DANE'S name was moved by Mr. MORLEY and agreed to. At least a dozen other amendments were passed over amid laugh- ter, the Conservative and Unionist members who had put them on the paper being absent or refraining from moving them. Mr. SEXTON, for Mr. KILBRIDE, moved an amendment to give the arbitrators power to reinstate in cases of farms that had been tenanted since the date of eviction, as well as unoccupied farms. Mr. MORLEY did not consider the amendment necessary, for he ex- pected that if a new tenant was not a bona-fide farmer and had no substantial interest in his holding he would be willing to go out on the terms set forth in the bill. Believing the outcome of the amendment would be an evicting campaign, he felt bound to oppose it. Mr. DILLow, in supporting the amendment, hoped some plan would be devised that would give the arbitrators power to decide whether the new tenant was a bona-fide one. After further discussion the committee divided, and the amendment was rejected by 128 to 57 votes; majority, 71. Clauses 1 and 2 were, after discussion, added to the bill. Progress was reported after a num- ber of amendments to clause 4 had been dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday, the West Lan- cashire Railway Bill and the Manchester Ship Canal Bill were read a third time.</p>
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH admitted, in reply to Dr. KENNY, that there had recently been an increase in the number of registered lunatics in England and Wales. Sir EDWARD GREY, replying to Mr. STUART WORTLEY, said the Government had no confirmation of the report that JABEZ BALFOUR was on his way to this country. In reply to questions, the CHANCELLOR of the Ex- CHEQUER said it was hoped that the House would by Tuesday next have finished the Evicted Tenants Bill and committee stage of the Equalisation of Rates Bill, and the Local Government (Scotland) Bill would then be taken. Sir EDWARD GREY informed Sir E. ASHMEAD-BARTLETT that war had been declared between China aud Japan, but that the Government had received no information as to any military or naval operations except as to the encounter in which the Kow Shing was sunk. The Kow Shing was a British vessel. The Japanese Government had informed her Majesty's Government spontaneously that they were ready to make full reparation if their officers were in the wrong. The House then went into Committee on [the Evicted Tenants Bill. The Conservative and Liberal Unionist benches were again hlmost vacant. After some discus- sion on an amen dment to clause 4, proposed by Mr. CLANCY, to give the arbitrators power to award a grant to the evicted tenants for the purpose of sowing or stock- ing the land, Mr. Morley who at first opposed, yielded to the expression of opinion which had come from different quarters of the House, and promised to reconsider the matter before report. Clauses 4 and 5 were then agreed to. After further discussion the bill as amended was ordered to be reported to the House. The report stage was fixed for Monday. The House then proceeded to consider the Building Societies (No. 2) Bill as amended by the Standing Com- mittee. A number of further amendments were proposed and discussed, and several of these were accepted by the Government. In the course of the discussion on clause 2, Mr. BILLSON said he heartily approved of the clause as a whole, and thought the sections of the clause which required the number of mortgages were necessarily good. He was quite sure that if a law to that effect had been in operation when the Liberator Scciety was pursuing its business, it would have exposed at once the character of the business they were doing.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords, Lord COLERIDGE took the oath and subscribed the roll on his succession to the peerage. Two bills were advanced a stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD MAN DROWNED.</p>
        <p>John Edward Iyalker, a single young man. 22 years of age, belonging to Latchford, was drowned in the Ship Canal at Weston Point On Wednesday night. The deceased, who was mate on the Enterprise, of which deceased's brother is captain, was coming with his boat from Liverpoel to Manchester, and on reaching the Wes- ton Point Locks he put a fender down the side, when the boat caught the wall of the waterway, and he was thrown overboard between the wall and the boat. It was found impossible to rescue him. The body was reCovered early on Thursday morning, and lies at Weston Point awaiting the inquest. THE MERSEY WATERMEN AND THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday representatives of the Upper Mersey Watermen's Association waited upon the officials of the Manchester Ship Canal Company with reference to the question in dispute between the company and their watermen. The matter was gone into thoroughly, and the result is that an amicable settlement satisfac- tory to th.. men has been arranged. Thus a strike has been averted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>CHINA AND JAPAN.</p>
        <p>DEFEAT OF THE JAPANESE.</p>
        <p>A" fimes " second edition telegram, dated Shanghai, Wednesday, says :—According to authentic reports re- ceived from Foreign Office sources at Seoul, the Chinese General, Yeh, last Sunday defeated the Japanese forces near Asan. The Japanese have withdrawn from Seoul. An army of Chinese troops 20,000 strong has crossed the Corean frontier from Manchuria.</p>
        <p>The Press Association is officially informed upon the highest authority that 11130 n the occasion of the attack by the Japanese troops upon the Chinese at Yashan, on the 27th and 28th July,the Japanese were repulsed with the loss of over 2,000. It is understood that China has accepted the challenge received. from Japan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SINKING A TRANSPORi.</head>
        <p>SINKING A TRANSPORi.</p>
        <p>The Japanese Government has published a summary of depositions made by the captain and chief officer (English) of the Bow Shing, the transport which was torpedoed by a Japanese gunboat. The account differs in some respects from those published previously with regard to the behaviour of the Chinese officers and soldiers who were on the ship, and it states that the cap- tain and the chief officer were fired upon whilst they were in, the water by the Chinese soldiew, who were ap- parently enraged because the officers, knowing that the Japanese ship was about to open fire leapt overboard to escape being involved in the general destruction. It seems that the Japanese officer twice summoned the Euro- peans, by signal, to " quit the ship immediately." The Chinese officers refused to permit this, and the Japanese ship approached within short range,discharged a torpedo and fired upon the crowded transport with her cannon. The captain and other officers then sprang overboard, and were picked Hp by one of the Japanese cruisers' boat after being fired at by the Chinese in the sinking ship. It is stated that energetic representations have been made to the Japanese Government.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>NEW RUNCORN REGISTER.</head>
        <p>THE NEW RUNCORN REGISTER.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the overseers published the new lists of voters for Runcorn, and as they are for the first time made out in wards, in accordance with the proposal at the recent County Council inquiry, they will no doubt be eagerly examined. As will be remembered the town was propmed to be divided into six wards, named respectively " Halton," " Mer- sey." " Central " and" Bridgewater," in Runcorn North Polling District, and " Newtown" and "Heath" in Runcorn South Polling District. The number of voters' names appearing on each ward roll, including the new ownership claims as separate parochial electors, is Halton Ward Mersey Ward 598 Central Ward 904 Bridgewater Ward 658 Newtown Ward 649 Heath Ward 425</p>
        <p>Total 3633 This number is remarkably near that approximately submitted at the recent inquiry, namely— Halton Ward Mersey Ward Central Ward 894 Bridgewater Ward Newtown Ward Heath Ward</p>
        <p>Total</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ANNUAL " TRIP " OF THE DUTTON WORKHOUSE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL " TRIP " OF THE DUTTON WORKHOUSE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The children of the Dutton workhouse, to the number of 56, accompanied by the master, matron, and teachers of the institution, had their sixth an- nual excursion to the se aside on Wednesday, Ethyl being chosen as the place of resort this year. The morn- ing opened v ery gloomy and wet, but this in nowise damped the spirits of the youngsters,who looked forward to the outing with the keenest interest; and were up and astir brig ht and early, preparing for the journey. With the exception of a slight wetting on the way to Sutton Weaver Station in the morn- ing, the children suffered no further hurt from the weather ; as when the terminus was reached about 11 a.m., the sun shone out gloriously, and the remainder of the day was beautifully fine. On the outward journey each child was supplied with a large meat pie to regale them by the way. The time previous to dining was spent on the sands in various , amusements. Dinner was served about 1 p.m. and con- sisted of a bountiful supply of roast beef, potatoes and cabbage followed by fruit tarts. In the afternoon the children witnessed a variety entertainment given by the minstrels on the sands. Then by the kindness of the Pier Company, they had free admission to the pier and after enjoying the sea-breeze for a while were then taken to tea; which included bread and butter, scones and buns. The return • journey was commenced at 7.15 p.m. and at 9.30 Sutton Weaver was reached. All arrived safely at the Workhouse about 10 o'clock p.m after a most thorough .day's enjoyment. The trip is organised by the master of the house, Mr. J. Cunnab,to whom much praise is due in keeping up the treat from year to year. The money is subscribed by ex-officio guardians and a few friends. The railway arrangements were excellent and reflect much credit on the Company's officials. The catering of Miss Griffiths, Gladstone Buildings. Rhyl, deserves commendation and gave every satisfaction. Accompanying the trip were Messrs. Williams and Heywood, guardians for Frodsham, Dr. Selby, Medical Officer of the Union House, and several friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY RAINFALL IN LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY RAINFALL IN LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Father Sidgreaves , of the Stonyhurst Observatory, re- ports that between ten and twelve o'clock no Thursday morning nine-tenths of an inch of rain fell, which is an unprecedented fall for so short a time in Lancashire. The downpour occurred simultaneously with sudden darkness, which created .alarm, while the rivers rose rapidly, and many floods occurred in various parts of East Lancashire, much damage being done to the crops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RETURN OF THE MILITIAMEN.</p>
        <p>The Militia Battalion 2rd South Lincashire Regi- ment, numbering about 800, returned to Orford Barracks on Saturday afternoon after undergoing 28 days training in camp at Chipping. They were headed by the drum and fife and brass bands of the regi- ment, and their progress through the streets was watched by a large number of people. The men appeared in excellent condition, and their conduct has been very good during the training.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>P.S. A. AT THE Y. M .C.A.</head>
        <p>P.S. A. AT THE Y. M .C.A.</p>
        <p>The chair at the meeting on Sunday was occupied by Mr. Wm. Roberts. The audience was favoured with two addresses, the first from Mr. Dereden, of South Shields, who gave an interesting address upon "Real brotherliness." Mr. James Evans also delivered and address upon " Sowing and reaping," which was also very instructive. Miss Gallop sang the two solos, " Contrite heart," and " Trust and obey," in a charming manner. Mr. Prank Hickman ably accompanied on the piano. There was a largely increased attendance at the meeting,which was no doubt due to the public meeting held at Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel on the previous week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EXPLOSION AT CENTRAL STATION.</head>
        <p>EXPLOSION AT CENTRAL STATION.</p>
        <p>An explosion of gas occured at the Central Station, on Saturday afternoon, by which three men named respectively Edward Spendelow, 22 years of age, Joseph Watson, 18 years of age, and William Wright, employed by the Cheshire Lines Railway, were severely injured. They were looking for a leakage in the gas meter belonging to the station when one of them, who was carrying the candle, came near to where the gas was escaping. A: sudden explosion was the result, and the men's faces and hands were severely burnt. They were taken to the Infirmary and their injuries attended to, after which they were able to proceed home. PICNIC TO LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>.0n Saturday the members of the Sankey Bridges Cricket team and other friends journeyed to Liver- pool, in order to play the return match with the Windsor Wesleyan cricket club. About 30 left War- rington by the 1.50 p.m. train, and when they arrived at Liverpool, a 'bus was in waiting to take them to the field, which is in Wavertree. after the match which resulted in a win for the Liverpudli- ans, was over, the party adjourned to the Wesleyan mission in Aigburth-street, where a substantial tea was provided, to which ample justice was done. After the room had been cleared, a social evening was spent. Mr. Bradburn of Liverpool occupying the chair. The chairman in his opening address, alluded to the good feeling which existed between the two teams,and hoped that that would long con- tinue. Mr. W. Brough on behalf of the Sankey IBridges team reciprocated those remarks, the two gentlemen wishing each team every success. A miscellaneous programme was gone through during Iwhich songs were given by Miss Bradburn, and Messrs. Peers, Jefferson, and Rimmer, and a recita- tion entitled "The Amen Corner," by Mr. C.George, The meeting appropriately closed by the sing- ing of the hymn "God be with you till we meet again," after which the Warringtonians rode to the station by special 'bus, and arrived safely at Warrington after a very pleasant excursion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>SCENE IN A THEATRE.</head>
        <p>SCENE IN A THEATRE.</p>
        <p>It is difficult to determine whether the extraordinary incident in the Theatre Royal, Glasgow, when one of the audience rushed upon the stage to protect the heroine during the performance of "La Dame aus Camelias" was an unconscious compliment to Dumas or to Mdme. Sarah Bernhardt. The incident is not wi.th- out parallel, for there is a well-known st,ry of a sailor who was so affected by a piece which he was witness- ing that he let himself down from the gallery by a rope, and, storming the stage, rushed to the rescue of suffer- ing innocence. He knocked the villain down amid the cheers of his comradee,and was only appeased when the manager appeared and hurriedly endowed the heroine with a large fortune in " property " bank notes. Pro- bably the earliest story of the sort is that of Don Quixote and the puppets,which Cervantes is said to have borrowed from real life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WARRINGTON KNIFE WOUNDINe.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON KNIFE WOUNDINe.</p>
        <p>CASE. TRIAL AT LIVERPOOL ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes, on Friday morning, beforo Mr. Justice Henn Collins, Thomas Baguley, 23 Yeari of age, a labourer living at Warrington, was indicted fer having, on the 2nd June, unlawfully wounded Patrick Corfield, a labourer, with intent to do him some grievous bodily harm. Mr. CollingwooKl Hope (instructed by Mr. J. Lyon Whittle. town , clerk of Warrington) appeared for the prosecution. ' The prisoner, who was undefended, pleaded not guilty. From the opening statement of counsel it appeared that on the 2nd June the prosecutor went to the Vine Tavern at Warrington sometime in the afternoon. The prisoner who was in the same house, come up to him and at- tempted" to pick a quarrel." However, nothing came of it in the public-house, but at seven o'clock the same evening the prosecutor was standing outside his house' door when the prisoner went up to him and said some- thing, but what it was he (Mr. Hope) was not in a Posi- tion to say, because the prosecutor did not catch the words. However, the prosecutor asked, " Did I ever do anything wrong to you or your mother ?" whereupon the prisoner, it was alleged, struck Corfield with a kuife, seriously injuring his left eye, and rendering his imme- diate removal to the Infirmary necessary. Several wit- nesses were called, and the prisoner was found guilty, and committed to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-08-04_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>The county agricultural show was brought to a clost at Bolton on Saturday, when abomt 20,000 people Pa,i," for admission. The total for the three days was 33.1010 as against 19,524 at Blackpool last year.</p>
      </div>
