        <p>A REVOLUTIONARY INCIDENT. -- Friday commemorated one of the most notable of all the events of the French Revolution, the ninth Ther- midor, and the fall and execution of Robespierre. On July 27, 1794, the Convention held what was perhaps its most excited sitting. St. Just began with a report on public affairs, Thuriot being in the chair. Tallier in- terrupted with insignificant references to a Robespierre dictatorship, when the " sea green Incorruptible" tried in vain for a hearing, and, amid wild tumult, the Decree of Accusation was passed against him. Robespierre fled to the Hotel de Ville, where he attempted to organise an insurrection against the Con- vention, which body, however, acting promptiv,declared Robespierre, Couthon, St. Just, and the rest hors la loi. They were arrested, and soon Robespierre, with broken jaw, was lying in the anteroom of the Convention Hall. On the following day, July 28, the conspirators against the Republic were swiftly tried, condemned, and exe- cuted, Robespierre having on the sky-blue coat snecially made for "the Festival of the Supreme Being." Thus was enacted the so-called Revolution of Thermidor.</p>
